      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>processional crois or crucifix, with the object of its being usei in the ceremanial observances. The Dean and Chapter do not all belong to the same school of Church thought, and while the crucifix would be a cherished and honoured symbol ta one section, it would be strenuously resented as a Romish emblem by others. The caugregation which attends the Cathedral is as mixed as the Doan and Chapter, and if tne crucifix is borne at the head of a procession, some would be willing to bow before it, while others would regard it as an outrage on Protestan- tism. One of the dignitaries has declared that if the crucifix is ever borne be ore him when he has to preach, he will denounce its use from the pulpit, and with such a state of feeling prevailing in their own midst, the Dean and, Chapter are not likely to adopt; an inn,vatios which would lead to the bitterest of conflicts. For the second time the Chancellor of the Dio- • case of London ba3 issued an order that the coffin of Mr. T. C. Druce be opened, in order to ascertain If it contains the remains of Mr. T. C. Druce. The first order led to protracted appeals, and the new order has alrea ly been appealed against, with ths result that there will be further delay and a great increase to the cost. The appeal lies to the Court of Arches of Canterbury, which seems to have no local habitation, and the judge of which has been confined for a long perio Ito his bed. In the last appeal, which came before Lord Penzance— namely,one from the Consistoriat Court of Lichfield —he was in bed, and did net even hear counsel. His decision was either written in bed or dictated from his bed, and no one can sly that this is a satis- factory way of administering ecclesiastical justice. After all, it is not so anomalous as the state of the law, which could not have been more cunningly dtvised if the object had been to make the pro- ceedings interminable and justice unobtainable by poor clients.</p>
      </div>
