      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>(XI rights reserved. THE</head>
        <p>(XI rights reserved. THE</p>
        <p>SECRET OF MARK PEPYS.</p>
        <p>BY FRED J. PROCTOR, Author' of "Timothy Twill's Secret," "Jean</p>
        <p>Marlou," "Dr. Dobbs," &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>CHAPTER XIX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIX.</p>
        <p>' 017 R JOURNRY COASTWA.RD.</p>
        <p>In my conceit I judged Lucia's sadness to be sham. She had communicated with Muro and enlisted his services in this wicked scheme.</p>
        <p>"Sandy," said Mark, in a confidential .whisper, "that rascal, Muro, is the evil genius dogging our footsteps. Arm yourself, and bring Jip." Olive led the way up the hazardous path leading to the sepulchre. The stones were cleared from the entrance, and when we got fairly inside we peered through the dingy air. Olive stopped, placed her finger on her lips, and cast her eyes aslant io indi- cate where we were to listen. Was that Lucia's gentle laughter which sounded in my ears like the ripple of the tide in a shell? I gazed about with a half-bewildered look, and then stepped in cautiously forward, but the Princess seized my arm, saying, in a frightened whisper: "Follow me, or death may overtake both!" _</p>
        <p>Mark and I stared at each other, and Pecos held Jip in with great tenacity. Olive took the lantern and led the way to the innermost recesses of the cavern until she spied a newly-laid heap of small stones in a corner. Her sharp eyes soon discovered a woman's footprints, too defined for an Indian's moccasin. "That looks like Lucia's tread," said Mark, his manner stern.</p>
        <p>"Pointed toes an' ma' heels," I added, as he put his hand to his aching forehead and sighed. The glittering cross he had discovered must have convey4d an unexpected message. I could see he was deceived in Lucia, and that his whole nature was changing towards the pretty Spaniard. As for me, while sorry for him, I felt I had scored a point, for my estimate of Lucia had apparently been borne out. Priding myself on a profound know- ledge of human nature, I rubbed my hands and smiled with self-satisfaction. My sublime conceit was taken out of me by Mark's frown and snub: "Many women wear shoes, you jackass!" "These clipped arrow-heads, which cut like broken glass," said Olive, "were covered with a deadly poison, and placed points upward in the ground. Our ancestors wore no sandals, or only such as these could easily pierce. Thus the robbers knew of this danger, and knocked off the heads. Some banished member of our tribe has aided in this theft. The Evil One triumphs," continued Olive, trembling from head to foot. "Not even my mother can save me from the wrath of the priests! " She wrung her hanas despairingly. "The girl will gang oot o' her mind unless we promise t' save her," I urged, noticing the wild look in her eyes. "Olive," Mark responded, with an infinite amount of tenderness, "he who says he cannot overcome a discredited devil, is a coward. We must seal the entrances and trust to luck. I shall aim to please the Queen and her courtiers, thus building up a personal influence which, should the theft of the bodies be discovered, will enable me to save thee; so be silent." "My lips are sealed," she responded. "My lord speaks words of comfort. Let us go hence." We left the caverns, and as opportunity offered the entrances were satisfactorily closed and cemented. Many days passed in suspense, and then, by degrees, Olive's gaiety returned, and as the Princess took every means compatible with propriety, to shew her gratitude, Mark was thrown much in her company. He taught her the simplest English words, while she helped him to acquire a fuller knowledge of her tongue. As for Lucia Diaz, he strove to forget her, but, in view of after issues, lam glad to say I neither aSsisted nor discouraged this. Having lost confidence he would never again trust her. "She has been false to me and her vow," he once remarked, with great depth of sadness, "and it is my earnest wish, Sandy, that she may never again be mentioned in my hearing." But he was not happy. I never saw him enjoy a hearty laugh, and he seldom smiled. He could not altogether banish the old love, even though he had taken on the new. But he endeared himself to our little colony by many acts of kind- ness which he blushed to hear made known. He was content to remain here, for our periodical com- munication with San Leno brought no evil tidings, and whatever danger threatened from the Mother Country he was safe in Golden Vale. What he suffered when he thought of Lucia's treachery was known only, to me, and that in a meagre sense, for the spirit of this powerful man groaned in silence. Any pangs of remorse for that early past in England were totally eclipsed by the mental anguish caused by Lucia's insincerity and flight. Although I had warned him, yet now I pitied his misery. From devotion to indifference was a mental transition that few can realise, and if, in he bitterness of hie soul he was led to curse the hour he met Lucia Diaz, there were reasons which compelled me to pity where I could not solace.</p>
        <p>Now, in course of time, sensational reports came to us of the influx of miners to the northern dis- tricts. Mark puzzled me by suddenly proposing a journey to San Francisco. I measured his risk by recalling Harry Puke's covert sneer about the shadow which overhung my young master's career. I was convinced that Mark had no desire to imme- diately break from the associations of Golden Vale, and his secret object might be to gather news of Muro ; or, perhaps, a returning infatuation for Lucia was a redeeming inspiration. I sounded him, but my curiosity was promptly snubbed. He knew his own business best, and would brook no interference from me. He was restive and broody by turns, and I felt sure he was about to commit some egregious blunder. In my estimation Lucia's artificiality and hollowness should have deadened finer feelings and crushed the inner sincerity of the man. •No doubt she had gone where tempta- tions would surround her, and her freedom of life would fail to preserve 'what simplicity of manner was left—that simplicity which, after all, . is a woman's' greatest charm. Princess Olive's fears were lulled, and Lana wished him God-speed. This proved that he had measured his danger, and promised speedily to return. Yet I was full of fear lest the fatal passion for Lucia would rise like an avenging Nemesis to blacken the chivalrous fame of Lancelot.</p>
        <p>We left- quietly, well equipped, and without raising suspicion. We made for Little Yosemite, Trail, intending to pass Rocky Point, and strike ofl/4 north-west by Big Oak Flats. We sighted Cascade Creek, and were resting at noon by the roadside when. some gay Californians and a horde of women dressed in male attire and mounted on spirited horses darted furiously by. "Sandy," observed Mark, keenly alive, to every phase of life, "something is brewing: There is not the look of the vigorous miners about those men."</p>
        <p>judging by that lack of roughness which comes of ill-cooked food, poor housing, and coarse and baggy clothing, these fellows were town bred. With alacrity, acquired of long habit, we were soon on the strangers track, and came up to them in Yosemite Valley, at a place calculated to please the eye and invigorate the mind. As we "covorted " around in a free-and-easy style, making ourselves genial and winning confidence, a few settlers came into the valley by way of Eagle Peak Trail, and joined the strangers as if by preconcerted arrange- ment. They differed from the Californians in that they had a characteristic recklessness and abandon, and as we mixed with them the girls' pithy talk and the men's rough chivalry interested US. Each was armed, for this was necessary ihen no one would submit to much imposition or imagined wrong. "That fellow wi' the bushy hair an' whiskers," said L "is observing us verra suspicious—he's a-chattering we the she-dragons. Ye frown, guv'nor, but I winna stand a girl in pants at ony price. They may be guid lassies, but Mrs. Grundy values virtue by appearances." Mark tossed his head and laughed derisively. Then we pricked our ears at such expressions as: "She wouldn't stick Piety Hill, too many white chokers about." "Loafer's Retreat ain't worth a splotch." "Gospel Swamp is the gamblers' hell ; guess 'The Butterfly ' may be found there." Mark opened his eyes and tapped my breast with the stein of his pipe. They referred to Lucia! "When the pooty one sees the derrick an' tackle, she'll call on the saints, an' confess she stabbed the silly ' pigeon.' " "Dolores fleeced the lad an' silenced him with steel!"</p>
        <p>We noticed that the brawny miner, who had eyed us suspiciously, had ridden off coastwards. Mark was apparently callous of his disappearance, and wormed out that Dolores, alias The Butterfly, was asswto_sed to Parade 'Prigt9 9ilclPO</p>
        <p>tollowets oy tne veriest rogues and stray vagabonds that ever haunted a community. When the young victim was murdered, his chums went to Dolores's tent, bent on revenge. They tore it down, but she had shifted quarters and placed herself under the protection of rich admirers who bribed right and left to save her neck. They were the most daring and cleverest rascals unhung, a rollicking set, fond of Dolores's sinking and dancing, and able to pay for their whims. The majesty df the law was upheld by unblushing intimidation, a mock trial, and Dolores's acquittal on account of the delicate state of her health. The Butterfly's beauty had fetched popular sentiment. The law officers were badly paid therefore they were inefficient and corrupt. The law bristled with technicalities in favour of the criminal. The hands of leading citizens were steeped in sin, and so the lower rascals had their fling. Dolores joked about the hangman's rope, turned her trial into ridicule, an reigned in the gambling saloons, an Empress among the revellers! Mark drew me aside and said: "The lad's father is here, bent on revenge, so it is foolish to argue. There must be no nip-cheese policy while Lucia is in danger. I have forgiven her treachery, for a petted and impulsive woman does stupid things without counting the cost. We must ride as if the very devil were after us, and outstrip that brawny miner! We must rescue Lucia, and cheat the hangman! ,• We started off by way of Tamarack and Crane Flats, and struck through, the Tuolumne grove of big trees, following the lower reaches of the river, and from thence made ?Frisco without mishap. Several thousand • tents and shanties had sprung into existence by the sides of the bridle paths, over the sandhills and through the brush. The sites of the finest streets te-day„ with their magnificent commercial buildings and mansions were then but sand or black mud-dunes patched for vehicular traffic with logs and bushes. The authorities were too busy making money to wait on good health or to secure' home 'comforts. People bustled and jostled each other; everyone thought he had a license to follow some other calling but the one to which he was bred; and what with the bawling, fighting, bad language and the horrible drinking saloons, Yerba Buena had grown from a quiet fishing village into a Pandemonium. So high ran the fever to seek the "golden fleece" that neither threats nor punishment could make townsmen and women fulfil ,their most solemn engagements. Everyone was drawn inward by the irresistible magnet. Avarice was the storekeeper's god; the over-weening desire to be suddenly rich grew into a disease until every class of the community was affected. Everywhere it was hurry and eager curiosity, and daily;the bay was alive with launches and other craft' filled with immigrants and their goods. • As no housewife thought of cooking at home, , families fed at hotels and restaurants, many of which were miserable hovels with discomfort rank- ing at high prices. People ate, drank, and amused themselves; the spendthrifts gambling away earn- ings often in a night; and then sleeping in rows along the floor, or in bunks fastened to the weatherboard and swarming with vermin. Wages were high, and the elegant drinking bars throve, blazing with light, crowded to excess with a motley company, and luring the unwary by means of voluptuous women well dressed, who turned the roulette wheels, and joked about the lascivious pictures hung on the walls. It was into these saloons that we went searching for Senorita Dolores, alias Diaz; for Mark had despatched trusty messengers to each of the likely towns and camps, that no time might be lost in saving The Butterfly from her doom. We hung round•the'faro tables, went to the dircus and con- cert Jiagg and followed half-tipsy miners to their gambling haunts, where they speculated and lost their 'piles." If ever I had an inkling after the tables, this search turned my face from them for ever. The bankers got the hauls; and many a young man vowed never to' stake a farthing, but when he got among the crowd and saw judges and advocates, clergymen and 'physicians enjoying the greed and indulging in the devilry, he was en- couraged to play. "Look at yonder miner," observed Mark, as we stood in a two-storey frame building 'in Kearny- street watching the lumps of pure gold staked as if they were mere hazel nuts at a Christmas table; "when the fool has lost his dust he'll shake, shave, wash, and be off again to the diggings: The bankers cry 'Eureka,' and the poor devils of miners answer with a futile curse or a slug shot. Now, just look around at this company. I'll bet you my bottom dollar there are not two hats alike in shape and material." Apart from the betting instinct which coloured my friend's challenge, I laughed heartily to see he was right. Besides the natives there were pigtail Chinese, dandy black fellows, fiery-eyed Malays, tattooed Maories, Fiji ex-cannibals, a turbaned Turk, large-limbed Teutons, lank-visaged Ameri- cans, robust Englishmen, easy-principled Italians, and gay Frenchmen. Among these passed beauti- ful women from every country, making themselves understood by signs where they failed to com- prehend the language. "I have scanned every woman's face," observed Mark, "and Lucia is not here." But to my intense and secret astonishment I observed among the motley company, watching our movements but carefully avoiding us, the miner aforesaid. This man was shadowing us for a purpose. We went to our lodgings on that and several nights after a fruitless search, and one by one our messengers came back, reporting that no lady by the name of Dolores was seen, nor one of her description heard of. At last a travel-stained horseman came from Sacramento, who described the seizure of a Spanish lady, of whose name he was uncertain except that some knew her as Dolores, and others hinted she once went by the name of Diaz, and aided and abetted Muro in his nefarious schemes. Ife stated how the roughs of, Sacramento spoke of her as a graceful Castilian, who bewitched the wealthy rascals of the city, and secretly aided the Hounds with the money obtained from her rich acquaintances. In consequence, the Hounds waxed fearfully bold and bloodthirsty, tearing down tot's by night and robbing and murdering the inmates. There were no persons brave or foolhardy enough to resist this savagery; but the authorities, urged thereto by a bend of strangers who had come from the south, seized the lady and imprisoned her. ~ This roused the anger of the llounds, who gathered in force, broke open the gaol, and dis- covered that Dolores had been secretly removed, and they, therefore, cheated of their prize. But later on these desperadoes and their prOtgee were caughtjn a trap. The Vigilance Committee knew the, ring,leaders, and having a auperior force handy fouwteen of them were captured, red-handed, and amid the ominous ringing of the engine-house bell, they and the Senorita were condemned to death: At headquarters blocks with the necessary tackle had been hastily fixed to the beams which projected over the windows of the hall, the loose halters were pulled by the members of the Vigil- ance Committee, and the Hounds, with Dolores encased in a sack to her waist, were seen dangling by their necks. Our messenger said he stood among the thousands who witnessed this awful sight, unable to put forth a word or hand to save the lady; and, the shouts of exultation which succeeded the swinging-off proved how satisfied were the mass of the people. ' Dolores's beauty, height, the colour of her hair and eyes, her vivacity and musical abilities tallied in every particular with Mark's description of Lucia. She revelled in gaiety, careless of her reputation, seeking notoriety by the maddest tricks known to women,. She said the crime was trumped up, and at first mocked her excusers like one crazy. She laughed, scolded, entreated, and wept by turns. When her peril was greatest and hope gone, she bore herself bravely and sang Spanish love-songs. Some Mexicans, who saw her swing, said they knew her to be Lucia Die; the mistres. of Miro. • ".</p>
        <p>_ "And yet," said Mark angrily, "white men themselves that they are not copper-coloured savages! What of her body ? " "The mob gave it .to the crabs, tied in a sack, weighted with stones. The women were loudest in demanding her death. They acted like frenzied beings, gloating over her downfall. And the men who lent themselves to it were the vilest brutes in Christendom." _</p>
        <p>..... This intelligence was a heavy blow for Mark, but he bore himself with fortitude. "Sandy," said he, "I have the consolation of knowing that I tried to save Lucia. I'll just see that the ranch is going along all right, and then turn my back on this God-forsaken hole!" "The district 'reeks o' blood, an' the blackest crimes gie the scoundrels frae all quarters o' the globe the kind o' enjojment they like. I'm sick to deeth—lat's awn' quick!" He paid and dismissed the messenger. Some strange impulse prompted me to follow and watch the man's movements. As he led his tired nag down the street he was familiarly recognised by a miner, and after a short conversation the two repaired to the nearest saloon, a place reeking with tokeect. 10.9i0 end the Nees of het mem</p>
        <p>I cautiously peeped through the open door, and seizing a favourable opportunity, slipped in un- observed. The miner and messenger were hob.. nobbling; and the former was apparently worming information, Mercury seeming nothing reluctant. As I was loth to make a fool of myself by claiming a doubtful acquaintance with the miner, I shot out of the door and bolted down the street as fast as my legs could carry me. It might be necessary for Mark's safety that he should learn the news. I reached our "hotel" in breathless haste, to find that my chief had saddled his horse, paid the bill, and gone none knew whither. I was on the horns of a dilemma until I heard Jip's bark in the backyard, and going out to him, found Mark's charm tied around his neck. This meant that I was to retrace in steps to Golden Vale, taking the dog with me. I obeyed, and finding no "token' along the trail, lingered and continually listened anxiously. It was far into the gloaming of the following day before Mark overtook me, having come in by a cross-out, to the delight of Jip and ,the relief of my mind. "Well, truant," said he, "why did you follow the miner P " "Because I thought I recognised Harry Puke; and if so, he_was up to no good purpose." Mark burst out laughing. "I knew the 'bearded pard' when I first clapped eyes on him at Yosemite. But I'm not going to discuss with you the ero., bable why and wherefore of his ruse. I gave 'him the slip, and now we'll press on for Golden Vale, to see whether absence makes the heart grow fonder."' "And to break altogether from early tamale. tions," thought I. Our return journey was too hastily accomplished to admit of much extraneous conversation, but we could not banish• the thoughts of Lucia's tragic death; and when I roundly condemned Harry Puke and shrugged my shoulders with supreme disgust, Mark was ominously silent. He must have keenly felt the reflected shame of Lucia's disgrace and hanging. But time is sorrow's healer, and by degrees my friend's bright disposition would assert itself. liana and her courtiers welcomed us as though we were ambassadors come from our Sovereign lady Queen Victoria. The magnificence of our reception impressed even the saditious Indians. But I was not altogether easy after the profuse delight of Goya and hill mad dreamers. They aimed, through anarchy, to arrive at Utopia, and our warm welcome by such anti-monarchists pleased me but little. Yet I knew this much, that with the spirit of his retaliation smothered by Kana's kindness, Mark's fame would be ennobled by his loyalty to the quick-witted mother he once came to despoil. If he would but have tender thoughts for the beautiful Princess Olive—if her grace, her kindl7 disposition and her intelligence would only win his fancy—then might come the dawn of an abiding love. The welding of a Briton's interests with those of the Royal heiress would strengthen the Indian's throne. So argued the diplomatic Kane, who, by subtle wit, meant to truimph.</p>
      </div>
