      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CHAPTER rX.. AN INDIAN WEDDING.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER rX.. AN INDIAN WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Day by day I watched the friendship between Mark. and Olive grow stronger, and as, the weeks sped on this ripened into an affection which proved that his aim was to have the plea of a closer and more-sacred tie in protecting her. This was neces- sary when he saw the concealed fanaticism of the priests, and the superstitious awe the people had of their revered dead. It was a saying in Golden Vale that "A crime against the living is pardon- able, but the desecration of the dead can never be forgiven." The feelings of Olive, therefore, may be imagined, for she was as one living continually on the brink of a smouldering crater. Mark soon established himself a favourite at Court, and every month showed him more of the stability, wealth, and magnificence of this wonder, ' ful race. His lion strength gave him power over the athletes of the valley and great popularity among the masses. He did nothing mean, and iv:mg affable to all. I was surprised how his good juilg- ment developed, and how the counsellors deferred to his opinion, when, as a Knight of the Squirrel, he took his seat in the House of Representatives. • On one occasion a horde of Pah Ines, headed by some white men, attempted at midnight to force their way into Golden Vale. Goya, the mighty man, atrributed this to our presence, and there were a few who began to growl, as though an agitation against us had been progressing for some time; but the way Mark again buckled on his weapons and led us to resist the foe, completely silenced the malcontents and secured him greater favour with • the Queen. . .</p>
        <p>Therefore, when in course of time he proposed to Kant% for the hand of her daughter Olive, his suit was well received, and when duly announced to the people they rejoiced with exceeding great joy—all save Goya and his cronies, who sneered and spread sedition against us by covert means. No man ever rose to' fame without the envy of some of his compeers. As protracted engagements were unknown in Golden Vale, details were soon settled, the marriage dry fixed, and a medallion struck in honour of the allience. The ceremonies both interested and edified me. • ' ;</p>
        <p>The walls, tables, brackets and glasses of the pretty bridal home were profusely decorated with flowers, devices, ,and such mottoes as: "Be tender and charitable.'' "Preserve simplicity in manners and conversation." "Be neat in your attire, and clean in your person." "Modest at all times, be cheerful in adversity." "To ensure obedience a husband must win respect." "Sorrow and dis- appointment are the lot of man, but domrc''‘. ;,lv is given by the Lord as compensation." Le tolden Vale sacred books were pregnant with wisdom, their advice equal to anything given in the Talmud, Koran, and other Eastern volumes. Everything shewed that their teachers had ,a profound know- ledge of human nature. We were carefully " &amp;coached " for the ceremonies, and the comicalities of many a situation equalled those at the dress rehearsal of amateur theatricals. The civil ceremony took place in idea's principal room, the bridegroom occupying a chair to the right of the prettily-decorated hearth, Olive sitting on a stool to the left, spinning. "This," said I, as best man, taking Mark's right hand and introducing him to the company, "is Mark Pepys 'of San Leno, landed proprietor, and a suitor for the hand of the fair Princess of Golden Vale." _ _ .</p>
        <p>"Welcome!" exclaimed the guests, saluting. "This," said the Queen, placing her hand upon her daughter's head, "is Olive, Princess of Golden Yale, whom we give in marriage with the White Chief."</p>
        <p>Thereupon Mark took a bow and arrow,, ran out, and brought in a dead rabbit, shot for the occasion, and some dry twigs, giving both to Olive. She laid some paper, broke the twigs, placed them on the hearth and the rabbit over them, Upon a kind of gridiron. Then ,the bridegroom was to obtain light from a tinder-box and flint, .for his bride to set fire to the twigs; but to avoid anything clumsy I struck a match and handed it to him. While the rabbit was being singed the principal officer of state advised Mark somewhat in the ancient Toltec form: _</p>
        <p>"Beloved 01 the white race, lay to heart our advice, handed down by our forefathers locked in precious gold-leaf. garriage is a Divine rite, children the Lord's heritage. The young of bygone ages served in the Temples and offered copal to the gods; but the scales have fallen from our eyes, and where darkness reigned Divine light now shines. The Lord accepts the prayers of His children, for they are pure of heart, and as a precious stone, without blemish. So live, that when bidden to step into the Spiritland your delight may be ever- lasting." Thereupon an attendant handed a buckskin garment, trimmed with fur, to the bridegroom, and a cap of the same material decoratethwith short eagles' feathers. Into his belt a tomahawk was placed, and in his right hand a fir cone. It was obvious that the bridegroom was expected to engage in outdoor occupations, and the bride in domestic duties.</p>
        <p>Then the chief minister caused various miniature implements of husbandry and technical science to be placed at Mark's feet, with a trite symbolical remark for each. He was admonished by the priest to be faithful and industrious—for indolence is the forerunner of many vices. When the Royal mother rose with dignity, and, tailing Olive's left hand, said: "My beloved daughter, be as gentle as the dove, prudent, and circumspect. Speak deliberately, but with judgment. "The vain woman will mince her words and speak through her nose, but I charge thee to act with becoming grace. Jump the brooklet decently, salute those of lower estate so as to win respect. In publie let thy habits be prudent, and in private let thy cheerfulness bring joy to the household. • "Adorn thy person as pleases thy husband—not over nice, but with a delicate sense of thy new estate. See, likewise, that thou dost not paint thy face after the manner of vile women, nor stain thy lips after the fashion of the immodest.</p>
        <p>^"Should thy husband be perverse, exercise Dgie.4o.. Seq neble healt will cherish thesi_ ails]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>admire thy Itindly disposition. iSo may tny lire De as the freshness and verdure of a perpetual spring, thy home a sweet-smelling paradise, sanctified by the incense of love."</p>
        <p>Then Bana crowned the bride with a chaplet o flowers, and the chief minister, placing a hand upon the bowed head of each, said: "These twain shall henceforth be one. Accursed be he who severs the knot."</p>
        <p>Lucia's last mocking laugh made my ears burn! At this stage a beverage was served out, and we drank to the couple, a golden goblet being handed- to the bridegroom, and one of chased silver to the maiden, both drinking to our prosperity. The civic authorities then brought in a register of 'Royal marriages, the characters of the older records ( learly pointing to Toltec origin. One strange point worthy of note about this register was that the name of liana's consort was completely erased, though the date enabled me to make a pretty good guess as to Olive's age, namely, about eighteen. ' Both bride and bridegroom signed with a quill pen, and Rana and her Prime Minister witnessed their signatures. Then a magnificent procession was formed to the Temple, prettily-dressed girls burning incense and sprinkling flowers along the - path. Mark retained his buckskin garment for a time, but his bride was in pure white, decorated will fresh and pretty flowers, and a white silk sash was about her waist. She wore no ornament what- ever, save a necklace of pearls and a silver Palenque cross, the gift of the bridegroom. The High Priest, his assistants and choristers met us at the church door and sang a hpautiful hymn of praise. Hymen were difficult to please if indifferent to the lovely melody of that bridal march.</p>
        <p>The work of the pious monks was now seen in the form of religious ceremony, but the priests alone had charge of the books used in the solemni- sation ; so were I to attempt a description of the language as Pecos slowly and solemnly translated It to Mark, scant justice would be done a beautiful service. One or two unique customs were im- pressed on my memory. Upon the sacred tome each of the newly-wedded placed the left hand, with the right on the heart, t and solertmly swore to cherish each other. Re- member, infidelity was punished with death or, . banishment, but divorce had no place in Golden., ' Vale law. Therefore, Mark was entering upon.tt , very serious business, and his replies were given gt ' a style suitable to the occasion. This Kama after. i wards remarked. He gave his bride a plain silver ring, and: strangely enough she placed a plain gold ring uporil his finger. Then she nestled her head upon his; bosom, and he kissed her forehead in return. I now helped the bridegroom take off his buck- skin garment, and the High Priest covered him with a rich knight's robe; while a costly dram; with a skeleton embroidered upon the breast, wait , i placed on Olive. A chaplet of bronze leaves wazo - now put upon Mark's head, in the fashion of the , triumphal warriors of Rome; and then the pro-i cession was reformed.</p>
        <p>On halting near the baptismal for., the High, Priest dipped his finger in the water, and after, marking a cross on each palm, made them join hands and repeat; "Let all the world deride pr - threaten, we are steadfast." Then a small table was brought forward, on which lay a parchment containing a religious oath, to which Mark alone subscribed. Thereupon the Court physician, a more civilised form of the "Medicine Man," pricked the top of the bride- groom's second finger with a needle, and Mark ,L pressed the bead of blood upon the parchment at the end of his name. Olive signed her name after, but in a line with his.</p>
        <p>The bride now bound a strip of calico round, his finger, indicating her womanly function as a nurse. After this Bans and I. moved forward as if to part the couple; the High Priest ordered a Minister of State to seize and remove the Princess, and then holding up his hand deprecatingly, bade the bridegroom do his duty without fear or anger, se becomes a valiant man. "We have vowed," said Mark, defiantly shield- ing his wife, "and are mated. Let no man put asunder that which the Lord has joined in holy' wedlock." "Amen," exclaimed Olive solemnly. ,Then the High Priest blessed the union, which had not been marred by one hitch or untoward event. To, the singing and congratulations of thousands, the happy couple were conducted to their home, and there left to enjoy thtir honeymoon. ' So ended that memorable marriage, ,spoken well of by everyone save the evil-eyed Goya, who in his heart hated Mark, and was already working against him, his schemes masked by false loyalty. For the next fortnight I led a romantic ,life studying the geological formation of the picturesque mountains, whose volcanic upheaval took place thousands of years ago. At last the bride came from her seclusion, and Mark delighted to walk with her through the flowery paths of the bees, through hucklebloom and bramble, cherry-banks and buckthorn. The loving pair would watch the ' winged toilers hugging their favourite flowers and ' nestling their blunt faces upon the honey-charged bosoms ef Mother Nature. They had leisure • during their care-obliterating honeymoon to enjoy .7 the tremulous sunshine in the vistas of the Royal gardens, to breathe the soft air of the upland ) meadows, and to bask at eventide in the rich alpen ' glow. As indolence blunts the mind, I kept - myself from getting rusty by a careful study of botany; and from the native 'doctors I acquired a , considerable knowledge ,of herb properties, which : afterwards proved valuable. Perfect health made :-• our lives worth living. Even Jill grew lazy, and '1 i Pecos lounged -about among the honey flora getting as sleek as a mole. But the stern business of a 'workaday life soon broke in upon our enchantment. , What with social pleasure and public functions, -, Olive and her consort found themselves in great t, request, and my heart was glad because of their s ,happiness— a happiness increased by the extreme friendliness of Goya and his ring of agitators, all conspirators of that baser sort who ever keep a nation in a state--of ferment. Mark's foilgiving 1 nature overcame his scruples, and he welcomed ,- their favourable change of front as an augury of , petite and goodwill. But I doubted the sincerity ' of - Goya, and instinctively shuddered when be flattered my master and fawned about the Royal ~ circle. His bonhomie warned me that he plotted mischief, and a fear lest he should attack Mark when off his guard was my constant disturber. I . measured Goya's character by closely watching him, and I think he was conscious of my BUS' ' 'picion, for the brutal fellow invariably avoided me, and if we chanced to meet he could neither look ' me straight in the face nor appear at ease. The ' shifty eyes told me he still harboured malice, and as two Britishers would be seriously at the mercy of mutineers, I set myself to circumvent the dusky villain. My estimate of Goya shortly received , confirmation, but-he-got to the windward of me by ! a clever piece of manoeuvring. , (To be continued.) 46. ' SPECIAL HOME READINGS.</p>
        <p>SERIES lII.—YOUR Cf3ILDREN. No matter whether physical or mental labour meant, or even if,fts it is to often the case in these days of fiarce straggle for existence, an excel; of either hag to be a,complished, Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cccoa will prove Of Inestimable service. The jadednet,s and tirewness which chat alto ises thew ands of young msn and women of the present clay too often resolves itself into a question Of dist aildren and young persons do not require 50 much fa( d as nauriihment, and a partially cigested Brod-beverage, such as Dr. Tibbles' Vi Cocoa, giveS strength, stamina, and Wilds up and strengthens the tissues. The disinclination for further effort and OzeP. tion so oren sarnerienctsd will b-come a thing of the all _ past : and heat in summer, and cold in winter, an the bleak uncertainties of or trying climate can te faced with Dr. Tibblee VI-Conoa,Wh'ch has concentrated powers of nutrimen",-and imparts stamina and staving powers, adds to powers of endurance, and enables those who use it to undergo greater physical exertion and fatieue. The " British Me licat Journal " says : " Vi-Cocca is a very palatable beverage of great stimulating an.d enstaining properties. The " Lancet " says : " Vl' Cocoa is in the front rank of really valuable foods." We say that for breakfast and supper there is nothing to equal Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cceoa. Merit, and merit alone, is what we claim for Dr- Tibbles' Vi-Cooos. and we are prepared to send to an reader who names the Wariin,rtost " Exarainer,". dainty sample t'n of Dr. Tibblos' V!-Cocoa free and P' pain. There is no magic in all this. It is a plains Ti-Cocoa in tle o ti honest, straightforward offer. It is done to Introdurl vthi.edomeoearsitsasofa Zc ei- tra sfvuermry home. Dr. vitality, is invaluable ; nay, more than this, for all villa wish to face the strife and battle of life with greater endurance and more sustained excition, it is absolute/ indispensable. Dr. Tibbies' Vi-Cocoa, 6d., 9d., ls. 63. Can be_ obta