      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>IT is better to be able to say no than to be able to read Latin.</p>
        <p>IT is astonishing how debts will expand after being contracted.</p>
        <p>WHEN a man talks much they call it criticism; when a woman talks much they call it gossip. CAUTIOUS. Wise Father (to married son) : "You are living very nicely, I see; but are you, saving any money?" Wise Son (whispering) : "Yes, but don't tell my wife." DEFINED. —" Papa, what is intuition?" "In- tuition, my child, is what a woman claims to have when she makes a good guess." 'You protest that you love me, Emily, but I am still waiting for the first kiss." "Well, why do you wait ? " DOUBTFUL INDIVIDUAL: "That snake don't measure no 61t. long." Showman: "My friend, here's a tape-line, and the case is not locked, so if you have any doubts, just reach right in and measure the reptile." " lann's ad-a-crown I found on the bedroom floor, mum." "That's right, Jane. lam pleased to find you are so honest. I left it there to test YOU." That's just what I thinks to myself, mum."</p>
        <p>MissEn Taint.—" And you were at Venice!" exclaimed Mrs. Homely to her caller. "How interesting Of course you saw the lions of St. Mark's?" " Well, no,' replied Mrs. Trotter. "The fact is, we didn't go to the zoological gardens at all."</p>
        <p>FOUND OUT.—A retired farmer, living in a quiet village in Essex, found the milk left at his house was not so pure as it should be. The next morn- ing when the boy came he said:—" Why, you young rascal, you have been putting dirty water in this milk." Boy: " That I'm sure it wearnt, fur it vial all clean from the pump.' Tun WORM TURNS.—Hoax: "What, you buy- ing a bicycle? Why I thought youpositively detested them." Joax: "So I do, old man, but I've been run over long enough. Now I'm going to have my revenge." A CRITICAL TIME. —Landlord : "Well, are you going to pay me what you owe, or get out ?" Woman: "Here's your money. My husband is playing chess with another man In the parlour, and he just told me he'd rather pay rent than move." CALLEn TO EARTH.—In a dreamy rapture he kissed her golden tresses. "The future," he ex- claimed joyotnly, "with. its castles in the air 1" She turned with sudden earnestness. "Reginald," she said decisively, "don't deceive yoUrself, I tell you, once for all, I shan't live above the second storey under any circumstances 1" How To ICNOW A GENTLkIIIAN.—The late Rev. Dr. Sutton once said to the late Mr. P., a veteri- nary surgeon :—"Mr. P., how is it you have not called upon me for your account?" "Oh," said Mr. P.,_ I never ask a gentleman for money." " Indeed I" said the vicar; "how, then,'do you (Yet on if he doesn't pay?" "Why," replied Mr. I. "after a certain time, I conclude that he is not a gentleman, and then I ask him." A SOPicti .farmer famed for his strength was often challenged by people from a distance who had heard of his reputation. One day there arrived from London Lord W., a well known amateur pugilist. He found the Scot working in a field. " Friend," raid his lordship, after first tying his 11rse to a tree, "I have come a long way to see which of us is the best wrestler." Without saying a word the farmer seized him round the middle, pitched him over the hedge, and resumed his work. His lordship slowly gathered himself together, whereupon the farmer said:' "Welt, have you anythina more to say to me ? " "No," replied Lord W., "but perhaps you'd be so good as to throw me over my horse." His CHANcn CAME.—An amusing incident is reported as having occurred during the progress of an otter hunt in the north. An over-dressed' young man had been repeatedly asked to "clear out of the way" by the excited and eager sports- men. "Confound you, sir 1" he protested at length to an old farmer who pushed him on one side. "I've come to see the sport, and, bai Jove I mean to see it, too." The farmer said nothing at the time, but an opportunity presented itself There was a check, and the hounds were at fault. A portion of the pack was on the balk behind the young fellow, who suddenly saw, or fancied he saw, the quarry. " Heah ! heah ! " he shouted excitedly to the dogs. " Heah he is! Good dawgs !' The dogs behind him made a rush, and the unfortunate young fellow found himself in the middle of the steam before he was aware of his danger. As he stood with the water up to his shoulders the farmer previously referred to thrust his head over the bank and wrathfully observed— " Coom oot o' that, will yer 1 'Ow's the dogs to know which they're to worry ? "</p>
        <p>A mortortmArt engaged himself to a farmer in the South of England. It was arranged that he was "to hae his meals i' the hoose," along with the two other men employed on the farm. On the morning after his arrival he happened to be in for breakfast a little earlier than his companions. Being a man of "unbounded stomach," he fell to and quickly cleared not only his own bowl but those of the two others. Just as he was finishing the third the kitchen.maid made her appearance, and turning to her, he said—" My lassie, when yer makin' my parritch after this I'll be obleeged to ye if ye'll dish them a' into ae bicker. I dinna care for them in batches like that ?"</p>
        <p>A. PARTICULAR MAN.—" YOU know Jones?" " Why, yes; I know Jones, of course." "Know what a particular man Jones is ? " "I always knew he was very neat and Precise in. his habits. But what of WV.? "Well, it's all right for a man to be neat, but; hang me, there is such a thing as carrying neamess too far." "What's Jones been doing now." "You know I came over with him on the Hibernia—got in last Saturday ? " "Didn't know it, but I congratulate you--,-on getting in safe. ' Heard the boat had a mighty rough. pas- sage." " That's just the story. Only two days out when we had 'the .worst storm I ever was in. It came at night. Passengers all scared to death. Thought we were going to the bottom each minute, and we came precious near it. Boats were got ready. Somebody said there was a hole stove in the side. .Turned out to be a fake, but it frightened us all the same. The men were fighting and the women were praying, and every mother's son and daughter of 'em getting into a life- preeerVer." Well, and what's that got to do with Jones?" "Oh, yes—Jones I Well, he took down a life-preserver, looked at it, and then called td the steward. Steward caine-:--lialf dead himself, poor devil—but thought Jones didn't know how to fasten it- on. 'Steward,' said Jones, 'here, take this and dust it for me ; I.e utt wear it in its present conclitioa !' P,.rticalar We?, I si:muld say so I" INFORMING rm OLD OZNTLEIMAN.—She had accepted ilim, aid be was very happy, until she broke in on his hilBSl by suggesting that the little formality of seeing .papa hail better be gone through without delay. It would be all right, of course. No pateutal objection would be made, but he must be asked for his consent, just as though he really had sonic say about the matter. The young man demurred. He slit'ank from telling Mr. Dinsmore that he loved Ida aud Ida loved him, and that Ida's papa's consent -to their engagement was all that Vi a necessary to make them happy. He said he would se o Mr. Dinsmore later. He said he :would write to him. Ile said he would call him up by telephone next day. There were many. reasons why Mr. Dinsmore ought not to be bothered that night. Perhaps he was smoking in his den. Perhaps he had gone to bed. Perhaps he was not at home. Ida would listen to none of these excuses. Ps pa was in the house, and he had not gone to bed, and he was not so absorbed that he could not be seen, and he must be asked that very night. If Mr. 'Sanderson would not ask him, Miss Dinsmore herself would broach the subject, and Mr. Sander-. son let her have-her own way. - The two went to Mr. Dinsmore's den and knocked at the door. "Come in," sail a voice on the other side. Miss Dinemere opened the doer with one hand, and led Mr. Sanderson with the other. Papa Dinsmore looked surprised, and raised his eyebrows in- terrogatively, and his daughter said: "Papa." " Well, daughter?" "Charlie and I are a notifi- cation committee, papa." "What sort of a notifi- cation committee?" "We have come to notify you that you have been nominated to the proud position of father-in-law. There ! that's all. You needn't make a speech in reply. Sit down, papa, and go on with your reading and smoking." The girl then closed papa's door, and the notification committee returned to the parlour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>EVERY WOMAN</head>
        <p>EVERY WOMAN</p>
        <p>Should send two stamps for our NEW BOOK containing v uable information to Ladies,how all Irregularities may b entirely Avoided or Removed by Simple Means. Recommended by eminent physicians as the only Safe, Sure, and genuine Remedy. " Never Fails." Thousands of Testimonials.—Mr. W. B. BLANCHARD, Claremont HOMO, Forest-road. Balaton, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 2, 1898.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 2, 1898.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I recently saw a charming hat of pale antelope felt, with brim turned upwards. The bows were of deep heliotrope velvet, some loops lying back- wards and not seen from the front, and others on each side falling over the stems of the feathers. Velvet cut on the bias may be best, for the loops and folds continue round the left side, and there are loops forming a precise flat bow at the back, while another long feather lies along the right side, the tip just falling over the hair.</p>
        <p>While en the subject of headgear I may say that it seems probable from the general outlook that the " Trelawny " hat shape 'has come to stay the winter with us; and as it is shown in very attrac- tive aspects by our chief milliners, and is in general becoming. and good style, we may congratulate ourselves if his probability should prove a cer- tainty. Th.e more authentic models are trimmed low and broad, but this is not always becoming, and matters have therefore been compromised in 501110 model which are trimmed high, and ly choice with ostrich tips. In such a compromise the shape itself is ef white satin, covered with cream guipure lucc, and bound narrowly round the edge ot the brim with black velvet. Folds of lace and two reps of velvet encircle the low crown to the front, w'aere three black• and three white ostrich tips are placed. A twist of rope velvet appears to hold these, and is again fastened by a diamond brooeh.</p>
        <p>Velvet and narrow braid in combination is very handsome on a cloth gown, or a wide braid of some fancy description, the edge finished with narrow braid arranged in continuous loops. A trimming of this kind which I saw lately was of a light mulberry cloth, trimmed black braid, and the yoke of. pale blue glace silk, closely covered 1. ith ecru guipure. The tight-fitting bodice was made with a pinafore overpart, the lower portion repre- senting a bolero cut in tabs of varying depth, the deepest hanging straight at the centre front. This is a most becoming bodice, and very smart, and the sleeve is close-fitting, and has a small cap epaulette.</p>
        <p>Ready-made skirts of chenille emiroidered net are having a large sale. They are for the most part well cat and hung and not particularly ex- pensive, while, if a second skirt of plain net is worn under them, the slip may be of imitation instead of real silk. Enough embroidered net is supplied for a full bodice, and some lace for a berthe and sleeve-frills, and satin-ribbon for a sash are all the trimmings that are necessary. A more elaborate way of making the bodice of one of these net gowns, however, is with swellings of the material round the figure and a large butterfly of accordioned silk or of jet passementerie in front, or with short bolero fronts of jetted net and a full vest of chiffon or mousseline pouched over a jet belt. Fur is popular as a trimming for evening gowns, and odd strips can often be turned to account in this way. A cosy-looking gown for a young matron, for instance, mifelt have a skirt of rich golden-brown silk or even wool ort3pon and a pouched blouse-bodice of deep-brown velveteen cut down in a deep V back and front, to show p yoke ot ivory-satin covered with earn guip9re covered with gold sequins. This opening orld be outlined with narrow bands of sable or punk, and the long, tight sleeves might have small epau. lettes and deep cuffs to match.</p>
        <p>The new evening materials are simply charming, and this description applies not only to the elaborate and costly fabric, but equally to the inex• pensive combination of wool and silk, or to gauzy stuffs interwoven with tinsel, or nets faintly patterned with lace designs. Cloth is this season shown in the' bright, yet pale tints so necessary now that the electric light gives a faded effect to the very faint tones of former years. Gauzes run with stripes in ribbon design are always popular, as over even a poor silk of the same tone they give a really rich effect, and produce that ethereal appearance so indispensable in the young girl's ball-gown.</p>
