      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Your loving JENNIE.</head>
        <p>Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>A CARD.</p>
        <p>_ Reverend Joseph Holmes' Improved Mexican Pre scrip tion is tne only original guarantaed Remedy for all those who suffer from nervous weakness, exhausted vitality kidney or bladder disease°, gravel, prostrate. spina weakness, brain Fag and kindred complaints—discovered in Mexico. It has stood the test for twenty-two years Thousands cured. Send a self-addressed stamped envelope to JOSEPII HOLMES' REMEDY CO., Bloomsbury Mansions, Bloomsbury Square, London, and get particu- lars of this great Specific free. Mention whether for male or female. Genuine sufferers need only apply. k Beware of swindling Imitators and ignorant quacks.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>Undoubtedly, the turkey is a popular bird for Christ- mas Day,and next in favour perhaps comes the go ose.ln Italy, the dishes served at the maiority of middle class tables are stewed eels, scallops, macarone and turkey' stuffed with chestnuts. Belgium which has borrowed largely from other countries, prefers truffled turkey or goose, stuffed with chestnuts, in addition to liver pud- dings and Walloon wafers, which latter are a special dish for the occasion. Portugal has tarkey stuffe I with truffles, roast sucking pig, and roast duck, followed by rice puddieg. , The Austrian Christmas' dinner is roast turkey or geese. with pstatoes. The turkey is dressed with ' apples, and Germen vermicelle, mixed with bon and poppy seeds. The last named are alco baked in the bread and arranged so as to form a cross, when the. bread is cut. The customary dessert is composed of hasty pudding in which is mixed almondsen addition to the ordinary ingr 'clients. The German Christmas :inner generally consists of boiled pork and sauerkraut, sausages, goose stuffed with chestnuts, beef with sour sauce, smoked goose and potatoes. In Switzerland roast goose is the principal dish, with a dessert of Swiss confectioneries, or a dish known as " Geneva fritters." In Frante, those who are concerned with the observ- ance at alLusually par take of liver puddings well trailed, and plentifully seasoned with garlic, together with the national " poulet." • Many of the dibutantes who are to be presented as Court in the spring, are going through a "trial course" of country balls and parties. This is the latest idea, and certainly the, plan seems to have much to recommend it. Lady Mabel Annesley has already been out to several parties with her pretty stepmother, so also has Lady Agnes Lygon, who is Lady Beaucharnp's daughemee rex as can be seen at present however,the beauty of the year will be Miss Gladys Wilson, who is even more lovely than her sisters. Lady Annesley, is the eldest daughter of the late Wil- liam Armitage Moore, of Arranmore, co : Cavan. She married Lord Annesley some six years ago, and since their marriage they have lived a great part of each year at Castlewellan,which is one of the most beautiful parts ef the " Emerald Isle." It is situated in a woodland slope 1,000 feet above the sea level, and from the win- dows, a glorious view unfolds itself of wooded slopes and distant peeps of the Memo Mountains. A We or entertainment of any kind at Castlewellan is certain of success, for Lady Annesley has, like Lady Dufferin, a wonderful knack of remembering what are the indivi- dual tastes of her guests. The future Countess of Strafford, Mrs. Colgate is one of the most beautiful and accomplished women in New York Society. She was presented at Court two years ago, when she created quite a sensation. Mrs. Colgate possesses a fortune of £2,000,000, and some very splen- did jewels. One ornament, a riviere of diamonds she is very seldom seen without. This she usually loops across the bodices of her gowns. The wedding will take place shortly. The late Queen of Denmark's favourite stone was the opal, and to her eldest daughter, the Princess of Wales, Ner Majesty has bequeathed a remarkable necklace of these gems, which are said to bring luck to all the Danish Royal Family. It is rarely indeed that the Prin- cess fails to don an opal in some shape or form, and her example is followed by Mrs. Langtry, who possesses some very fine specimens, and nearly always wears one of these stones somewhere about her dress. The multitudinous millions of prisoned fires in the opal suggest a thousand thoughts in connection with this wonderful jewel. The glittering smoothness of Re polished surface seems to prison the warm tints of red and green, and blue, that shine beneath it a miniature volcano. It is small wonder that the poet novelist as- cribed to it uncanny qualities and yet it is the jewel of hope,though indeed as one looks intently into its depths, it resembles Pandora's box, with hope very much at the bottom. The fashion of wearing a high band ef velvet round the throat is again revived, and many women who have handsome necklets of pearls, corals, garnets, or ameth- ysts, are mounting these on the bands. In the case of pear's, this mode of procedure is particularly successful, especially when the wearer has passed her girlhood. The Queen of Italy, whose pearls ,awreithfoaumtosuosmfeoratidheoifr sizel e aolir Clv be levaeutt,y seldom wherewith wears hers counteract their effect. A novel fabric for gowns is a kind of rep almost pop- lin like on the right side, whilst on the reverse it looks pre face cloth. Both sides may be impartially em- ployed for the same gown, At one of our leading dress- makers I saw last week a model in dark blue,which was made up on the rep side, except for the tabber and re- vers, which were on the cloth side, and pipe! with black satln. The effect was delightful and obtained at comparatively little cost. As thin soled boots and shoes are responsible for much serious illness, we should do well to avoid them, espe- cially at this season of the year. Every medical man declares against the practice, yet many women habitu- ally neglect the saving clause in their health, and won- der how this and that illness has been acquired. when damp feet are the cause of their suffering. Men are more sensible in the matter of their boots and shnes,end it is earnestly to be hoped that women will reform their habits in the miter of footgear. Many serious interne ailmests date their origin from cold and diet produced by inn fficit nt protection to the feet. MINCEMEAT.—One pound and a half each of the eel. lowing ingredients :—Suet, brown sugar, currants,mus- ratel raisins, eultaras, apples, a quarter of a pound of mixed peel, two lemons, one glass of sherry, one class of rum, an eggsponnful of salt, a little powdered cinnamon and nutmeg, a little cayenne pepper. Mix the sherry, rum, sugar, lemon jeice, spice, etc., together. Chop the apples and suet finely, also the other ingredients, and mix all together. Press it tightly into jars, and cover them with stout paper.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week Ending Saturday 19th November, 1893. Dunn' the week the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington Registration District 45 23 The excess of births over deaths was 22. The Birth Rate was equal to 372 per 1,000 of the population. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 190 per 1,000, against 196 the average of the previous 4 weeks, and against 9.2 the corresponding period of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zvmotic diseases was equal to 11 per 1,000. against 4.5 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks, and against 2.5 the corresponding period of I 1897. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 10 2 0 2 4 5 23 The deaths included-1 from scarlet fever, 1 from puerperal fever, 1 from phthisis, 4 from lung disesses, 1 from heart disease, 1 from injures, 14 from all other I causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week:—Scarlet fever, 7 eases Total number of patients in hospital for week ending 119th November, 37. METEOROLOGY—The mean temperature was 49-2 Fah., I and therefore 2 degrees higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 59F., was recorded on the 18th inst., and the minimum temperature 40F., on the 16th inst. The rainfall measured 11 inch. Vie general direction was S.W. the reading of the anemometer denoting 540 JOHN H. GORNA.LL, Medical Officer of Health. - BIRDS, ANIMALS AND FISH STUFFED, AND mounted in Glass Cases any design. HEADS and HORNS mounted, also for sale. Birds made into Hrnd Screens or Fire Screens. Deals in British and Foreign Birds and Animals' Skins. Butterflies, Birds' Eggs, Glass shades, Stags' Heads. Cabinets. store Boxes, Nets and all Requisites for Butteriy sad Egg Collestoss,—E, CoolcE, Naturalist and Furrier, 21, Renshaw - street, Liverpoo Established 24 years.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON &amp; DISTRICT A glance at the shops affords ample evidence that Christmas is at hand. . The array of presents, toys and BAND 0.61 HOPE UNION. cards is, if anything moro fascinating tan ever and the fabrics displayed for ball and dinner g owns, bewilder- UNITED t ALLY IS P.ll.Ttlt HALL. ingly lovely. Christmas trees promise to be very popular again this year, and any number of glittering ornaments have been especially prepared for them. Tne best will be lighted with electricity, and, glitter in the most dazzling/ manner. All those on view are raised on a platform of either green baize, or moss, on which the presents are laid. In the majority of cases these are too ambitious to be positively attached to the tree. In these nineteenth century-end days of ours, it is by no means an easy task to amuse the little ones. They are so blase and used up. At a dance, they are quite ready to despise a stand up supper, or the piano played by their bostese, or a friend, and year after year one foolish mother tries to outdo the other until, at the pre- sent time, a children's party costs as much as a grown up affair. The Christmas tree is the most expensive thing of all, for the toys, such as the infant of to-day considers worthy of acceptance,are as costly as they are ingenious. The Christmas card is supposed to have been the in- vention of Sir Henry Cole, a great patron of art,and the founder of South Kensington Museum. It appeared first in 1848, and gradually grew into favour though it is only within recer.t years that it has swept its former rival—the valentine—comp'etely from the field. One of them had to go, for Christmas Day, and St. Valentine's are too close together to admit of friends exchanging Christmas cards on December 25, and Valentines on February 14. In 1883 it was calculated that 2,700,000 valentines passed through the London Post Office, but by 1889 this number had dwindled down to 300,000 or thereabouts. Valentine's Day no longer brings extra work to the Post Office autherities,but every Christmas se.s an increasing number of cards posted and delivered. This season one of the largest firms has designed over 350 varieties, its total output being more than 15,000,000 Christmas and New Year Cards. The work of production and de- signing is commenced fully a year beforehand, so that the manufacturers are even now busy preparing novel- ties for 1899.</p>
        <p>The interior of Parr Hall presented an animated spectacleonThursday last week,wh en the first united rally of band of hope societies in Warrington and district was held. It will be remembered that about a year ago the Warrington and District Band of Hope Union was formed, and the grand rally on Thursday evening is one of the fruits of its labours. Much of its success was due to the energetic efforts of Mr. Lloyd Jones, the hon. sedretary, who was chiefly responsible for the arrangements. The chair was occupied by Mr. R. A. Naylor, the presi- dent of the linion,and he was surrounded by a choir of band of hope children numbering 250 voices under the able conductorship of Mr. Walter Brough. There were also on the platform Miss Naylor, Rev. G. Harbottle, Rev. Gamble Walker (Birkenhead)' Mr. W. Chandos Wilson (Manchester), Mr. Windle (secretary Warrington Young Men's Christian Asso- ciation), Mr. R. Lloyd Jones (secretary Warrington , Band of Hope Union), Mr, S. B. Stringer, Mr. 1 Prince, Mrs. Yong°, and Miss Whitfield.</p>
        <p>The preceedings commenced shortly afterseven o'clock with the singing of a festival hymn by the choir in a manner which reflected great credit upon all concerned, During the evening the choir sang a number of temper- ance choruses such as "Sing Praises," " The Social Glass," " We love Water Bright." In the selo and chorus "Kindly words and smiling faces " Miss Wood- ward was the soloist. Mr. E. Wilkes, A.L.C.M., effi- ciently presided at the organ. The programme was pleasingly diversified with recitations, speeches, a dialogue and a violin solo. The reciters were Miss A. Morrell, Mr. B. T. Parkes, Miss Minnie Whitmore and Miss Amy Shaw,and the pieces were recited in a manner which left nothing to be desired. Miss Whitmore wa encored. Miss K. Thomas, of Widnes, gave a very care ful interpretation of her two songs ; " Never More,.., being especially well sung. The members of the Bold- street ,Band of Hope contributed a dialogue entitled. I, The Flower Queen's Court." The children, who weret prettily attired and represented various flowers entered into the affair with zest, and both sang and recited remarkably well. The accompanists were Mrs Horton,and Professor Ainsley. The programme throughou was capably rendered, and greatly enjoyed by the large audience present, who showed their appreciation by repeated applause. Addresses were delivered by the Chairman, the Rev. Gamble Walker, and Mr. W. Chandos Wilson.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN sail it was a great pleasure to see such a large attendance of Bend of Hope children, and he thought the Union might congratulate itself upon the fact that it had such a magnificent choir. (Hear, hear.) He was sure that the choir had already given evidence of being able to produce good harmony, and to the con- ductor and the members of the choir the greatest praise was due. He thought it would be well if teetotallers put on a little brighter appearance than they had been accustomed to. (Laughter.) A few years ago they had a great influx of persons into the temperance movement and he was not quite sure whether they did not do more harm than good. They began to blackguard the early teetotallers and those who instituted the great band of hope movement fifty years ago. The early teetotallers were very good men,arid they made very great sacrifices in the interests of the movement. They had just one idea, and they of the present day could not help but admire their concentrativeness. They did not associate anything else with the cause of temperance. Their main object was to keep people sober. For that purpose they made tremendous sacrifices in the interests of national sobriety And had much opposition to eneeunter. It seemed a great pity that those who joined the move- ment later on should begin to " pitch " into those men who fought well for the cause when its friends were few and its enemies many. Another mistake which had been made was in clustering around the temperance question a lot of other things, one of which was party politics. As far as he was concerned, he wished party politics were at Jericho. (Laughter.) They hod done more harm than almost all the advocates of temperance had hcea able to do good. They needed to banish narty politics from their movement. (Hear, hear.) No political party had ever done anything for the cause of temperance ; the question was rather which of the two great political parties had done the most , harm. Another thing, it did the temperance movement a vast amount of harm when they began to blackguard , not only old teetotallers but brewers and publicans. What good did it do them by consigning publicans to i the bottomless pit. (Laughter.) There were some , brewers and publicans who were just as., good in the , sight of Heaven as they were. They as temperance workers had got a good thing and intended to stick to It. But perhaps if some teetotallers were weighed in the balances the publicans would have the best of it. , (Laughter.) They did no good to the cause of temper- ; ance by throwing stones. They all had their , imperfections, and they as temperance workers , were trying to lead people to live sober lives. ; As regarded the early temperance pioneers it was a great satisfaction to him to know that one of them—Joseph Livesey—wes going te have due honour paid to his memory. (Applause). Livesey was one of the most prominent men who started the Band of Hope movement in Preston 85 years ago. He was glad that temperance organisations were mustering their forces and were going to build a handsome ball in Prestos to be called the Livesey Memorial Hall. Probably next year would see this work commenced as most of the fund required had been secured. In conclusion Mr. Naylor urged them to work together and do all they possibly could for the •advancement of the cause of tem- perance. It must make all rejoice to see hundreds of children present that night who had adopted in their youth the principles of total abstinence. If they con- tiaued to work in this way one thing was certain, and that was that if the children were true to their pledges they would never disgrace themselves by becoming ! drunkards. (Applause). The philosophy of the early teetotallers was summed up in the sentence 'lf you never take the first glass the devil himself cannot make you take the second. (I..ctughter and Applause). The Rev. GAMBLE WALKER then delivered an earnest and impassioned address. At the outset he congratulated the president and secretary on that magnificent meet- ing. He said the subject of temperauce was one of the most vitally important subjects which could occupy the attention of men and women in the great couatry of which they formed a part. They recognised that they belonged to the greatest empire ever known and that there had never been any parallel in history. There might be something in the tact that they were a great nation but he could not help thinking when he took into consideration the social customs and habits of the people that their greatness was being undermined by in- temperance. (Hear, hear.) When he read the recent speech of Sir Michael Hicks Beach in which he advised them to drink more beer he could not help thinking of the song .‘ Beer, beer, glorious beer." (Laughter.) Evidently Sir Michael seemed to imagine that the more beer they drank the better it would be for the country at large. Hs believed with the Chairman that they had . no right to attack brewers or publicans for the simple ! reason that they were carrying on a perfectly legitimate business that was sanctioned by the State. It they wanted to attack anything let them attack the State that made it possible for these men to carry on such a business. (Applause.) They had to remember that they belonged to an age when the greatest honours that the Country could be- stow seemed to be reserved for the brewer and publican. The easiest way to obtain admission to aristocratic cireles was to ally oneself with the drink traffic.(Laugh- ter.) The easiest way to a permanent .place in the Council or to become Chief Magistrate was to be closely identified with the liquor trade. They must not forget that in days gone bye the honours were reserved for men of brave deeds and noble theughts.They were not in tended for those men who while carrying on a perfectly legitimate business had by their trade helped to slag their thousands. (Hear hear.) What then was their duty • with reference to this legitimate business? They bad been dabbling at this temperance questi3n for long enough now. (Applau,e ) They had spent far tco much time In distributing diresome little tracts which had done far more harm than good, and they had spent far too much time in having lectures on this aspect and that aspect of this great temperance question. The time ha a come for them to put their shoulder to the wheel, and see if they could not do something practical. He should like to see the temperance qusstion a perfectly distinct question from all political parties. Until they as a natioiatad learned the lesson that no party could deal with the question they were doomed to disappointment. The temperance question was a distinctly moral question, and it could only be solved in so far as individuals were prepared to shoulder their responsibility and to play their part in its solution like men. The speaker showed how it was a moral question, and asked how they were to tackle it ? In the first place they must remember that what was distinctly and emphatically a moral wrong could never be politically or legally right. (Hear, hear). It needed all the energy and power they possessed to see that the poli- tical and legal wrong which existed in connection with thetraffie should be swept away. They must remember in the second place that no money was legitimately earned which did not minister to life and help on the coalmen good. " o maintained that money which was earned as the re. . of the sale of drink neither ministered to life nor helped on the common good. He cared not whether it was earned in a public house or at a church bazaar. (Applause). They must remember that no wealth was ihonest that took advantage of the weakness or of the ignorance of men,or that did not give some true equiva- ent for it. He would take that opportunity to apply this question to their own experience. Was the money that the brewers and publicans were boarding up to- day in the town of Warrington ministering to a higher life or helpieg on the common rod. The money that was given by brewers and publicans to religious or philanthropic institutions could never bring back the but moral power of these whose!money had been spent in drink. (Applause). The remedy for this drink traffic was in the hands of the workpeople of the country if</p>
        <p>they would only make it a question not of party but of moral principle. (Applause.) After referring to the , broadness of the temp6rance platform he said if they would make it an individual question and put the energy of their individuality int it they would win a glorious victory. (Applause.) Mr. CrraNnos WILsoN, at the request of the Chairman, who thought some church people might have been offended by the previous speaker, delivered a band of hope address to the chitdren.</p>
        <p>At the cortelusic,n, the Rev. G. iI&amp;RBOTTLE prep lsed a vote of thanks to the Coairman, speakers and stagers for their services.</p>
        <p>Mr. WINDLE seconded, and the proposition was un- animously carried The Rev. E. LLOYD JONES said that at the next rally to be held on January 19h, Dr. Porter, of Southport, would be the principal speaker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ASTHMA CAN BE CURED.</head>
        <p>ASTHMA CAN BE CURED.</p>
        <p>A NOTED PGYSICIAN WILL PROVE THIS TO ALL SUFFERERS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>For the next three days, fres sample packages of the celebrated " Schiffmann's Asthma Cure" will bs given away at Hill and Co. The Cash Drug Stores, 62, Butter- market-street. The doctor wants each sufferer from Asthma, Hay Fever or Bronchitis in this town who has not already used his remedy,to call awl gat one. When asked regarding his reasons for giving his remedy away,in this manner, he said : " People are naturally sceptical about an asthma remedy, and when you con- sider the number of so called cures' on the market you can hardly blame them. Now I claim that my Curs cures. I.; will instantly relieve the most violent attack. It has permanently cured thousands who had been considered incurable. If I did not believe it why should Ibs giving it away? The sufferer who gets a sample package cin tell in two minutes whether it is as I represent it, anal it does'nt cost him a penny. That is fair ilent it ?"</p>
        <p>It certainly dons not look as if anything could be fairer. Those living out of town can get a free sample by writ- ing their name and address (nly) on a post card ad- dressed Dr. R. Schiffman's British Depot, 31, Snow Hill, London, 8.C., uptl December 8, not later.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>WARRINGTON HOSPITAL SUNDAY COMATITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HOSPITAL SUNDAY COMATITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Hospital Sunday Committee was held at the Infirmary on Friday afternoon. The Rev. H. V. Pigot presided, and there were also pre- sent :—Revs Canon Willis, G. Allton, P. J. Byrne, J. F. Matthews, J. S. Hughes. R. W. Drummond, W. R. Hartwright, Fathers Whittle and Wilson, Mr. Thomas Barton, with Mr. Alfred J. Jolley (hon secretary). Letters of anology for non-attendance were received from the Rev. James Davies, and Mr. E. Jenkins (Salvation Army).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>SECRETARY'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY presented his annual report which stated that one place of worship which contributed to the Hospital Sunday Fund in 1897 1 tiled te do S 3 in 1898. On the other hand seven, five of whioh were ex- tra municipal, commenced or renewed their offertories. A comparison of the two groups showed a net gain of £lO 143 11. The total ren.ims had been £284 154 4d— an iccrease over 1897 of £22 164 10d. The expenses had been for advertising £ll 103, tor stationery and postage £3 94, a total of £l4 104 as compared with £l4 14s over 1897.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN referrei to the satigfactory report which reflected very great credit on the secretary. The total amount collected was a distinct advance on 1897.</p>
        <p>The SEcarrs.ar gave a comparative statement of the amounts collected during the nast four years. The col- lections in the Borough were 1895. £l4l ss; 1896, £149 14s 21 • 1897, £169 lOs ; 1898, £167 9s 11d. 00t3ide the Borough, the collections were r-1895. £B3 91 61 ; 1896. £62 83 31; 1897, £72 83 61; 1898, £97 53 11. It would be seen that the churches outside the Borough showed an imnrovemeot of £25 on the preceding year, and the churches within the Borough a decroase of practically £2. The R.v. FATHER WHITTLE called attention to the fact that TIO contribuion hd bean made by Lymm Parish Church for two years. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- - The SECRETARY said that now the Rev. G. B. Thurston bad been appointed rector there was a likelihood of the contributions being renewed in future. The Rey. Canon WILLIS moved the adoption of the report. , The Rey. W. H. DRUMMOND said he had great pleasure in sec mding. Be thought they migh', expect that the collections in the town were likely to diminish and in- crease in the suburban districts. There was a de.:ided trend of people from the tovvu to the surrounding dis- tricts and that was one reason for the diminution. The Cruanmel.4 thought what Mr. Drummond had said explained the change. In his parish they had lost a great many of the former parishioners who had gone to enrich the suburb 3 and wealthy congregations out- side the borough. The resolution was unanimouttly carried. RE-ELECTION OF THE SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. FATHER WHITTLE proposed the re-election of Mr. A. J. ,J)lley as secret try. The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS seconded and Cam:ix WILLIS supported. The Chairman thought the committee was fortunate in having such a secret try. The proposition was unanimously carried. Mr. k. J. TOLLEY said he accepted tho office with pleasure. It was a great privilege to him to be of a;- sistance to an institution of that nind. His eervicts were given very laPartily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>DATE OF HOSPITAL SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>THE DATE OF HOSPITAL SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>After som.l discusiion it was agreed that Hospital Sunday in 1899 should be on March 12th.</p>
        <p>THE DISTRIBUTION OF HOSPITAL RECOMMENDS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. H. DROAIMOND asid it seemed to him a little unfair that the minister of those churches that had the largost collo2tions eh)uld receive more hospital recommellds than others in poorer districts where the recommends were mast needed. The thought an arrange- ment might be come to whereby those who had more recommends than they required should hand them to the secretary of the committee who would distribute them where they were required. The RtCTOR thouvt Mr. Drummond had made a valuable suggestion. The Rev. G. ALLTON said he was in one of the poorest parishes in Warrington. and yet he received the fewest number of recommends,owing to the number distributed being based on the amount collected on Hospital Sun- day. In former years there was no cause for complaint. But this year he only received five recommen is which he distributed on the day he received them. There were many persons in the parish who were deserving of re- commends and yet he was unable to give them. He did not think it was fair to expect the older incumbents to go begging recommends from others. The Rev. J. S. HUGHES said he had been receiving recommends for five yeses but he had not used one of *them. He wculd be glad to give them to clergymen in poorer districts. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said he would be pleased to do any- thing he could, but he thought it would be better if gentlemen in the position of Mr. Drumttiond and Canon Willis who had surplus tickets would distribire them amongst people conn,cted with other places of worship. Theremas a liability of friction if he were to distribute them. The Rev.J.F.Marisaws thought a great many people did not kaow where to apply for them. He had a sur- plus number of tickets which any other minister was welcome to have.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. R. HARTWRIGHT said the institution was quite undenominational, and he did n(4 think there should be any diffi3ulty in dividing the ticke's. It was ultimately decided that any person in possession of tickets who had no use for them should send them to the Secretary of the Committee, and that those in want of tickets Should apply to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>VOTES OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>The Cammwerr in proposing a vote of thank; to the 'Secretary for his services nferred to the efficient manner in which Mr. Jolley cnrried out his duties. The Rev. Canon Willis sec-nded the proposition which was unani- mously carnet —Mr.Jo'ley responded. The Chairman's services baoi,g been acknowledged the meeting termin- ated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT FOOT-</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT FOOT-</p>
        <p>BILL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the sub-committee of this Association was held at the Roebuck Hotel on Thursday last werk. Mr. J. Lewis presided, and there were also present Mr J F Whittle (hon. treasurer), T P Bradshaw (secretary), and Messrs P McHardy, J Bannon, and T Mair. Helens Recs applicd for permission to play their cup tie lon December 26th instead of December 17th and the ap- plication was granted sabject to consent of opposing team .Newton-le-Willows v Orford darracks,and Warring- ton Olympics v Gclbaroe were granted applications ta play cup ties on December 10t1.—Messrs P. Knight and D. McCormick wtre reported for misconduct is match Latchfcrd Brittania v. Latchford Reserve on Saturday October 22nd. After hearing the referee's statement it was decided to censure both players. J Unsworth was atso reported .for misconduct in same match and was suspended tor 14 days from Monday next. A,liton of Latchford Brittaaia was reported far rough play on Saturday,November sth, and was suspen- ded for 14 days from Monday next. A report by Mr. B. 'CAIeY Was ordered to stand over until next meeting. A letter from the War- rington A.F.O asking that the match Warrington v.New- ton Athletic be played on the Arplesi ground was read,the Newton rcpresentative eating that the Newton Athletic Club had arranged to play the match at Warrington. The Secretary reported that the match Barnton Rovers V. Warrington St. Mary's resulted in a draw of three goals each, and that in accordance with rule 11 would have to be played within 14 days, which expired on Saturday, December 3rd. It was decided that the match be played en that day, the referee to be ?dr, J. Lewis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>GENERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>A boat in which forty-five workmen were crossing the Volgo on Sunday, suddenly capsized, and twmty-nine per- sons were drowned.</p>
        <p>A special 1..0n0-,-M- correspondent hears on high authority that a marriage has been arranged between Prince George o Greece and Princes Victoria of Wales.</p>
        <p>Catherine Pezoy, aged eitiity--foriT,--wav burnt to death On Monday night by a fire which broke out in some industrial dwellings in Old St. Luke's, London.</p>
        <p>At Form ,y on Monday, the death took place of Mrs. Helen Formby, who is believed to have been 104 years of age. She was the wicow of a Formby lifeboat man. , By an explosion on board the Joaquin river steamer F. C. ' Walker. while en the way from San Francisco to Stockton, five persons were killed and eight b dly injured. A telegram from Havana states that a box of cartridges and gunpowder exploded on Monday morning near the lieina battery, killing or injuring forty pecans. Miss Mary Gellatly, Dundee, will recendy sned Piper Findlater for breach of promise of marriage, was quietly married to a carpet weaver at Dundee on Friday. During the preparation of the effects of the late Dr. Jeffrey at Barnsley for sale by auction the auctioneer's men found some old tins in a cupboard containing 255 sovereigns. Mr Mastland Kersey, from Klondike, sailed from New York on Saturday on board the Cunarder Lucania, having with him his great Klondike nugget valued at 50,000 dollars.</p>
        <p>Arthur Haynes, a tobacconist, was fined EDO at Clerken. well Police Court on Saturday for using premises for betting purposes. The parsons found on the premises were e 5.311 fined £5.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Spedding, a well-known manufacturer of Batley, committed suicide on Saturday morning by cutting his throat. Deceased was a prominent temperance advocate and local preacher. _</p>
        <p>IHarry Bowers and Benjamin Wasdelt, of Lower Genial!, have been aided at Brockmoor, Staffordshire, by a fall of masonry, due, it is believed, to the heavy snowfall, causing the earth to give way. Messrs. Von Straughton awl Zoon, butter merchants, of Manchester, were fined £4O and costs at the Leigh Police Court on Monday for giving a false warranty with butter and also for issuing a false label. On Tuesday. the Vicar of Wentworth, Roth-thorn, the rev. P. G. A. Verini, was fomd de id in his room. There was a bullet wound in his head, and near was an empty rifle For some time he has been in failing health. At Kilrush Police Barracks, on Sunday morning, Sergeant Tuohy accidentally shot himself In the right shoulder whilst removing his revolver. He was removed to the hospital, where he now lies in a precarious condition A man who is &amp;leo,' to have been concerned in the recent robbery of the Duchess of Sutherland's jewels in Prance was arrested on Monday in Lomb-n. Sotrie of the stolen pro- perty is said to have been found in his possession. A caravan in the, Soudan has been atta lred and captured by the Tibbao lierhada tribe at Cessalez, near Ghat, on the border of Fezzan. Light camel loads of feathers arid WO loads of Soudan skins were lost. Twenty persons were killed. While the ceremony of opening a new college was pro- ceeding on Sunday at Ptrosa, about thirty miles from Turin, 1 the hall of the college suddents collapsed, injuring a large number of persons. Amongst the most seriously injured were three bishops, one of whom has since died. A destructive fire broke out on Monday night at the boot and shoe warehome of Mr. Feldman, in BishopgatA, London, a building of four floors. The. fire broke out in the top floor, and thus the fl tines were prevented from spreading to the adjacent buiidings. The factory was seriously damaged. From North Truro. Massachusetts, confirmation is received of the loss of the American stenner Portland on Sunday morning, off the highland Light. All on board—forty-nine of a crew and sixty-five passengers—perished within a short distance of the Ind. Many of the bodies have been re- covered.</p>
        <p>At the Old Miley, on Tuesday, three men nareesl Potter, Bray, and Thorntoe were each sentenced to twelve strokes with the cat in adlltlon to imprlsonment—Thornton to eigh- teen months, Mee to twelve months, and Potter to six months' hard labour for highway robbery with violence near the Custom House, London. The stable-lad, Luddon, who on Monday was admitted to the Savernake Cottage Hospital. Marlborough, suffering from injuries to his head and body, died on Saturday. The lad w:s employed at Manton House. a Wiltshire training establishment, and it is alleged that he was the victim of illtreatmeat ,t someone's h ands. A shocking fatal accident occurred at the Robin 110041 Quarries, Rothwell, near Leeds, on Monday. It seems that about one o'clock a portion of the side of the quarry gave way, and fell upon the workmen, killing two and seriously incuring two others. The giving way of the land is attribu- ted to the recent inclement weather. On Sunday, Mr. James Pickering, a member of the firms of Thackrah and Pickering, one of the largest in the heavy woollen district, and residing at Dewsbury, cernrni ted suicide by cutting his throat with a razir Deceased, who I was a borough magistrate a d formerly member of the West was highly respected RiAdtinagn'inuiqnceisl; held on Tuesday. 9,t Hreenwlch, on the body of a German sailor. Wilhelm Hamann. aged 25, evidence was given shewin ' • that the deceased and a sailor named Schmidt, now in custody, had a quarrel. The jury returned a verdict of manslaughter against Otto Schmidt, who was committed for trial on the coroner's warrant, In the Divorce Court. on Tuesday, before Sir F. Jeune and a common jury, Mr J. T. Palmer, a fruiterer, of Windsor, obtained a decree nisi for the dissolution of his marriage on the ground of his wife's adultery with Robert Owen, a fruiterer, also carrying on business at Windsor. The jery awarded £3)O d amages ;teethed tee co-respondent, who was condemn- d in costs. An'agrarian outrage has been committee atlKilbaha, West Clare. Some cattle belonging to a farmer normal Green, who was bringing them to a local fair held oe Saturday, „ were in the morning found maltreated. A donkey had its ears cut off, and four heifers were tarred .11 over. Green is the occupant of an evicted f arm. and is coemeauently looked on with disfavour in some quarters. t flee large sum of £1,853. which represents the amoe it of he proceeds a the benefit aw %dad to George R Baker, the Lancashire cricketer, last summer, has just been paid over to that most deserving professional. The arnoun,. is by far the greatest gained by any Lancashire player,other benefits being: R. Pining. £1.e50 ; A. Watson, £1530; J. Briggs, £1,1).0 ; F. H. Bugg. £1.00) ;and it. G. Barlow.£l.ooo. While blasting operations were in progress at Heatheote, on the new Buxton to Ashbeurne railwey, about nine o'clock on Monday morning, two lmen named Joseph Sutton and George Redfern were terribly injured, owing to a shot going off unexpectedly. The latter succumbed to his injuries while on the way to the Devonshire Hospital at Buxton, whither they 'were removed on an engine. Sutton is ex- pected to recover. Two vrainen, said to he sisters, quarrelled in the early hours of sun lay morning after leaving a puolic-house in the neighbourhood of Miller's-court, Dorset-street, Spitalfields, one taunting the other with having just been in prison. Shortly after the women hid returned to their lodgings the qdarrel w s i'1•1 on hearing terrlole screams the neighbours . etered found one of the sisters, Lizzie Roberts, !Flee en the floor in a pool of blood, with a fatal wonnd in her chest. By the time :he police and a doctor arrived the unfortunate woman had succumbed. The other woman was arrested.</p>
        <p>Send . . F7,14..V.L; ra L.Fokr,arictcpci For Illusrztc.-.:." Ppice Lis! of</p>
