        <p>THE Gord on Memorial College The Gordon Memorial College. seems, at first sight, rather in the air. As expounded by Lord KITCHENER it is an attempt to introduce elemen- tary Western education into the Soudan,taking to begin with the children of the chiefs and notableS if they will come. We have no clear idea as to r whether there is any desire for the education it is proposed to give, and whether the college would be a success. Khartoum is to be the centre of the new life and light in the Soudan. Trade will flow to it, and Western ideas will find their way into Eastern minds. A college is, therefore, a part of the preparation for what is coming, and it is a good thing to start it at once, before any mischance can occur, and as an earnest of our intentions to the people of the Soudan. The air of Quixotism about the project will pass away on further exami‘ nation, because it will be seen that something had to be done to justify our good intentions and to make Khartoum a real capital. The name oE Goanox could not be very well perpetuated in any better way. Ile was a wise governor, and a real father to the people over whom ho ruled. The advent of Mandisra was a disturbance of no ordin- ary kind. It was erected on the ruins of the slave trade, of which Khartoum was at one time the life and the centre. For the time being it crushed out the life of everything which was good, and the whole resourcas of the people were de- voted to military pursuits and the support of military power. Mandisin has now passed away, and the Soudanese themselves are glad to be re- lieved from what had become a horrible night- mare. It is, heace, the right moment to inaugur- ate a new movement for linking Khartoum with Western civilisation,and enabling the Soudanese to see the difference between the military civilisation which has gone and that which remains. The col- lege will train the youog in secular knowledge only. There is to be no interference with the re- ligion of the people. It is not a mission college in this sense of the t,3rin. It is a memorial to a</p>
        <p>_ man who was an undoubted Christian, but who never seemed to any of the professors of other re- ligions with whom he was brought into contact .112 the least a propagandist. To preserve thio feature of his character in the management of the college is undoubtedly a good thing. It is pro- posod to give the Soudanese access to the advan- tages of Western ideas and literature without any intentional slur upon their IVlohammodanism. The</p>
        <p>future of Khartoum is one that greatly concerns us and we must begin the work by giving it civi- lised institutions as soon as possible. The city will need to be rebuilt physically as well as morally, ' and Colonel KITCHENER and the SIRDAR will find ' that they will have plenty of work to do for Boma , little time to come. England will give them sup"</p>
        <p>_ .... port and sympathy in their noble work, and the Gordon Memorial College will be taken up in ea most and made a success.</p>
        <p>The first may be uismissed as impracticable, and as one to which this country weuld never submit It is the extension of the French system of the Pass-book, requiring every man to carry about nn a little book, containing his name, occupation, and identifying details. Lot any British Govern- ment seek to introduce such a system into this country, and see what would happen. The plan would be rejected with scorn and indignation. Our working-men would never submit to it, and we do not see why they should do so. What have they done as a class that we should compel them in the interests of foreign Governments to carry a pass-book with them, and put it in the power of any officious policeman to demand the production of the book on the:slightest possible pretence ? The system would not work in this country. All that could be done in this way would be to impose some extra precautions upon travellers passing out of and coming into the country. The essport system, however, has never prevented. Anarchists from entering countries where their presence was not desired, or leaving them when they wanted to do so. To be effective the traveller's pass-book would need to comprise a photograph and some other particulars. The plan would check travelling, and perhaps it would also facilitate clever disguises. The second proposition is the formation of an international police force, stationed in certain recognised centres. There would not be any ob- jection to this course. In London, for instance, French, German, Russian, Austrian, and Italian police would be attached to our political section a" Scotland Yard, and British police would have to be similarly affiliated in St. Petersburg, Paris,Vienna, Berlin, and Rome. Working together, it would be possible for such a body to be informed of all the move Lents going on, and to recognise their countrymen despite all disguises. But even this international police, though connected with the the principal ports as well as with the capitals, could only identify persons, andcheck movements, and disseminate information. There would still have to be some plan of dealing with suspected persons. In short,they would have to be expelled and denied the right of asylum. Here, however, we come back to the point which has been much discussed of late,as to the difference between crimi- nals and mere revolutionary politicans.Some coun- tries may not care to make the distinction. But it is very wisely made in this country, and in a very comprehensive way. A Republician intent upon upsetting the house of Savoy, or a Monarchist wishful to upset the French Republic, as long as arms and assassination were not meditated, could claim an asylum in England. We should regret having to cede anything on this point. Lord PALXERSTON was beaten in his endeavours to oblige NAPOLEON lIL by legislating some part of this right of asylum away. Englishmen would hardly forgive any other statesman who attempted the same thing. Hence it seems that the diffi- culty involves English sentiments, and that the Powers may come to conclusions to all of which we shall be unable to subscribe.</p>
        <p>P' It isto belioped that the views expressed by the Grand Jury at York Assizes will not lose weight from any false humanitarianism. Everyone who has made any study of criminals knows that the men who do deeds of violence are:almost invariably cowards, and that the best deterrent against deeds of this sort is to return to them a percentage of the vain they have inflicted wantonly and viciously On their fellow creatures. It is far better to do this than to subject them to a long term of im- prisonment, which will make them less capable than ever of discharging their duties as citizens. The Common Sergeant at the Old Bailey this week ordered two men who attacked a clerk in West Ilam to each receive twelve strokes of the cat; and even Mr. Michael Davitt could scarcely take exception of this proceeding, seeing that for the mere purposes of robbery these two men clouted a harmless person about the head and neck, and eared not whether they killed him so long as they escaped. The spirit of the time is to reduce the hardship and vindictiveness of punishment ; but there must be discrimination between those who act under provocation and the lazy hounds of the earth, who are simply brutal.</p>
        <p>A case of great interest and importance to cyc- lists has been decided this week. The National Cyclists'Union brought an action against the Great Northern Railway Co. for the purpose of testing the right of railway companies to charge for the conveyance of bicycles, the argument advanced on behalf of the plaintiff being that a cycle was as nauch.entitled to be considered personal or ordinary luggage as, say, a gun-case, a fishing rod, or a pair of roller-skates. Mr. Justice Channell decided against the plaintiff, and his verdict calls for seri- ous attention on the part of all wheelers. The ground taken by the defendant company was that the term personal luggage is synonymous with baggage, and denoted some- thing in the nature of a bag or package. We must admit that there is as much reason in one argument as the other and Mr. Justice Channell could hardly have decided otherwise, dealing with the matter from its purely technical and legal point of view. But what we deplore is the neces- sity for any such test case. The managers of our railway companies, with few exceptions, seem to have adopted a golden rule to oppose all fresh de- mands made upon them by the public, instead of meeting their patrons half-way. If people are permitted to carry personal luggage within 1 a certain limit of weight, free, whyshould they not have the similar privilege in re Toot to their bicycles ? The conduct of the railway companies is selfish and inconsiderate, and in our opinion one more good reason why railways should be con- trolled by the State. It is probable that the dis- tinction drawn by Mr. Justice Channell will be tested in the Court of Appeal,when we hope that a result more satisfactory to the huge body of cyclists may be arrived at.</p>
        <p>One of the reforms which appears to get "no a:orrader "is that by which coroners' juries shall -LI) relieved of the obligation of viewing dead clies—a duty which they object to and Which is Tr•oductive of no beneficial results. People who have had any experience of the manner in which inguests are conducted will be well aware of the perfunctory and purely formal manner in which the bodiesare viewed. Whatever purpose this re- volting custom is supposed to serve, it gets abso- lutely no fulfilment. The futility of the proceed- •jjag, ix not the only objection to it, however. In snarly cases there is real risk taken by the jurorsin enterwg the presence of the dead and on Wed- itsday twelve good men and true ia Bolton made an effective protest against doing so. The inquest was relative to the death of a child from measles, and the jurymen objected strongly to entering -the house where the body lay, so to get over the difficulty the Coroner's officer placed it in a perambulator while the jurors viewed it from a safe distance in the street. The incident was a very unpleasant one for all concerned, and pre- sents a stroug argument in favour of the discon- tinuance of the viewing of bodies by unwilling jurymen. One properly qualified person could do all that is necessary in this direction and thus all these inconveniences and unpleasantnesses would be avoided. The Army authorities are, at present, much exorcised in mind as to the adoption of a really reliable, and up-to-date quick-firing field gun. ExPoriments made quite lately with one or two ‘poos believed by their inventors fully 'bie of "licking creation" have not expectations, and the War Depart- is naturally in somewhat of a 7, While we are desperately trying</p>
        <p>LP().tl5</p>
        <p>Weal capa</p>
        <p>re'alised</p>
        <p>ment-</p>
        <p>914ndary,</p>
        <p>first one gun and then anther, our French neigh- bours are perfecting their field artillery with the utmost rapidity and ease Some striking details of the performances of Frond' guns of this class are to hand. At recent mauceuvres a battery of the new quick-firers discharged twenty-one shells per gun per minute. A target consisting of 200 wooden soldiers was erected at a distance of two miles and a half, and in one minute and three- quarters every one of them was destroyed. We should imagine that nothing more effective as a "killer" could be desired, and perhaps our authorities would do well to take a leaf from the Fienchman's book. International piracy of this kind is quite legitimate, though rather difficult to carry out.</p>
        <p>As a post-mortem luxury cremation does not seem to increase in popularity ery rapidly. People are too conservative in their ideas to alto- gether relinquish the privilege of mouldering in the grave according to time-honoured custom. They have to return to dust and ashes, and they show a marked preference for doing so gradually, comfortably, and quietly. There are people, with more original ideas and less sentiment, who pre- fer the quicker process of evolution called crema- tion. And during the seventh year of the exist- ence of the Liverpool Orematorium Company, Limited, just completed, the earthly clay of twenty-three individuals was consumed " on the premises and while you wait." The Company wails rather mournfully about the backwardness of people in patronising their establishment, but takes consolation in the fact that the number of the consumed has trebled since the opening of the institution. What better augury could there be? People cannot be expected to long with any in- tensity for the "burning fiery furnace."</p>
        <p>The law of England embodies many anomalies, and even in acts of Parliament which are of recent date and presumably the better for the experi- ences of the past,flaws are to be found. One of the most modern enactments,the Workmen's Compen- sation Act is discovered to be by no means perfect, and in connection with it an interesting point has arisen recently. A workman in London, whose health had been seriously affected through his in- ' halino• the fumes of white lead while at work, claimed damages from his employers. The latter argued that the claimant's illness was not due to an accident in the meaning of the Act, and judg- ment was given for the defendants. In the wording of the Act "If in any employ- ment to which this Act applies, personal injury by accident arising out of and in the course of the employment is caused to a workman, the employer shall be responsible." Plaintiff's claim could not, therefore, be legally entertained, and the unfortunate fellow obtained no redress. It is undoubtedly hard that a workman who may obtain full recompense for injuries received in some unforseen accident, should be unable to get any compensation for seriously impaired health unmistakeably due to the conditions surrounding his daily toil.</p>
        <p>.I„ ! , i , , , , , , , • , ' , t t ',k .. '. t!, •.: ..it .; , , .. t. , ;Itt , 't t , ... b b • , , .. , • .'t A t , ',,</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MORPETH DOCK DISASTER. INQUEST ON A RUNCORN MAN.</head>
        <p>THE MORPETH DOCK DISASTER. INQUEST ON A RUNCORN MAN.</p>
        <p>A BARGEMAN'S EXCITING EXPERIENCE..</p>
        <p>The adjoureed inquiry hate the —cir-cumstances attend- ing the death of Arthur Williams, aged thirty-one, master of the flat James, and who lived in Albert-street RUCCetn, was resumed on Wednesday, at the City Coroner's Court, Dale-street, Liverpool before Mr. T. E. SamySon. Mr. Tongue watched the case on behaif of the Mersey Docks and Harbour Board, and Mr. Felder, (Weightman, Fodder, and ' Weiehtman) was present on behalf of the owners and captaia of the steamer Blanche Rock. George Williams, brother ef the deceased, said that the latter was a healthy man. He left ihome at nine o'clock on Monday morning, the 13th Octeber, to go to his flat James, which was lying in the Morpeth Dock, Birkenhead. The flat telem;ed to the Manchester Ship Canal Company, Deceased was in good health when be left home. Witness did not see him again. Thomas Hands, bargeman, who lived at 2. Wilson's street, Birkenhead, stated that on the 17th October he was mate of the flat James, then lying at the north side of the Morpeth Dock, Birkenhead. The deceased was the master of the flit Between half-past nine and ten o'clock at night on the 17th October witness was in the forward cabin of the flat, and the deceased was aft. The flat was securely moored with ropes fore and aft, and , several other flats were lying abreact of it. The first thing that drew witnesses attention to anything bring wrong was the forward mooring rone breaking. He then ran on deck, and found that the flat was being carried away with a tranendous rush of water, The deceased had by this time alto got on deck. A rush of water carried them under the bridge and alongtowards the outer gates, which he saw were broken and submerged. They were carried right over the gates, and at this time deceved was holding on to the middle hatches. That was the la it witness saw of him. Witness was ten holding on he the windlass, and as they passel over 1 the gates the flat dropped some distance, owing to the lesser depth of water in the river than in the dock. He supposed that was the moment when the deceased was washed away. He did not sea him again. The flat shot out almost into mideriver, and began to spin round and round till it was carried alongside a steamer, up the side of which he managed t a climb by means of the pilot's ladder. Other evidence showed that the body of the deceased had been picked up in mid-river, opposite Bronaborough Pool, on Thursday afternoon last week, and taken to the Prince's Dock mertuary. Hugh Jones, piermaster in the employ of the MerseV Docks and Harbour Board at the Merpeth and Egerton Decks, Birkenhead, said that on the night of the 17th October he was on duty at the deck, and was in charge of the Morpeth Dock entrance. There was a lock at the *armies Dock entranee. It was high water (18th llin ) at 12-24 a.m. on the 18th. About half-past nine wit- ness heard a whistle in the river, and he walked to- wards the river. He then saw the stern light of a vessel dropping down from the north. She got into the fairway for tie dock, and then let go her anchor. That struck him as being a little unusual. The steamer came on with her anchor paying out. It proved to be the Blanche Rock. She tame on close to the wall of the entrance, when witness sang out that she was to keep to the south side. He got a rope ashore, and made it fast to one of the mooring rapes. The steamer lifted her anchor, and the effect of that was to bring the ves- sel a little bit astern. Her stern would then be slightly act ass the entrance. He saw the master moving the telegraph, hut which way he cauld not say. As a mat- ter of fact, the vessel Ntartel to go slowly ahead. Seeing that, he hailed the master, and told him to go full speed astern and not ahead. The captain made some reply to the effect " Look at my ted graph ; its Fall speed astern,' " and at the same time he moved his telegraph right back. Witness at the same time heard the teli ringing. The vessel continued to come ahead riet withstanding. Witness hailed her several times to ge full speed astern, and when the steamer struck the south part of the outer gate he fancied the engine had been reversed. but it was too late. , There was a big smash, one part of the gate over- lapped the other, the glees fell into the river, and the water poured out. Immediately afterwards several flats were carried into edge river at a great speed. It was very dark at the time. and the?Elh ho hailed the flets,.he got no answer. Witness previous to the acci- dent saw the master run from the bridge down in the direction of the engine-room, acid heard him call out</p>
        <p>something. The evidence of the master of ths Blanche Rock, Thomas Bolhow, was that the orders he rang down to the engine-room after they had stopped ware Slow astern," and then. as he saw the vessel moving ahead, " Fall ;mead astern." He noticed that she was still r.,,oinq away ahead, and after shoutino• several times he ran down to the engine-roam himself, as the vessel stria, k the gate. The engines were then just on the point of being reversed. William Williams, chief engine?r, stated that at the time of the acoident he was on duty et the stoo valve, the second ,engineer was stationed at the telegraph to transmit orders, and a trimmer was on duty at the re- versing gear. Witness could swear that the order, after stopping, which he received from the second crtgiraeer Was "Full speed ahead." Shortly etterwarels he heard ' the steward call out, •t Full speed astern elle wints to go; she's going ahead." Ere then reversed the gear, but the collision fellowed -just afterwards. Be declared that the second engineer had given the wrong order. James ScheAeld, second engineer. stated that alter s'epoing for ahnut two minutes he received the order from the bridge "Astern slow" ; then immediately after- wards "Full speed astern." These orders he transmitted faithfully to the chief engineer. Michael Carroil,coal trimmer, who was on daty at the reversing wheel, said he heard the oecond engineer call out "Full speed ahead" twice. Having heard other evidence, ths jury returned a verdict that the deceased met his death by accidental drowning. They believed that the accident happened owing to the confusion either in conveying or executing the orders in the engine-room, bit felt that the captain bad done all In his power to avert the accident.</p>
        <p>CHINA THA SHRVICHS, Dinner Services, Toilet and Trinket Bets. Cheapest honse.-7. S. GARLA ND. Paraliblag Inantoog•r, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXAMINER' FRIDAY. DECEMBER 2, ISgB</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER' FRIDAY. DECEMBER 2, ISgB</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A SMALL SESSIONS AT RUNCORN. On Monday, at the Runcorn Petty Sessions before Messrs. A, Thomas and James Handley, there was hardly anything of interest, save the remanding of a prisoner to the next day, (reported in another column), and a few school cases in which Thomas Robinson (boatman), Peter Cawley, Taylor's Row and Thomas Whitby were fined 6d. and 43 61 costs.—Alr. J W Light. burn prosecuted on behalf of the School Board and evidence was given by Attendance Officers Bowyer and Howard.</p>
        <p>CONCERT LT PRESTON BROOK</p>
        <p>A concert party, in connection with the Runcorn Branch of the Mersey Altission to Seamen, gave a capital concert in the Preston Brook Mission Room, on Thurs- day evening (November 24th), the proceeds of which were in aid of the Mersey Mssion. The chair was taken by the Rev. H. J. Clarke, vicar of Daresigury, (who had kindly lent the room). The inclement weather bad an effect upon the attendance, but the quality of the items submitted was highly satisfactory. During the interval Mr. W. Shaw (missionary), in a few well chosen sen- tences gave an account of the work of the Mersey Mis- sion and also a resume of the good works that were be- ing accomplished by Missions to Seamen all over the world. A capital mu anal programme was submitted by the Misses CJward, L. Co ward.L. Shaw,M. Hill,Mrs. Outhwaite, and Mesers.Shingler, aill,P Shaw, J Woods, Outhwaite, Webb,Master Rutter and Master 0 uthwaite. After the usual votes of thanks to the Chairman and artistes the concert concluded with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL i3OA.RD ELECTION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL i3OA.RD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF THE POLL. Through out the week the candidates individually and collectively have been making a thorough canvass of toe electorate, and frorn early morning until late at night has the town and distriet been flooded with the usual election Squibs "in the shape of literature in- tended to secure the vote and sympathy of those res- ponsible for the selection. Meetings were held and stir- ring speeches delivered by both sidee. The town was literally flooded with the pamphlets, and the local bar- bers rejoice in that their supply of shaving paper has been visibly augmented. On the dey of the election (Wednesday) there was not so much interest evced as was generally expected, and up to 12 o'clock only shout aqa art 31. of tile vat ore had polled. But towards night the town assumed a much livelier appearance, sad busiuess at the several booths became more brisk. The arrangements made by the returning officer, Mr. John Ashtee (Clerk of the Rural District Council) for the comfort of the electors and tee recording of their votes, as we'l as for the counting which took place at the School Beard Offices, on Thursday afternoon, were highly satisfactory. The following were the officials at the several polling booths :—Heath Ward : 481 voters, presiding officer, Mr George F Ashton ; clerk, Mr J F Moulton; personating agent, Mr S Walton (progressive). Halton Ward : 389 voters, presiding officer, Mr A Lightburn ; clerk, Mr W Howard; per- sonating agents, Messrs E Frith (progressive), John Malt:erne (Father Gore). Mersey Ward : 559 voters, presiding caber, air 11 Wright ; clerks, Messrs. T Bowyer and G W Yates ; personating agents, Messrs A Riley (Progreisive),C Timmins, jun. (Church), James Caskey (Father Gore). Central Ward: 744 voters, presiding officer, Mr W E Hough; clerks, Messrs A E Hulse end J U Austin ; personating agents, Meesri C Handley (Progressive), George Smith (Church), Joseph O'Brien (Fateer Gore). Bridgewater Ward : 633 voters,preaiding officer,Mr Frank Johnson; clerks, elessrs J D Jones and George Howard ; pers3nating agents, Messrs W Gerard and El 'fonks (Cnurch),Robert Wright (Progressive), Tuompson (Father Gore). Nawtowe Ward : 604 voters, presiding officer, Mr El V Linaker ; clerks,Messrs C Leach and F Ashton; persenating agent, Mr T Lea (Progressive). Just a little after half-past one o'clock on Tharaley the poll was declared by tee Returning Officer (3/Er. Jo au Ashton) as follows : Rev Canon Wood 2601 Mr W J Cunningham 2515 Mr E Imison. 2510 Mr J Littler Mr F J Norman Rev H B Gore 1990 Mr James Pritcnard Mr S Taylor 1855. Me William Hanley Mr John Wilson Mr W Cotlir 667 The first nine were declared elected. Mr. E. Imison proposed a hearty vote of thanks the returning officer for his impartiality and for the ex- cellent manner in which the electi on ha I been carried out. Mr. Samuel Taylor seconded and the vote was heartily aeccrded. Mr. John Ashton ore behalf of himself and the offiaial A connected with the election responded in suitable terms. It will thus be seen thee the composition of the Board is precisely the same as that of the old one viz , fur Churchmen, four NoncenformiNts, and one Roman Catholic ; and with the exception of Messrs. Littler, Cunningham, and Handley, all were members of the old Board. It may be interesting to state that the number of Voters on the rate book was 3,415, which multiplied by nine gives the interesting figure of 30,735 votes, of which 22,001 were recorded, which can be c tiled a very heavy poll. After the result became generally known the success- ful candidates were the recipients of general cengr {Lula- tione. The result was reeeived with very little excite xient, but with manifest surprise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A BORRESP3NDENT..I The School Board election is now a thing of the past, and it is only to be regretted that once again the management has fallen into the hands of the Voluntary School supporters. Not that I would cast reflection upon these gentlemen personally, but it is the principle that I aim at.</p>
        <p>Well, those now responsible for the management will, without doubt, be continually reminded of the fact that they have gone in on the plea of "down with the rates," and it is to be hoped that for the purposes of future elections this important plank in the platform should not be lost sight of. Let us see how far the rates will go down. Die result is, however, an objec,-, lesson to all, and plainly demon- strates that an election cannot be won without vigorous fighting.</p>
        <p>Apropos of the fuss and hubhub made during the School Board election over the" saving of rates," it is worth while calling attention to the munificence of Sir John Brunner, whose generous gift to the town of a new wing to the Technical School does constitute something in the nature of a " saving of rates." In the first instance there is a saving of £5OO the grant given by the Science and Art De- partment. Then there is the building itself which will not only be handsome, but its very erection will enhance the already fine structure that exists, as well as increase its value.Further,its erection,will no doubt be entrusted to Runcorn contractors, and a further benefit will be reaped by the townsfolleon that score. The vote of thanks to Sir John Brunner is expressive of the feelings of the whole of the townspeople and a genuine ebullition of grateful- ness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>FOOTB ALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTB ALL.</p>
        <p>Saturday's display -hy—R—un-c-o-,:n shows that thwe is still at least a portion of the team that is un- equalled, viz., the forward rank. This is not the first time they have shown their metal—as witness Oldham—and will not be the last. And though it is to regretted that Runcorn's prestige has de- clined it is my belief that the low water mark has been reached and that the future will sse the Run- corn team rise once more to its former pre- , eminence.</p>
        <p>Because I venture to make a few remarks con- cerning the young players those veterans who form the backbone of the club must not think that any slight of them is intended. They are as good as ever. But a youngster like Farster does deserve a mead of praise for his display. He is a clever fellow at the game, and won golden opinions on Saturday. Bowen has a lot to learn and though he shows good form occasionally the whole of his work lacks finish.</p>
        <p>Upon the whole S(turclay's match 07 as "hard lines" for Runcorn, who pressed their opponents throughout and were in their half of the ground for three parte of the game. Had Houghton been present and Forster in place ot Bowen there is every probability that a different result would have been recorded.</p>
        <p>Very little is heard now of J. Butterworth aid J. Faulkner, who still hold alolf from the club. As it is quite evident that they do not intend turning out for Runcorn, I think the Committee would show good sense if they glve these players the transfer to Warrington that they so much desire. They me certain to go elsewhere at the end of the season and the Runcorn Club will then lose no only their services but the handsome sum offered by Warringten for their transfer. To do this would be the height of folly, and I trust the Committee will make the best of the bargain while they have the opportunity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN WESLEYAN TEMPERANCE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WESLEYAN TEMPERANCE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING,</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Runcorn Wesleyan Tem- perance Society was held at St. Paul's Church, on el on- thy evening, when there was a large attendance. Tee chair was occupied by Mr. T. Simister (in the absence of Mr. Harry Baker), and there were supporting him on the platform, the Rev. S. Wilkes (District Temperance Secretary), Rev. C. W. L. Christien, Rev. J. A. 8.1 Malvern, Mr. D. Bisbrowa, Mr. A. Rowe (Halton), and! Mr. J. D. Jones (local secretary). 1 The Rev. C. W. L. Christian read a letter of apology from Mr. Harry Baker. Mr. J. D. Jane; (hon sec.) submitted the annual re- port of the Runcorn Circuit Wesleyan Temperance Society, which was as follows : "Toe General Temper- ance Secretary(Rev.G.Armstrong Bennetts)reports a very encouraging increase in both sections of the Temperance work of the Connexion. The figures are 4,634 Bands of Hope, an increase of 921 and 1,553 temperance societies, an increase of 119, with 87,389 members, an increase of 2,463. The Liverpool District has transferred two cir- cuits (Wigan and Cherley) to the Bolton District ane after deducting 123 Baude of Hope and 2,561 members which the change involved, there is an actual increase of 3 Bands of Hope and 163 mem- bers, making for the entire district a total of 200 Bands of Hope and 24,415 members. There is no circuit in the whole district without a Band of Hope. The Temperance Societies in the district were much less satisfactory. thero being an actual decrease of eight societies and 335 members. We must leak for the explanation to the St.Helene' circuit which returns a decrease of three societies and 619 members, and at the same time an increase in the Band of Hope Section of 339. The Runcorn district can boast of a Band of Hope in con- nection with each of its eight societies with a member- ship of 1,181. (Applause.) With regard to the Temperance Societies it is gratifying to riven that a vigorous so- ciety with 70 member; hae been esta.bliihed at Halton, and that another is in eourse of formation at Weston Point where a most encouraging evangelic work is in progress. The work of the Circuit Union will not be deemed satisfactory until 8 societies instead of two have been e tab ished, and the 467 members have increased to .1,000. (Applause.) All temperance people will re- joice at the resolution unanimously passed at the last conference, marking as it does a distinct advance in temperance sentiment. During the coming year a Smithy closing Campaign is to be carried on under the leadership of Mr. Arnold F. Hills. It is hoped that dur- ing the life of the present Parliament the boon of a sober Sabbath may be mocured by passing a law te prevent the carrying on of the worst of trades on the best of days. (A.ppleue.) The temperance workers throughout the country- continue as earnest as ever in seeking to era- dicate the great evil of drunkenness. The battle is be- ginning in earnest' and the question is shall the interest of the public or the publicaa be docnieant ? (Applause ) Locally, we have to record that once more the Bench of magistrates have very wisely refosee to gran:, an applic lion for for an outdoor license in a very respectable dist tict in Higher Runcorn. A very strong feeling prevails that it is grossly unfair on the part of the brewers to make such a neighbourhooe, inhabited by thrifty respectable workpeop'e—(applau;e)—the dumping ground for ders- dot beersheps. (App'aus ,). To their honour be it said, the Runcorn magistrates have set their face against what is known as •' licence by barter." (Applause.) Throueh the vigilauce f the Runcorn Women's Temper. aims Society an abaailoned license was objected to so that it could not have a resurrectien—(applauae)— a trck the pre—'69 licenses poesess. It is to be hoped that the members of the Runcorn Circuit Temperance Society will be faun I ready to cake their places in the tanks when the bugle calla to arms. (Loud applauee)." The Chairman said he had been looking forwarl wits great pleasure, and was sorry that he had not the opportunity of hearing Mr. fl. Barker. His (the speaker's) convictions wita reference to the drink traffic had been intensified, and deepened as year after year went on. He V 7 a a s'ronger abstainer than ever. It was a fact th it made h m 'eel gritaful,andhe hoped that his convictions w ,uld grew even stronger. Whilst he de• plorel the fact that 90 out of every 103 adults who signed ths pledge went back to thee bad habits, he re- joiced that 90 out of every 100 children who signal the pledge kept it. (Applause). Thus there was a strone reason for them to Inok alter the young people. (Ap- plause.) It seemed to hem that the one hope, or at least the principal hope, lay in the young. Therefore it be- hoved them to ent.ce the young to keep the pledge. (Applause.) Tee Rev. C. W. L. Christien delivered a stirring ad- dress upon the temperance question, in the COUES9 of which he sail them was net-erne, mere dreadful than to witnees suffering that they coeld not aid. The liquor tr fffic was organised—he woald even say rcemsed—cruelty. It was the means of inflicting erevi- one suffering upon peep's,. The public houses were always set at the corners of their best streets, and in the best -parts of the town and he often wished that they could have erected chapels there. They stood there land drew in the population, dragging them into the very vort ix of degradation. He would remind them of the words of the late Mr. Gladstone that strong drink was respoasible for more deaths than the tier ie terrible visitants of pestilence, famine and war. Gen Tal 1 Booth, a man whose experience could be relied upon, had also saidlhat nine-tenths of the misery of thia coun- try wan brought about by strong driek. Ire London they could go rOuni hundreds of miles of streets where they would not fin I the average wage of the inhebitante amount to 10s a wo.de, and where they would find sie families in a halve, and p rhaps one oF these families taking in lodgers Po as to help them to exOst. They would find the public houses flourishing in t one streets. Those houses were thickset whose the people were poor- est. It was a striking though lamentable truth, that those who were least able to pay were first to buy the drink. (Hear, hear.) He was desirous of driving a bargain with these p sople who took the money spent in drink over their counters, which was that re y -hould give him all the money that did harm. It would be a vast amount, as some £132,000,000 had been spent in the Ilquor traffic in the last year. Mr. Christiee also spoke of the vast fortunes that were made by the brewers, discillers and big brewery companies, all of which money he affirmed could never have been made butthreugh .the degradation of the manes of the people (Applause.) It appeared that people were not content with this dangerous work being carried on dui lag the foe days of the week but wanted the seventh—the Lord's day. He thought that man was subject to plenty of eempta'aion without beine, open to it on the Sabbath. (Applause.) There were other places tt go to without the drink shops. They wanted a Sunday Closing Act and their work would never be accomplished until it was obtained. ( Applauee.) Therefore he felt c infident of their support in moving the following resolution : " That this meeting representing the We;leyan Mettle- dist Church in the Rancorn Circuit, hereby exorese its profound sorrow for the widespread evils resulting from Sunday traffic in strong drink, and earnestly appeals to Her Majesty's Gevernsnent to introduce a Bill to Parlia- ment for Sunday Closing in England, thus to mitigate the evils, and give to England the boon that has been already granted to Ireland, Scotland, and Wales. The mewdrig further reaolyes that cool is of thja r Isqlu-jon he se!nt to. the Premier and the Home Secretary and the member for the Dvision." (Loud applause ) The Rev. S. Wilkes, in Seconding. the resolution spoke of the petition to Parliament from Cornwall, where they were desirous of having a Sunday Closing BO!. They went to interview Sir William Hammett and he heel ex- pressed the willingness of hia eaate to do all e ponitve But what had they not to contend with, they —when they arrived its the lobby of the Howie 60 strong from Cornwall—holed it filled with the pail agents of the organised litre r ttaffic button-heline all the mem- bers they could lay hands up an. Yet still in the petition Presented before the 11011 SS against them there waa not a single ejgrraeurt from Cornwall. (Apple) T. 17 ,eere all from other parts. Why did those peop'e take so great an interest in the affairs of CornwAli ? It becau4o they ve,r, afraid. (Applause ) Afraid tee:: (one little corner ot the country should maks a start. And thus is, hammed that in spite of the fact that 130,000 signed the petition there was no Sunday, ninsinp. in Cornwall. They were all aereed upon the ad- visibility of passing a Sunday Closing Hill,that was witi the exceotion of those who were eigavel its tho drink traffic, therefor,, the (speaker), urged them that they should do all in their power to obtain the pessing of each a law. He had giving up abusing the House of Commons for the House was what they made it. (Ap- o sure.) So it behoved thent to see that their represen- tatives were pledged to a Sunday Cloaine Ace, end if they voted for no other they would seen hive a g meat in power strong enough to give them what they asked for. (Applause) After the TEM geritteman has given same graphic aeceounts of the evils ef drink, he concluded his remarks by subrnittine several variable suggestions for the carrying on of the work of the Tem- perance CAUSE, locally. At the conelusion Of his eloquent ! address Mr. Wilkes was heartily applauded. The re- ! solution was then put and unanimously carried. The Rev. .1. A. B. Malvern proposed and Mr. D. Si,- brown seconded, hearty votes of thanks to the chairman speakers, and the little children for the part they had taken in the gathering, which were accorded with ac- clamation. During the evening the following rseitatiene were even :—" A Child's Prayer," Mies Ryan ; " Tsmeerate Be" retitle Marion Greed t;" A dr inkard's ! " Master ! Joseph Bold ; "The Drink Lion" Master Jelin Parry. The meeting dispersed after the singing of the " Doxology."</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT T 3 CONSUMPTIVES. DR. LO/ICK'A PETLMOItIO WAFERS —Mr. W. Saunders, Devonshire Cottage, York Square, Stepney, 11. writes The Wafers have cured me of Consumption—the dire disease that has smitten down many of my family—and I write to tell sufferers how they may be cured, and should any adopt this rateßt of remedies through my instrumentality. I shall feel I have not been spared in vain.' In Asthma, Consumption, Bronchitis, Coughs, Colds, Shortness of Breath. Phlegm. Pains in the Chest, and Rheumatism, they give instant relief and a rapid I cure DR. LOCOCR'S WAFERS taste pleasantly. Bold at is. Id., 2s. 9d., 4s. 64„ sad lls. per bair, oy all Druggists. —Advt. (5)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Council was held at the Town Hall on Thursday morning, when there were present : Messrs. Samuel Taylor (presid ng), W. Hand. ley, D. Bisbrown, F. Wiswall, E. Grice, J. Wilson, E. Gatley, T. Dean, W. Collier, J.Horsefield, G.Howitt, Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk), Mr. E. Marshall (secretary), Mr. J. Wilding (surveyor) and Dr. F. McDougal (Medical officer.)</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer reported that the number of deaths for the month of November registered was 28, of which 4 were inquest cases. The number of births was 42. For the corresponding period of last year the num- ber of deaths was 22 and the number of births was 51. Mr. D. Bistirown said there was an idea abroad that diptheria was very prevalent in the town. Was it so ? Dr. McDougall said there was a great deal of alleged diptheria, but there was a gratifying diminution in the number of cases notified. Mr. Collier : There seems to be a prevalence of births. (Laughter.) Dr. Mc Dougall : I think that the births are very satisfactory indeed. There is no diminution at all. (Loud laughter.) The recommendations of the Highways and Buildings Committee were unanimously adopted, on the proposi- tion of Mr. F. Wiswall. seconded by Mr. D. Bisbrowa. SIR JOHN BRUNNER AND THE TECHNICAL</p>
        <p>EDUCATION OF RUNCORN.—A GENEROUS OFFER. Ihe Technical Instruction Committee reported that sometime ago thy requ(stsd Sir John T. Brunner to wait on the principal Secre •:1157 of the Science and Art Department with a view to inducing the Department to forego its stipulation that the Lower Art School b 6 not used for Secondary Day Sch 00l purposes. Sir John wroto the Department asking for P.O appointment and was informed by the Secrecary. that they would be pleased to furnish him with any information at the pro- posed incerview, but taat the Registration of the Art School must be considered as stopped. Before pro- ceeding to London, Sir John informed the Secretary .to the Council that he entertained no hope of gening the Department to recede from the position it hat taken up and suggested that, rather than repay the Grant ot £5OO, a room be built to accommodate the Day School, co that the Council would bay° some value for its money. The following is a copy of tho letter received relative to the interview :</p>
        <p>Druid's Cross, Wavertree, Liverpool, 4th November, 1898. Dear Mr. Marshall,—l had a very pleasant interview with Mr. Tweddell and Mr. Cole at the Science and Art Department, on Wednesday morning. They aesured me that if we will undertake to build an addition to the In- stitute on the lines of Mr. Wilding's plan, within a reas- onable time, and will thereafter res eere the Art Room strictly for the purposes of Art teaching, there will be no fear of our losing toe Annual Grant, or of tneir asking us to return the Building Grant. I venturA to say that there was no doubt of the willingness of our Committee to give the undertaking and I now aek you to be good enough to submit the matter to them, and to rok them to permit me ti defray the ces-, —Yours faithfully, Some T. Bereesert. The Committee further rec ommended that the Com- mittee and Council expreae their er %tofu' thanks to Sir John Brunner for his trouble in interviewing the Science and Art Department on their behalf and their appreciation of the eug.eetion contained in hi 3 letter of November 4th, as to complying with the requireinenti of the Department. Also that the plan of an addition of a wing to the Technical Institute prepared and submitted by the Surveyor be approved, and that tenders for the execution of the work be obtained. 'the following is a copy of a letter received from the Science and Art Department in reply to one from the Secretary asking if they will continue the Regietration of the School of Art in case the Council pledges itself to erect an additional room at the Institute :- 21st November, 1898. Rurcern : Technical Institute, Waterloo Road. School of Science, No. 9,157. Sir,—l am directed to acknowledge the receipt of your letter of the 11th instant, and to state that the recognition of the School of Art will be continued for the current Session, at the end of which it is presumed that the new accommodation referred to in your letter will be completed and that the rooms of the School of Art will be wholly devot'e:l to instruction in Art in accordance with the conditions of the Science and Art Directory—lam, Si r,Your obedient Servant,E ward Belehaw. E. Marshall, Esq., Town Hall, Runcorn. Mr. Wileon moved the adoption of these minutes and said it had fallen to his lot, since he had been connected with the Institute, to move s enrol resolutions of that character. The resolutions that morning wore only an. other proof of the valuable aid of Sir John Brunner in becoming a member of the Committee, and of his gener- ous co-opera,ion with the educationalists of Runeorn to provide for the needs and aepirations of the people: Mr. D. Bisbrown said he kal great pleasure in second- feeling of the committee and the public general'y f ing the resolution. The resolution represented the sin- eoerreSir John Brunner's kindness in corning forward at such a critical time and helping them out of a difficulty. The Chairman supported the resolution. It showed the deep interest that Sir John Brunner took, and he had come forward frequently and very generoualy to the help of education in Runcorn, and he was sure that every ratepayer and individual in Runcorn would feel most grateful to him for his generosity on this occasion. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>. . Mr. W. Handley pointed out that the Coromittne could not propose a resolution of thanks or acceptance, as they thought these resolutions should come from the Council.</p>
        <p>The Chaim:au said that heing so he had great pleasure in moving that the Council on behalf of thn Cmimittes and the peoplc of Runcorn accapt Sir John Brunner' s gift, and record their deep gratitude for his munificent kinduess.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Wilson seconded the motion, which was unani- mously carried. The minutes were also confirmed. MR. COLLIER AND HIS SCHOOL BOARD RITE. Under the head of 't General Business." M. W. Collier asked the Chairman, as overseer—and he honed that he would reply—for information for the pub'io— . what was the School Beard rate fir the last year ? I Either be had p tid too little or too much. He had paid Bid. in the pound arid it had been stated that the rate was 70. He would Ike to know definitely. The Chairtwal I d think that COTOSI within my province as Chairman of the it-petit:az. Mr. Collier : lan asking you as overseer. The Chairman : lam not here as overseer I am hero as Chairman of the Council. Mr. Collier I think there is no teas en why the in- forination.should be withheld ? The Chairman said that without having time to go in- to the figurts ho did not think he ought to be expected to answer such a question at a moment's notice. It Mr. Utllier would nill a public meeting—say at the Pubic Hall—he would be pleased toattend after due notice and give all the figntes. Mr. Collier : I will give you the filures if you will vouch for them. The Chairman : I shall not de so. I will not vouch for them.</p>
        <p>FlAnd.oy Raid that Mr. Collier ought to give netiee of such a question which involved the explanation of accounts. No doubt the Chairman would be ready, al an overs,.er, to Rive Mr. Collier an explanation. Whether it would be satisfactory to him or not wa3 another</p>
        <p>question. Mr. Collier : You are a well-informed man, Mr. Handley, and perhaps you can give me the informaton. (Laughter.) Mr. Handley : I dare say that I could, but lam not an overseer. Mr. Collier askod the Chairman if he brought the sub- ject up at the next Council would he have the figures prepared. The Chairman : I won't promise you. Mr. Collier : I have either paid a penny too much, or a penny too little, and I think the public ouuht to be made acquainted with the true facts. There were men round the board who could give him the information. If not, he would give them the figures if they would say whether they were correct or not. Mr. Wiswall : Is this the whole of the business ? The Chairman : I have nothing more. Mr. collier : It's all right ! Just say you will not answer the question ? The meeting then concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH BAZ&amp;AR. —A bazaar, cake fair, and jumble sale were held in the Parochial School, Halton,on Friday. The sale was opened by Mrs. Talbot, of Aston Hall. Owing to the size of the Parish it bad been found necessary to build a mission church,and this necessitated the aid of a curate to carry on the work. The proceed* for the day realized 253 3s. Id. ASX for CHINNING-MAWS HIGH-CLAS3. MINOAL WAT4BS.-4firt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WESTON POINT.</head>
        <p>WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>TEA MEETING AND PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>The annual tea rneetine in connection with Christ Church, was held on Friday last, when there was a a large number of friends present. After the tables had been cleared a capitl entertainment was provided, con- sisting of a pianoforte solo, " Blake's Greed March," by Miss M Orme ; songs contributed by Mrs Santos, Mrs Carlow, Misses Done, F Musgrave, and a recitation given by Miss Berrington. The comic element was introduced by Mr. Alf. Grounds. The tableaux which were taken by the lady members, were vc ry pretty and realistic, and created quite a farere of enthusiasm. The following subjects were presented:—" Shepherdess," Miss McLean; " Venetian Lady," Miss B. McLean ; " Pear's Soap," Miss Marshall ; " Cinderella," Miss Stott ; " New Woman," Miss Limb ; " Masher," MiSq L Dunbavin ; " Mary Contrary," Miss Grounds " Jockey," Miss D Bankes ; " Early English," Miss Bankes ; " Grecian Lady,"" Dancing Girl," and " A Puritan Maid," by Mrs G Rathbone.—An interesting part of the proceedings was a presentation to Mr.R.Cookson (who for fourteen years has been head master of the day schools on the occasion of his marriage.—The Rev.D. S. Bowen said he was pleased to be able to assure them that with regard to the church they had been making progress, and they had been more euccessful than in aay previous year. The only drawback was the organ. He did hope that they would try to get rid of the present organ, and replace it by one that was better adapted to the aervices. (Hear, hear.) Five years ago the average attendance at Sunday School was 80, last quarter it was 132. After some further remarks flee rev, gentlemen called upon Mrs. Bankes to present Mr. Cookson, on be- half of the villagers, with a service of plate, consisting of 83 pieces, and a dinner service of 86 pieces.—Mrs. Bankes, wh) was received with applause, said that she was very pleased to be able to present to Mr. Cookson the handeorpo gifts. She was quite sure they welcomed Mr. Cooksolfand his wife amongst them, and hoped they would have a bright and happy future. (Loud applause.)—Miss Barrington, on behalf of the children of the day school, said that she had much pleasure in presenting their head master with a dozen large knives, a dozen small ones, a game carver, and a meat carver. She wished him very much happiness.— Mre.Cooks m's Bibie class then presented him with two solid silver serviette rings.—Mr. H. Turner made the presentatiou,and said that they thought it was the most suitable time to show the ie appreciation of their teacher's services. (Applause.)—Mr. Cookson, who was warmly greeted, thanked them for the presents. He could not imagine what he had done to deserve them, and they had come to him as a great surprise. He valued them for the good se ishee they broueht for, what a friend of his had styled, his'' new venture." With reference to the Biole class, Mr. Cooksen said he was extremely ! proud of it. It was a clap, the members of whin he had breught up himself from their boyhood. He could safely say that a member had never left him for any ether reason beyond that of removal. He begged to think them once more. He should always value the testimonial, not only for its intrinsic worth, but also for their good wishes and goodwill, and for the sake of " Auld Lang Syne." (Applause.)—Mr. Cookson, on be- half of the choir, then preiented Mr. Wa ltrr catlow,who is leaving the di.rict,with 3 vols. of Eilicett's Commen- tary, and a voluele of vesper voluntaries.—Mr. W. Tat- low in brief terms thanked them for their kied words and useful present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>TYPHOID FEVER Ar RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this authority was held on Thursday, Alderman Walton iSaaith presiding. Dr. J. 13. Crocker, the medical officer, reported that during the month of November he had inspected 125 ships, of which 103 were British. Insanitary or defective con- ditions wer,. found on 32 of them. From one ship three ,of the crew had been under medical treatment for typhoid lever at Runcorn, but in all other cases there were no infectious disease on hoard. On the motion of Mr. A. G. Copeland (Manchester), seconded by Alder- man W. Robinson (Salford), Alderman Walton Smith was re-elected chairman of the authority. Mr. Huddart (Salford), was re-appointed deputy choirman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>---...-.„... FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>---...-.„... FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>TRINITY MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.—The monthly open meeting', of this class wa3 held in the Lecture Room on Sunday afternoon, when Mr. Charles E. Linaker pre- sided, and referred to the work which the President of the Class (Mr. W. D. Ringrose) was carrying on so suc- cessfully in eulogistic terms. Mr. F. Boston resumed his admirable discourse on the friendship which existed between David and Jonathan. He described the manner of making and keoping, true friendships, as well as of the wr.ck of friendships. Daring the proceedings Miss J. S. Linaker contrihuted two sacred solos, which v.ere much enjoyed. The attendance was so numerous that many could not he admitted to the building. PETTY SESSIONS.—Ori W,sdnesday, at the Petty Sen. sional Court Howe, before Dr. Steel (presiding), and Mr. John Ocklestomthe following cases were heard.—Thomas Brown, Booth-rreet, Frodsham, for being drunk and disorderly in Booth-street, on the 22nd October, was fined 26:1 and 8 61 costs.--Arthur Hulse, Arthur ,Hen- cook, William Allman, and Oliver Morris, boys of a ten- der age, were summoned for setting off fireworks in the streets, on trio 20th October, and were orJered to pay the costs 4s 61—Ernest Ainsworth, Booth-street, for using obscene language in Bath-street, on the 12th Novemb'r, was fined 51 and Be 61 costs.—Sarah Ann Rogers and Elizabeth Fletchor, for using obscene langu- age, on the 12th November, were each fined 5s and 83 61 Winson, fartnor, Dunham, Overton Hills for allowing a cow to stray along the highways in that district, was fined 53. This case was proved by Acting Sergeant Young.—Joseph Jepps, junior, Helshy, applied for a certificate of exemption from vaccination in respect of his 4 year old child. The child had been suffering for a long time, and vaccination had to be put off from time to time. The disease the child suffered from was water on the brain.—The application was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THE NEW VICAR OF ST. THOMAS'S.</p>
        <p>The R-e;.-14—.V..Pig. vicar of St. Paul's. War- rington,has been appointed v"car of St. Thomas's Church Stockton Heath. He will succeed the Rev. T. S. 'Pelts, who rwiened the living, whill is in the gift of Sir Gilbert Greenall. Bart., in conssqaence of ill-health. Mr. 131,r0t was appointed vicar of St. Paul's in January, 1891, and during his connection with the pariah much valuable work has been done, and his removal will be much regretted. Mr. Pigot will take up his new duties early in the New War.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CEICTRCTIES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CEICTRCTIES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCII.-CHORAL</p>
        <p>SERVICE..</p>
        <p>There was a crowd,id-congrec,raliaa Ira Wycliffe Church en Sunday eyeliner when tile choir, aasisted by a few friends, gave seleaiiola froin Gaul's cantata " Toe Holy City." A smail,but effi :lent instrumental band was also in attendance,composr.d of the following :—First M. R H.Peareon and Mr.E.H.Clare; viola,Mr.T.Peplow. basi, Mr. R Woitfield ; reload violins, Mr. A. Knowles, and Mr. W. Brown ; 'cello, Mr. H. Boad ; harp, Mrs. F. Pcndlebury. The baton was capaVy wielded by Mr.W. H. Payton, and his accustomed place at the ergan was filled by Mr. F. Hickman. After the tune- ful introductory—" contemplation," the congregation jelled in singing a hymn. The Rev. John Yong., prior to reading the lesson, made a few observations. He said the musical service that evening was not to be considered in the nature of a performance. They must remember that they were met for the worship of God. After referring to the elevating influence of music he expressod a hope that they wo old he spiritually the better for having met within those walls that night. After prayer had been offered, concluding with the inton- ing of the .Lord's Prayer, the chorus, tenor solo, and qoar,ist "No hadows yonder" was Rung. Mr. A. Ceriffiths efficiently took the solo part, and in the quartet he WAS accompanied by Miss E. Kershaw, Miss F. Booth, and Mr. J. Knowles. Mr. 4. Griffiths, in audition, gave a finished interpretation of the air "Me sonl is athirst for God." Daring the evenin,c, solos were well rendered by Mrs, R. H. P-arson, "These are they which came " ; Miss Booth, "ETe bath not seen" ; and Mr. F. Fieldhouse, " A new heaven and a new raeth," and "I hcard the voice of harpers." Mis. Pearron also sang in the qoartat ard chorus for female voices " Liet the Cherubic Host," in crimpany with Miss Boot, Miss E. Ilioowles and Miss Wonversley. In the trio "At eventide it shall de light," Mrs. S. W. Jeffery, Miss E. Knowles, and Miss Booth took part, arid the same vocalists also sang ia the quar- tet ire which they were joined by Mies Wernersley. " Great and marvellous nrc Thy works." The choral parts by the choir were exceptionally well sung and the effectiveness was considerably heightened by the performance of the instrumsttalkts. In addition to accompanying the vocalists, the band gave a fine reua. daring of the intermi-zzo, " Adoration." The congre• . Ration joined in singing several hymns appropriate to the oacasion, an I also sang the last verse of the chorus "For the-. 0 dear, dear country." A collection was taken an ty-Inif of the rhcir funds and realised £B3s 3.0 d</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON- MARKETS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday, new wheat (white) fetched 4s 04 to 4s 24 (701 b to the bushel); red wheat, 3s lid to 48 ; new oats, 2s 01 to 24 44. Flour, best seconds, per 2401 b, 203 to 21s Od Bran, lid to Is per 201 b. In the War- rington Provision Market, fresh butter, is to is 3d per lb; fresh eggs, 6 and 7 for is; fowls 4s 64 to be per couple; ducks, .58 to 53 5d ; English beef, 5d to 104; mutton 64 to 93/44 ; veal, 64 to 104 ; pork 6d to</p>
