        <p>Every person's future is To a certain extent in his own hand. Many a future which might have been bril- liant has been darkened by lack of timely preparation to meet the exigencies and changes which occur in every day life. Many a home which might have been glad- dened by life an i health, and coaafort,has been saddened by poverty, illiess, and death, in many cases wholly preventable. There are, indeed, few that do net feel the system needs recruiting after the Irving feeling of lassitude, with others, "not quite up to the mark," "a , sort of all over sinking feeling," " too weak to do any- thing a7out the house," "these recurring headaches," "no appetite for my food," etc. These, and a dozun ' others, are common expressions which all point to the urgent need of some good tonic mixture, a reliable me- dicinelof established reputation and of proved virtue. There are several tonic mixtures to be had, hut none which have been so uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters; it has never been known to fail. It's always the same, there can be no mistake about it. The careful preparation and a manipulation of manufacture under the most srringent rules, assures the certainty that Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Tonic, is always efficacious and genuinely successful in all eases of Indigestion,Weakness, Nervous- ness, Low Spirits, Sleeplessness, Dyspepsia, Liver Com- plaints, N iuralgia, and Infiuerza. We claim that Gwilym Evans's Quinine Bitters is a General Benefactor of Suf. feting Humanity. Hundreds of testimonials prove this, coming from all classes of the community. Pain is no respector of persons, be advised, try Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters,a sovereign remedy for various ailments, for rich and poor alike. When you buy Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, you get what you think you get. absr- lutely the Tonic of Natures own providing, comprised of -the lbeneficient properties contained in Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Guntian, Burdock, Lavender, and Dandelion in ad.lition to Quinine. It is not so much what you pay for a thing, but what you get for what you pay. Don't listen to the "Just as good" argument, there is no Tonic 6' Just as good" as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, it stands supreme. To prevent disappointment, above all things see that you get the right article, with the name "Gwilym Evans "on Label, Stamp, and Bottle, without which none is genuine. Sold everywhere in bottles 23.9 i. and 45.6 d. each, but should any difficulty be ex- perienced in procuring Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, it will be forwarded carriage free, for the above prices, by the Sole Proprietors : The Quinine Bitters Manufac- turing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
        <p>GWILY3I EVANS' QUININE :BITTERS. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. GWILYAI EVANS' QUININE BITTERS.</p>
        <p>white cow lying on the fotpatil bte Aim; from the nose, and its eyes were almost out of the sockets. He asked Clarke why he had beaten the animal. and he said it would not go along. He had walked it from High Lagh, and it would net go any further. Witness took a big stick covered with hair from him. and he also found several pieces of the stick which htd broken off by de- fendant striking the animal.—dr. Fairhurst said he saw blood streaming down its nostrils, and its eyes were al- most out of its head. There was a pool of blood on the ground. Witness told him it was a gross case of cruelty. Be heard that the cow had been on the ground for two hours. Clarke sent for a "knacker's" cart, but the cow got up about eight o'clock and commenced to sway about. Witnws m tdc defendant put the animal in Grappeanall Park until the cart arrived. Clarke admitted that he ought to have had a cart to take it away from the farm. Be told wituess that his mister said it was able to walk to London.—lnspector Greatrex, R.S.P.C.A., said that on the following day the animal wzs slaullaterad and witness made a post-mortem examination of the body. Clarke, who was there, said ha could see what ha had done with the s'iek.—Mr. Whitlow said he had no objec- tion to have the anim l slaughterad on the farm, and he had witaesses to prove that the animal was in a fit condition to travel.—Clarke said he drove the animal for Mr. Shepherd, slaughterer, and not for Mr. Whitlow. —rho magistrates dimaissed the case against Whitlow, the Chairman remarking that he might have exercised a little more consideration in disposing of the cow. The bench consi ered it a Case of gross cruelty. and Clirke would be fined 20s. and costs, or 14 days' imprisonment. They alga expressed the opelioa that the man who employed him was extremely censurable for sanding him for th cow when it was in such a state.</p>
