      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A FAD CASE OF BACKSLIDING. At the Warrington Boroueh Police Court on Saturday before Mr. Birchall (in the chair) and Messrs. Heyward and R. T. Fairclough, a respectable looking young man named Percy Yeomans was chareed with stealing three pairs of ladies' gloves. value 83 3d from the shop of Mr. Joseph Banks, 57, Horsecaarket-street on the Friday night.—The Chief Constable said that prisoner came to Warrington about three weeks ago as an assistant in a draper's shop in the town. He gave way to bad habits and lost his situation. A few nights ago he was picked up in the streets in an unconscious condition and taken to the Infirmary by the police, where he was treated for injuries to the head. He lost his situation as the result. On the Friday evening he went to Mr. Banks's shop and made a most extraordinary offer to work without a ages. A desultory conversation was carried on between him and Mr. Beaks for about an hoer and a half. Mr. Banks found it necessary to go upstairs leaving the prisoner in the shop. When Mr. Benke came downstairs the pri- soner said he thought he would go. As he went out Mr. Ranks noticed that one of the pockets Of his mackintosh seemed bulky, but did not sus- pect anything until he missed the gloves. Information was given to the police and Detective Roper met prisoner ih Dial-street endeavouring to get a pair of gloves on his hand, Another pair of gloves was in his pocket but the third pair was missing. Prisoner informed the police that his father was a commercial traveller at Gloucester for a large brewing firm and he (prisoner) came to Warrington from Cardiff where he bad been for three years. He was discharged there for stopping away from his work.—The Chairman said they were sorry to see a young man like prisonerma such a position. It was a terrible thing to be throwing away his life as he appeared to be doing. He would be sent to gaol for seven days. A 'MELEE Is BUTTERMARKET-STREET.—BEVERM SENTENCE ON A PRISONER. William Griffin, 3, Hill-street, was charged at the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday, with committing wilful damage to the door of the Pelican Inn, Buttermarket-street, to the amount of £2 on Satur- day night, also with being drunk and disorderly at the same time and also with assaulting Thomas Blenkinsop while assisting the police in the execution of their duty. --William Chalmers, licensee of the Pelican Inn, Buttermarket-street, said he put prisoner out about twenty minutes to eleven on Saturday night. After he had done so prisoner rushed at the door and kicked in a very expensive walnut panel, which cost £2. Witness caught hold of prisoner and a struggle took place,during which Sergeant Lewis and Constable Gibbs came up' n the scene. Prisoner butted at Lewis and behaved lik3 a madman.--Thomas Blenkinsop, joiner, Smith-street, corroborated. He was in the street when he saw Lewis and a constable struggling with the prisoner, who was kicking something fearful. He was called upon to assist the officers, and he caught hold of prisoner's legs. Prisoner nicked witness about the body, but he stuck to him until he arrival at the Police Station. The officers were hindered by the crowd.—Sergeant Lewis said prisoner struggled violently all the way to the Police Station. He refuse] to he searched or to give his name, and they had to hold him OR the floor while his boots were taken off.— The prisoner was committed to gaol for five months— two months for the first offence, one for the second of- fence, and two for the third offence —Blenkinsop was thanked for his services, and was awarded ss. by the magistrates from the poor box.—William Birchall, Car- lisle-street, WAS summoned for obstructing the police in connection with the -above case.—After evidence by P.C. Menks,P.S. Lewis,P.C.Corbett,P.C. Newey,and P S.Wil- Bunsen, the Chief Constable said defendant was known to be very respectable. He had had drink on that night. and he thought that accounted for his conduct. He di not wish to press the case at all.—The Chairman said the Bench had deci led to dismiss the case on condition that defendant gave Sc. to the poor box. They did not wish to record a conviction, and hoped it would be a lesson to defendant in the future. It was only by the support of citzens that the police could efficiently do theu duty, and it was for every man to give such assis- tance to the policeas they required. With regard to the officers concerned the magistrates were quite satisfied that there was no undue violence used by them in bring- ing the prisoners to the Police Station. A BRUTAL HUSBAND. At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Tees lay. before Mr. Birchall (in the chair), and Mr. W. Owen, Patrick Burke, of Lythgoe's-lane, was charged with making use of obscene language in Lythgoe's-lane on Monday night, and further with assaulting his wife at a quarter past eleven the same night--Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—P.C. Smith said he was proceeding down Lythgees-lane when he heard someone say "he is a brute to kick a woman like that." Witness saw a number of people amongst whom were prisoner and his wife. Prisoner was making use of filthy language. Witnefie put him in the house. His wife said he had struck her, but prisoner denied it. A young marl said " you did, and I interfered."—Prisoner then went to the young man and got him by the throat but witness pulled him off, and he then made a rush at his wife.— Sergeant Evans said he went to the house later on and the woman complained to him that her huabend got hold of her and pitched her into the street, a distance of four fest, and kicked her. Prisoner made a rush at hi 3 wife and witness arrested him.—William Griffiths, painter, 62 Oliver-street, gave corroborative evidence.—Mary Jane Burke said he came to the house about twenty minutes past eleven. He had had some drink, and pushed her outside the door without a shawl or anything on, and later pushed her to the edge of the parapet where she fell down. She was pregnant. Be hit her on the face with his open hand and made at her with a kick catching her on the teeth, and her mouth bled. William Griffiths interfered and he left her to speak to him. P.C. Smith then came up.—The Chief Ceestable said the prisoner had been before the csort 33 times.—Cramplain- ant : I be of you to let him off this time.—Mr; Birchall: We want to show that men dannot kick their Wi1784 about like footballs. He would have to go to prison for a mouth with hard labour, and 14. days or 103 and costs for the obscene language.—The wife was then granted a seperation order for 3s a week. SHOCKING ILL-TREATMENT OF A COW AT GRAPPENHALL. At the Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday before Mr. J. C. Parr and other magistrates, John Clarke, 15, Old-road, Letchford,was summoned for cruelly ill-treat- ing a cow at Grappenh sll on the 15th November, and Richard Whitlow, Moss Farm, High Legh, was sum- moned for allowing the same to be sn illtreeted.—Clarke pleaded guilty, and Whitlow not guilty.—P.C. Thomas said that at 7 p.m. on the 15th November he was in Appleton-lane-end, Grappenhall, where he saw a red and</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
