      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WiRRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FIIIDAY.—Before the Mayor (In the chair), James Hepherd, Dr. Gornall, Aldermen James Fairclough aid Roberts,</p>
        <p>and Councillor Smethurst. ILL-T lEATMET cff A HEI-Fxß.—Arthur Mills and Harry Burrows were charged. the former with ill-treatment of a heifer by travelling it, and the latter with causing the same oa the 22nd November.—Defendants pleaded guilty.—P.O. Smith said Mills was driving tris maims! on Manchester-road on the day In question. It was lame in one of its fore feet and was suffering terribly—Evidence was called in corrobora- tion and Burrows was tined 211 and costs and Mills 5s aid costs. . . .</p>
        <p>AMEX: FROM WORK.—Mary Ann Trow, 321 Wlnwick- road, and Alice Sutcliffe, 12 Melville-street, apprentices to Messrs Platt, Wilkinson and Compiny were summoned for absenting themselves from work since November 10th and putting the firm to the loss of is.—Mr.A.Browne appeared for the complainants—who brought the case up because it set a bad example.—Defondants pleaded guilty.—The bench made an order for them to return to their work and carry out their Indentures.</p>
        <p>A TRIFLING Claer,—Blizabeth Hares, 92 Howard-street, summoned Sarah Atkins, 62 Howard-street, for assault on November 21st and there was a cross-summons for threats on the same date.—Evi lence was given for both parties and the bench dismissed the ease each to pay their own costs. BREAKLIG A WINDOW. -Alfred Waring, 45 lLail-street,was summoned for committing wilful damage to the window of premises in the occupation of Annie Ladder. Defendant pleaded gull ty.—The bench fined him the costs of the case and the damaged window. SATURDAY.—Before Mr. Thomas Meehan (in the chair), and Messrs. J. W. Hayward and R. T. Fairclough. DRUNBENNESS,—MichaeI Gibbons was fined 3s 6d for being drunk and disordiily on Wilderspool-road on Friday after- noon.—Mary Hulse, described as a professional street singer. was fined 5s and costs for a similar offence in Horsemarket- street the same night. MoNns.Y.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Aldermen Harrison and Bleckhurst. Councillors Shaw Green and Smethurst, and Messrs. Birchall and Hayward. AN ADJOURNED CAsE.—Samuel Melville and Ablgal,l Mel- ville, 274, Wlnwick-road, were summoned for neglecting their three children on the 17th of November and other dates during the last six months.—lnspector McAteer said the husband was not to blame, and he asked for the charge against him to be withdrawn- The female d-fendant was a drunken woman, and the children Were almost starving.— The Chief Constable stated that the woman bed been con- victed before at Belfast for a similar offence.—The magis- trates decided to adjoarn the case for a fortnight, and advised defendant to look after her children. _ _ _</p>
        <p>..... A NEGLECTFUL HUSBAND —iamuel Terry. 37, Scotlan4 road, was summoned for neglecting ,to maintain his wife and ctilldren, leaving them chargeable t 6 the common funds of the Warrington Union.—Mr. B. Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the BO4td of Guardiamuandiabl tha wife and tyro child ren were admitted to the house on the previous Monday. —Ellen Terry, the wife. gave evidence, and prisoner. who had been previously convicted for a similar offense, was sent to goal for three months. .</p>
        <p>_ A Lnni Burmax TRIIII3M—ThomIa Gregson, baker, of Lymm. was fined 10$ and costs (21 3s. 614 for baying ben drunk In charge of a horse and trap in Knutsford-road, on November 22nd. P.C. Coreoran proved the ease. . _ _ .</p>
        <p>.".BANAza."—Parcy Wolfenden, prk buther,lB, St. June'- street; Richard Bretherton, butcher. 4. St. James'-street ; Thomas Wheeler, butsber's assistant. 4, St. Jams'-street; Albert Taylor, trfbe dresser, Wilderspool-road ; James Young, labourer, n, Water-street, were fined 103 and costs eaoh for playing "banker "on the 30th November. in Water-street.— The Chairman said the magistrates were el:terminal to pit, a stop to Sunday gaming. TUESDAY.—Bafore Mr. Thomas Birchall (in the chair), and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Mr. W. Owen.</head>
        <p>Mr. W. Owen.</p>
        <p>OCCASIONAL LICENSE. —Mr. Shillcoek applied for a license to sell at the Conservative Club from Sto 2 ea the oe =don of the Trinity ball.—Grante4. DESERTING —lsaac Wilson was charged with deserting from the 2nd battalion King's Liverpool Regiment. Evidence was given by a Sergeant at Orford aarracks that when on duty at the gate on Monday he saw the prisoner loitering snout and found he was a deserter.—The magistrates remanded the prisoner to await eicort and rec it-amended that the usual re- ward be paid to the Sergeant.</p>
        <p>A CONSICIESTIOI79 OBJECT /B.—Gilbert Lowe. newsagent. 83. Chorley-street, applied for a certificate of exemption from vaccination for his child Bthel. He had a conscientious objection to it.—The bench grantel the appliestion. ,</p>
        <p>WEDNEsDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (in the chair) Mayor (Alderman Wright), and Councillor Smethuret. BevrEvelort o TIME.—Mrs. Kiernan of the Patten Arms, applied for an extension of one hour for the same evening on the occasion of a Scottish dinner. —Granted. ASSAULT ON A 13M3BAND.—liarriet Bate, 26,General-atreet, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on Tuesday night in General street and further with assaulting her bus-, band John Bate at the same tiMe.—John Bate, wiredrawer said at 7.39 on the day ill question he returned home from his work. His wife was fent but came In drunk about 11.33. He asked her if she was not ashamed of hereelf. She abused him and struck lathe with_her_tiet. and then took up a poker and struck at his head but he guarded it. She then caught him by the coat collar and pulled him in the ;yard. His son interfered and she struck him and then went into the entry and used filthy language. It was a freq aent occurrence as she hul been of drunken habits fo; several years.—P.C.s Corbett and Newsy eorroborated.—The magistrates said she would be deed ss. and costs fo.: the first charge, and for the assault she would have to enter .into her own recognisancas ' and find a surety for £5 and pay the coats. The defendant before Itaving tha court signed the Pledge with Mr. Taylor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>DARESBURY PEUTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY PEUTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Venn. J. C. P.re (chairman). W. Long. Knowles, Talbot, and Captain Rodger. STONE THROWING AT STVICTON HELM—Sig youths. named Joseph Horton, Joseph Heesom, John Timperley (14) John Griffiths (14), William Rutter (19), and Arthur Johnson (14), all residing with their parents at Stockton Heath, were summoned. at the instance of P.S. Gosling.. for obstructing the public highway at Stockton Heath on the 13th November, by throwing stones to the danger of passengers,—Derendants pleaded not guilty of throwing stones, and said they were thr ming turnlps.—P.9. Goding said that on Sunday after- noon, th 3 13th November, he saw the defendants in London- road throwing stones, rand. Rai turnips at each other. He watched them for a considerable time, and saw several people run past them to get clear of the missiles. W,tuess had warned thorn several times. It was a constant practice of defendants to throw on a Sunday afternoon.—Hach defend- ant was fined Is. lIISCELLaNgorTB —Martin Graham WAS fined 23 6.1 for not haying alighted lamp attached to his cart at 1.2) o.in on the 14th November. —Edwin Jones, for being drunk in charge of a horse and lurry at Stockton Heath on the 12th N•wember. was fined Ds., and Allen Iluc'cley. carter,of War- rington,was fined 5s for being asleep in charge of a horse and cart at 12-20 a.m. on the 3rd Novembsr.—Joseph Lithorland. a carter, also of Warrington. was similarly summoned for an offence at Seven 04‘413 on the 10th November. Defendant denied oeing asleep, and as there was only one witness present the magistrates gave defendant the benefit of the doubt and dismissed the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>RUNCORN POLICE coußr</head>
        <p>RUNCORN POLICE coußr</p>
        <p>TUFSDAY.—Bee and Samuel Taylor, APPREHENDED FOR HAD LINGIJAGE.—Peter Knight. Mill-street, Runcorn, was brought up on warrant charged with using obscene language In Mill-street, Huncarn, on the 14th November.—He pleaded gullty.—A fine and ousts amounting to 19s was imposed, but the prisoner &amp;aided to go to prison for 7 days, ALLEGED THEFT OF FERRETS.—Thoinas Rhaney respectable looking young man, r:siding at Picking-I'4h% Boats, was brought up on remand from the previous day, charged with stealing two ferrets the property of Mr. James Cooke, farm bailiff at Dutton, on the 14th November.— Prisoner pleaded not guilty—James Cooke said he was a farm bailiff In till employ of Mr. Wede, Aston Bridge. He identified the ferrets which were in the bag produced, as his. He lost saw them safe a fortnight ago in a box in a room in one of Mr. Wade's out buildings, He knew the prisoner, who was a farm labourer who had been working for Mr. Wade. George Chesty, farm labourer, residing at Crawton said be knew something about the ferrets. ithaney tolk them to his house on the Monday night and said to witness's wife that he had bought them. Witness did not see prisoner until 3 days afterwards, and on that occasion Rhaney asked him if be could find a customer for them. Witness replied "that he would sooner he sold them him- self," Afterwards witne3s soil them to Alfred Hyde tor 6s. He had however returned the money to Hyde that morning.—Alfred Hyde, farm labourer, Bratt's Bank, Norley, also gave evidence.—Thomas Hhaney (the prisoner) elected to give evidence,,hiruself and said that on the day in question after work he went home and was in bed that same night at 6 o'clock. Heiknew nothing at all about the ferrets.—The Bench remanded the case, giving Ithaney bail on his own rseognisances until the next Sessions, remarking that they Oonsidered that Gresty's wife was an important witness who should be summoned.</p>
      </div>
