      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>IfIE EXA'MINF.II, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 2, 1898.</head>
        <p>IfIE EXA'MINF.II, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 2, 1898.</p>
        <p>BATLEY T. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>Batley was t e s:ene of this en ragemeat. which the home team won by two tries to one try. Oakland and obtained rho winner's tries, Prole crossing the Line for Bradford. _ 11.31,1PAX V. LEED3 PARISI! 01EIURClit.</p>
        <p>The result of this con`eat at Halifax was a win for the bonoe team by one goal one try to nil. The scoring took place ira the second half, Birch crossing the line, the same player oaverting. _</p>
        <p>BRI.MLEY.</p>
        <p>thither; the ve—ni-le-cTf i-13171--rn--ate-h",-Which was Some- what one-aided, the home fit ien evantually winning by one g3al and bur tries to nil.</p>
        <p>LIVERSEDGE v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>Lceds visitel Liversedge. and returned home the win- nors by tire' vats three tries to one goal one try. Wright. Harrison, and Lumley did the needful for Lecds ; VV4ll4c, aid Tolkill scoring fcr Liversedge. HECKNIONDWIKE v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY. _</p>
        <p>The home tem won this m ttch at Eleckmondwike by one try-IPo lead by Mee in the second half—to nil. after a kien oatest.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" TEM&amp; v. RUNGORN BEMS.</p>
        <p>Tll-040 t;ill.li Diet on the -ifilier—spo—c;i-giound on Satur- day, and were represented as follows :—Warrlngtan : Jones, full back; Harris, E atcliffe, MooreS, and Peadle. hem threequarter-backs ; Reyle, and Reesom, half backs ; Taylor, Deakin, Culning,hain, Andrews, Barrett, Wilcox, Williams, and Knight, forwards. Runcorn Rang: Wriyht, full back ; Wnitley, John Williamson Jones,and Warburton, threequorter backs ; &amp;Mon and Walker, half b mks ; F iulkner, McD mild, Riley, Dixoa,Bradlev, Hughes, and Jane i Williamson, forwards. The visitors kicked off and for some time play settled about tee half way line. Ratcliff I secured and racing LICI the field go: over the line. The scorer klcked for goal but without result. Before long Knight scored a neat try, and Pendlebury improved. After a series of scrimmages Harris got possession and Scored,and Taylor improved. From the kick-off play was immediately taken to the Runcoralquarters, and Harris taking a:pass was tackled on the line b 7 the lees, but grounded the ball. Taylor kicked another goal. Harris, a litt'e later asfain got possession and breaking through the pr'ss evading many attempts to tackle got over at the corner and then finally grounded the ball under the posts. Jones failed at the goal-kick. Runcorn fr the first time invaded the W arringtoolgnar. tars in earnest, the homesters having j to kick dead est. before half time, when the score was Warrington 21 points, to nil. From the re-start the Recs. pressed and there was some close work for a short time the visitors having hard lions. Taylor then sowed, buo the kick for gnat failed, the ball striking the posts above the bar. Play bein.: taken to the visit ere half the Recs. struggled hard to keep the Warington men out but Heesom at last got over. No goal reedited. Toe Runcorn teem I seemed to be going all to pieces now and Warrington made the mot of their opportunities:to 'score. Moons was the next to get over at the corner and Taylor improved. Later Moores passed to Pendle- I bury who male a magnificent run up the field from the half-way with the ball at his feet and was only checked right on the line. Play w eff without much incident until just before time, when Harris got a neat try after somel excitine play in front of the posts. The kick for goa was a hod attempt. Final score : Warrington 4 goals 9 tries (35 points) ; Runcorn Recs. nil.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE (A) TEAMS CEIAMPION3HIP. OLDHLU (A) V. Lamm Oldham, before 4,000 epec'ators. Oldham showed great supsriority. especi- ally in slag three-quarter line, anti in the first half Whittle, Holroyd, and Walsh scored tries, but no goals were placed. After the interval Oldham baffled their opponents repeatedly, aad Whittle, Holroyd, Walsh. Lees, and Pearce scoring tries in the ordsr named. Final :—Oldham 3 goals 9 tries (33 points). Leigh nil. B BOUGHTON RLNGSRS (A) V. Run° RN (A) .-- At Broughton. The Rangers started, and Anderson, with a good run. took Flay to the centre. Here the Rangers were penahied,and Divid kicked a goal for the visitors Soon after Jones snored a try. Half-time o—Runcorn (A) 1 goal,. 1 try (5 points) ; Broughton Rangers (A) nil. Changing !gods the Rangers over-played the visitors. Owen scoring 2 tries in quick succession, and Low's kicking a goal. Another try was snored from a scramble and Middleton also aided a try. Fusel :—Broughten Rangers (A). 1 ROL! 4 tries (4 points) ; Runcorn (A), 1 goal 1 try (5 point.). Wong (4) v. Sr. HICLENT3 M.—This friendly match was played at Widnes, bef ore a poor gats. In the first half the game was very evenly fought, the only score icing la try scored by Davies-, and Foster kicked a. goal. In the second half, Bown, Cullen (2), and Petero scored, end Foster kicked 3 vale. Final :—Widnes (4), 3 goals 5 tries (21 points) ; St. Helens (A) nil. Salford (A). 8 points ; Stockoort (A). nit. Rochdale Hornets (A), 1.0 poi Its ; Tyldesley (A.), nil.-</p>
        <p>LAwcAstuan Jusios Liceetrz. FLETCHER, RIISSELeS GAS ENGINEERS V. LOSTOCE</p>
        <p>GRAL&amp;SI. This match was played on the Engineers' ground on Satarday loaf or 3 500 soecrators. Lostock kicked off,and play was at once taken to the Lostock quarters, where in the first few rainntes W. Evans was almost over. Frem th 3 Acrimmage which ensued the visiting back cleared,but Knight pat in a fine kick, and the Lostask full back mulling J. Berry ran up and scored, J. Antra- bus kicking the goal. Restarting, the game was again taken to the visitorehalf,where a good passing movement by Scott,Raiford,and J.Antrobas,ended in the hitter be- ing floored by Ward. Play now settled do wn, and for a few minute a series of scrimmages took place. The next item was some passing, commenc id by Scott, the ball travellinT across the field to Evans, who passed to Lawless, the latter mooring. Half time score : —Fletcher's Engineers one goat two tries, Lostock Gralarn nil. In the second half, which was played in semi-darkness, Fletcher's had some defensive work Co do,as the Cheshire men played considerably better, and time after time they were almost over. Alter about twenty minutes' play the home team broke away, Scott racing well for the line, and paislng in the n;ck of time to Lawless that</p>
        <p>When asking for Cocoa, insist on hwing LDBURYS.--301d only In Pszkets and Tins as other Cocoas are often substl- uted Icr the sake Of extra prollt,,--Adrt (1)</p>
        <p>player scored. J. Antrobus kicked another goal* Thoroughly roused by the score against them, the Cestriaas played smartly from now to the conclusion of the game, but had not th luck to score. Final score : FletMer, Ituisell's Engineers, two goals, three tries (13 points) ; Lostock Gratam, nil.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF Tr-1E LANGASHIR!,.: SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The committee of the above competition met in Man- chester on tuesday night, and dealt with several ratior important matters. Aspey, of Widnes, and Woodhead, of Salford, were reporte I for wrostling, the first-named being suspended for a month, and Woodhead for one week. Murphy, of Widnes. was reported for using bad language in the match against Swinton, but got off with a caution as to his future conduct. In the latter case Murphy should have appeared two weeks ago, and it was then stated Widnes were summoned in time, but they denied receipt of tie offi3ial notification. The committee passed the following resolution :—" That in the cases ot all players sent off the fiell, such players to appear at the next meeting of the committee, the onus of attendance to lie with the representative of the club." Stockport claimed 2 points for their A team match against Tyldesley A. cancelled owing to the death of Leather, one of the Tyldeiley half backs. The two clubs were ordered to choose another date.—Morecambe were reported for a late start at Broughton on November 19, but their explanation was considered satisfactory, and a fine was not inflicted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>A.SSOCIA.TION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>A.SSOCIA.TION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN v. ADLINGTOL—A great amount of in- terest was centred in this match which was played on the Mesnes ground on Saturday owing to Adlington hav- ing defeated Parr the week previeus in an alliance fix- ture, and when the visltore kiclud off there would be between 600 aad 730 epecators prese.nt. Owing to the late arrival of Adlington the referee ordered' " two forty's" to be played and the home team, winning the toss elected to play again t the hill. The opening ex- changes were mostly in favour of Eeriest iwneand Dicky Lee tested the visitors' custodian in the first minute. The visitors forwards then paid a brief incursion into the home goal but the defence proved equal to the occa- sion, and E irleetown were soon peppering the visitors' custodian again, but he managed to keep his charge intact. Toe homstere forced four corners in quick succession but the were eventually g-t away. A spell of midfield play followed for a time ant then the home forward again took up the ruining and C. Lee scored their first goal 'twenty minutes from the start. Elceuraeed by their I success the .hemesters again returned to the attack and they kept up a hot onslaught on the visitors' goal, Smith 07r:ritually scoring Earlestown's second goal five minutes afterwards. With two goals in a minority the visitors livened op and went down the field with a rush, but the home defence played a capital game, and try as they would t',e Adlingtn forwards could not break through. dal, time score : Earlestowe 2, Adlington 0. On retarii. th homestere once mere gave the visiting deform • as anxious time and good play on the part of Dicky Lee gave Leech posse=sion end steadying himself thatiplayer banged the ball into the net giving the visi- tors' custodian no chance whatever. After this Lanes. town had all their own way, the Adlington forwards never once becoming dangerous. The match was fin- ished in almost total darkness. Score, Earlestown 3, Adlineton 0. NEWTON-LE-WILLIWS v. PEISLEr CuOSS.—The above mach under the auspices of ttio Liverpool and District Combination was played at Peasley Cress on Saturday, before a good corm sure of spectators. Both teams were strongly represented. R. Galloway turning out for Newton'after two seasons' absence. Newton won the toss and the hemesters at once took up the running, and after an exciting scrimmage In front of goal, one of the home forwards put the ball through one minute from the start. Encouraged by their early success the home forwards again came with a rush and eludieg the Newton backs scored their second goal five minutes after. These two early reverses tended to waken the visitors up, and they came very near scor- ing on three occaeions, Frankland skimming the bar with a lovely shot which deserved a better fate. Play ruled even for a time after this bole teams striving hard to score, hut all to no purpose. Half time score :—Peasley Cross 2, Newton 0. The second half opened slightly in favour of Newton but neither custodian was troubled very much, and when the whlstle sounded the score had remained un- changed, viz. : Peasley Cross 2, Newton 0. The pick of the Peasley Cross team were their backs whilst Ashton and Leithwaite played a splendid game for the visitors, but the Newton forwards will have to give a better show than they did on Saturday if they want to win matches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PARR HALL SATURDAY EVENING CONCERT.</head>
        <p>THE PARR HALL SATURDAY EVENING CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The first of the Saturday evening concerts inaugurated ' for the season was given on Saturday in the Parr Hall, Warrington. The attendance was only fair, many of last season's concerts being much better patronised. The opening item, a pianoforte solo, entitled " Valse di Con- cert," was executed with considerable taste be Mr. Alfred Ainsworth. Miss Lottie Herd (soprano), Mi3S Annie Lea (contralto), Mr. W. Wiicis (tenor), and Mr. A. S. Kinnell (bass) appeared in the part soap "Sweet and Low." and‘' Good-night Beloved" and the quartette " A Regular Royal Queen." Their voices blended well together,and the effdct was most pleasing. Miss Annie Lea contributed " When the vice of thy lute," and " Angus Macdonald." Tile singer has a sweet voice and both songs were excellently rendered, but in our opinion the latter suited her voice the best. For his rendering of " Mona" Mr. W. Wilde was encor NI and be responded with "Queen of the Earth," and later he received an encore for "The Irish Emigrant." Signor Bonlvia gave an in- terpretation of Wieniawski's vialin solo " Mazmrka " which was very favourably received. The same artiste also contributed (a) " Gondoliera " (Sgambate) and (b) " Danse Hongroise " (Brahms). There was a marked contrast in the compositions which Signor Bonavia exe- cuted in masterly style. Ia response to the Spontaneous and repeated applause he modestly bowed his acknow- ledgmeutr. Miss Herod was given an excellent reception for her songs " My land of Spain "and "For all eternity." Mr. A. S. Kin nell was en cored for his effort " 'Neath the rlling tile," and complied with "1 fear no foe " which was most effectlvely sung and re- ceived hearty applause. The same singer a lso gave "A border ballad," which was equally well rend ered.With a humorous sketch "A week at Blackpool" Mr. F. Crofts scored a complete success. A burlesque " Sketeb on a popular ac:or," was his next contribution and in res- ponse to an encore he gave " That's how they speak in Morrie Englaud." A humorous son; (with quick chang3), entitled " The Village Grocer " elicited warm applause. Mr. Croft's performance proved him a clever and versatile humorist. Mr. Alfred Ainsworth accom- panied at the piano in a most capable manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
