      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>REP RESS [0 )1 OF FIRE.</head>
        <p>REP RESS [0 )1 OF FIRE.</p>
        <p>It is always pleasant to welcome products in which utility is ad 'reel by beeuty, and it is seldom that these qualities are combined in so high a degree as in the " Salamander " Asbestes decorative panels, etc., manu- factured amengst a variety of ot her things by the United Asbestos C=ony, Limit ed, of 150 and 160, Charins, Cross-road, London. These panels are manu- factured after the most artistic designs, anti have a very pretty effect when used as facings, for walls and coil- tinge, b asides affording pr ab'y the best sef eguard against the spread of any fir o arising in an apartment lined with them. Large as the business of the Cempany is, it is surprising that the use of their or similar wares is not more general. Had the baildiag in which the recent fire in Cripplegate eriganated been lined throughout with this material it is outside the range of probability that it would hive extended to such an alarming extent and woe"d most likely kayo been con- fined te the very room in which the fire arose. That this is no mere conjecture was sufficiently shown in the case of the Burlington Hotel in Dublin where a fierce fire arising in a kitchen was, ac cording to the report of Mr. Farrell, M.R.1.A.1., checked by this material. With object lessons like this and others in the Company's ex- perience before our architects and Insurance Companies it is surely to be expectet that they will insist upon a more geeeral use of absstos at least in the construction of great public headings, hotels, fact ones, theatres, and other structures wherein the haz trd of lite from fire is of even greater importance than the danger to property.</p>
        <p>NATf °NAL A.S r. 3r-e's to John Pat, and Sandy in Qraut's Morelli. Cherry Brandy I" Acknowledged by all to be delicious and comforting and well calcuisted by its soothing properties to make all parties work together for V) e benefit of the nation. sold everywhere.— THOS. GRANT AND SON 3 Maidstone. Makers of Cherry Whisky, Sloe Gin, ice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>AN in m ereasing'y popular far of recreation for the long winter evenings is that provided by Gymnastic Clubs. Such Institution are rapidly multiplying. One of the lea ing manufacturers of Gymnastic Apparatus is the firm. GRIO. G. BUSSEY Co.. Lo..don, Contractors to the War Offioe, Admiralty, India Office, London County Council, &amp;a. Ask the local Sports Balder for G. G. 8..% Co.'s Catalogue. I not procurable, apply at 33 &amp; 38, Qieen Victoria Street' London. Factories—Peckham, L tram; and Blmswell Suffolk.—Advt. (3).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMM rums.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMM rums.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DEFEAT MORECAMBE</p>
        <p>Warrington's fixture on Saturday was with More- cambe, the wooden-spoocists of the League, who the previous Saturday were only defeated by two points less than Warrinzton. The severe drubbing both tams received put them on an eq nifty, and there was some confidence at Morecambe that they would at last win a match. Warrington had again to travel without " Smack " Bata, his younger brother 11 Sete partnering Ashore, behind the scrimmage. Jolley was an absentee frim the forwards, his place being taken by Scholtze. 'The homesters relied on the same team as lest at Broughton. Weather of a delightful description favoured the contest, and there was a crowd numbering about 500 present. Mr. Beswick, of Swinton, the official referee, did not turn up and his duties devolved upon Mr. Gritchley, of Widnes. The teams were male up as fol- lows</p>
        <p>Warrenton : Hallam, full back ; Fish, Duckworth, Cross and Is aerwood, tbreevarters ; Aanurst, and R. Bate, half backs ; Naylor, Swift, Fairhurst, Hill, Board- man, Sudlow, Sch.)ltze, and Eden, forwsrds. Morecambe : Elkin, full back ; Blac ow, H. WiPon, Garner, and H. Hadwen, threequarters ; L Jackson, and Armistead, half backs ; A Wilson, P Wilson, J Bell, F Jackson, R Graham, Edmondson, Gawaalock, and G. Had wen, forwards. Warrington won the toss, and Isherwood elected to play with a strong brerze at his back. PWilson started the game, and a series of exchange kicks between the backs of the two teams ended in Fish being tackled and thrown in touch. The homesters got a footing in War- rington territory and showed they meant business, a clever bout of passing between Jackson, Wilson, and Hadwen causing Warrington to fall back. Bate lost a chance of relieving by holding the ball too lone, but presently punted to centre where Elkin les'. groend with a poor kick. An off-side tackle of Isherwood enabled the latter to give Warrington the upper hand with a well-judged centre. The same player charged Elkin's kick down and had a clear courae for the Line when the whistle brought him to a standitill. It was now Warrington's turn to show that they were not ring to be beaten if they could help it, and the for- wards racing up field upset the home custodian on the line. Then followed a elose siege of tee Morecambe goal. A wide pass from the scrimmage to Fish should have resulted in a try but the ball travelled along the gronnd too slowly. Bob Bat 3 attempted to dodge over but was well tackled by Jalkaon. Aided by the wind Warrington kept the homesters closely penned, an a foolish kick by E'kin gave Hallam possession at the quarter-line, and the Warrington full-back coolly put his side ahead with a neat drop. , Morecambe appeared rather upset by this reverse and allowed Warrington to maintain the attack.. A clever dribble by Cross and Duckworth de.erved a try, but L. Jackson pluckily dropped on the ball and saved. H. Had wen played up prominently at this stage and by a good dash reached centre, Fish eventually terminating his career. The visitors were soon at the other end again, and a miskick by Garner eave Sudlow a lovely chance, but though be secured the ball he failed to take the stride or two which would have carried him over the line. Still at- tacking Warrington lost another chance of a try through Duckworth, who passed right over Fish's head at thn corner. Isherw wed made a fine ran bat was upset by Blacow whin in the act of banding to Cross. Neat passing by the Warrington backs at last gave Fish a chance and the left winger got in at the corner bat hav- ing had an interview with the touch flag en route no try was allowed. Morecambe were having far the worse of the gem o, and though the score was long delayed it seemed inevitable, L. Jackson laid Hallam out foully and had to retire, Ashurst avenging the deed a moment I later by claarging a kick down and scoring far out. Hallam used his sound leg be good effect, kicking a grand goal from' touch. Half-tineo arrived with War- rington leading by seven points to nil. Naylor opened the second moiety, and Warrington were the aggressore, Fish running speedily down the touch-line before passing to Sullow, who made no head- way. Morecambe get back to centre through a mistake by Hallam, bat made only a brief stay before Isherwood drove thorn back. Duckworth, Ashurr• and Fish ex- changed aid a score was narrowly averted. On the opposite side Isherwood capped a promising movement with a sprint which was almost swocessful. Gowanlock relieved reith a spanking run into the Warrington half, and being tackled be passed well--to Cross, who received with thanks. Hereabouts Armistead sustained injuries te the face and had to leave his vile with only 13 men. Though deprived of bath half backs the hornesters played up with determination and made a better show. H. Elad- wee was only up3et a yard from the line after a spirited dash. Then Fish was injured, but resumed play. The , game deteriorated a lot, but was fiercely contested. Morecambe attacked vigorously. Warrington defended with equal energy but could not clear before H.Eledwen I had drooped a fine goal for Mor mamba. P. Wilsoe se- curing at the Werring,ton quarter forced his way over after being tackled. No try was allowed and the home crowd began to murmur ominously. Seeing his side do- ing well, Armistead returned, but had not been pliyin: many minutes blore he was caught having a scrimmage with AshurSt, and both men were ordered off the field. Fairhurst took Ashurst'e place behind the pack. By the aid of some clever marks and good punting Warrington at last reached the Mereciambe line, and Fish made several attempts to get in. Thll3 arrived with the victory resting with Warrington, whose snore Was 2 goals, 1 try (7 points). Morecambe, 1 goal (2 points),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ST. HELENS v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, before 4.000 spectators. Beth clubs were strongly repre3ented, though S. Helens lacked the cervices of Traynor, and Widnes were short of Nee itt, Murphy and Handley. The home team played with the ground and wind in their favour during the first half, and ha] the best of the openiag xc hinges, and pressed hard, but Martin relieved with a good run to the Saints quarters. Foulkes eventually cleared. Play in centre øf the field followed, after which Batey failed to score from a penalty in front ef the St. Helens' goal. The St. Helens forwards dribbled to the Widnes line, where Sid- dall and Liversedge, by over anxiety, missed scoring. Play was very fast and exciting, and there were numer- ous fouls. Martin saved a d mgarous rush by the home forwards, but on the whole neither aide had any great advantage during the first half, although just before change of ends Siddall almost got through for St. Helens being collared on the line. Half-time :—No score. On the game being resumed, Widnes had the best of ,n attars for a rinse. Barnes, with a good run, transferred opera- tions to the Widnes' quarters, and passing by the last- named, Briers. and D3herty., got the ball close to the visitors' line, Plurnpton stopping the latter. Play Was subsequently confined to the centre of the firld, and the game was very evenly contested. rhe Widoes'efor wards mile a good rush to the St. Helai,os, but were quickly driven back. The visitors were fifirrded a penalty kick in a good positien, but Batey failed to score. Widnes had the best of the play at this period, but the home de- fend.srs were in grand form. The 'Sales forwards dribbled to the Widnes 25, and scrimmage on the visi- tors' line were very exciting, bet SC. Helens could not score. Play was even up to the c'ose, the defence on each side being perfect. Final :—St. Helens, nil; Widnes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RUNCORN v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>Irwell-lane was the scene of an exci tin contest be. tween the absve alizba on Saturday last, and a conten that displayed fully the fact that ia Runcorn there is still !Oat? Of tho old talent remaining which in years gone by made the name .of the; Runcorn team highly and fearfully respected. The result was hard lines per- sonified—Runcorn did the playing, but the Rangers got the score. When the teams lined up there was a good crowd present atiout 3,000 and the issue was eagerly , looked forward to. Both MUM appeared in time And took the field under the generalship of Mr. J. Smith, Widnes, as follows ; Runcorn Back, Forster; three-quarters, Warder, Wilkinson, Bowen, C Butterworth ; hilvep, Williams, Nickson ; for Rards, Taylor, Walker, Lightfoot, G tyter, Farmer, Darlington. Hughes, Tomlinson. Broughton Rangers : Back, Hacking • thresquarters, Lewis, Barratt, Chapman, Jackson; halves, Messenger, Nelson; forwards, Thomson, Lazsnby, Mumberson, Lamb, Brown, Deacon, Whitehead, and Stickney. Hostilities opened tame, the kick off being but feebly remolded to. Then there was a sullen change. Afte; a rapid piece of passing Lewis got possession and sailed over the Runcorn line scoring a try that fairly came as a surprise,. It was a surprise to both tam; as well as the spectators. CPapman took the kick but failed. From the restart Runcorn forwards got going, S. Walker lead- ling the way, and the rest b inking him up to the hilt. Time after time did they storm the citadel of the Rangers in the bores of getting over that tantalis- ing " white wash ;" only however to be sat upon a few Inches off. It was superb forward play and had the Runcorn threequarter line been anything like as smart as the Rangers' lot they would have had as many tries as they liked. Firmer had a drop. A. pcinalty against Runcorn then gave relief, but the fine defence of young Forster kept them at bay. Broughton tried another passing bout, which failed to take effect owing to forward passing. Nickson was prominent with an excellent bit of work, but he should remember he has not the weizht to break away. Exciting times followed</p>
        <p>and the Runcorn forwards were again seen at their best. Ma gnificient batli play on the Rangers' part,how- ever, averted all danger. Bowen and Williamson got in some good work. Forster came away with a fine kiek and f ellowed up but Runcorn had to be satisfied with hard lines. Jackson and Chapman saved. Deacon failed to get away. Runcorn forwards then tried Come dribbling and a lot of ground was gained, Broughton being so hardoressed as t 3 be compelled to kick dead. Jackson 40t away beautifully but Warder grassed him. Fr om the scram Messenger got possession and burst over. Nelsen co everted. Chapman made a mark shortly after, and Lewis only just lost an opening through missing his catch. Forster was plying brilliantly when half-time arrived :—Broughton Rangers, 1 goal, two tries ; Run- corn, nil. Runcorn restarted by kicking in touch. Then came a passing movement executed by Barrett, Chapman, and Jackson. Jackson retained final possession and raved for the Runcorn line, passed all but Forster, and that player tackled low and effected a brilliant save. Nelson made a mark and Chapmen kicked for goal without result. Forster was comp lied to kick in,o touch. Following up this advantage, Jackson got clear and fairly stepp3,l over Forster, secnring a try in good posi- tion which Nelsm converted. Runoorn got well up with a dribble, and Broughton Rangers were so hard put t that two of their for wards (Br own and Lamb) kicked over to save. Instead of a 5 yards' scrum, however, the referee awarded the Rangers a kick out. N with- standing this, Runcorn Came again, Walker, Darling- ten, and Lightfoot being conspicuous. Hacking kicked dead. Br eughton Rangers relieved the pressure by some good foot work and Messenger got , dangerous once or twieli. Offside against the Rangers I gave Runcorn a fires opening. With an exciting rusts Walker burst through the backs and registered a famous try. Farmer made no m stake and secured the major pomt. The event was loudly cheered. Following this there was a period of intense excitement in the visitors 25, during which Ward me punted over and mace or twice the ball had to be touched do wn or kicked over the dead line to prevent a score. WardertakTei wetland brought up Lewis in fine style. Forster played a brilliant game, and was noticeab`e for good cra•ch ng and tackling. lle also followed up speedily and ween the whistle blew time he was just putting in one of the best runs of the match. Toe final was : Broughtoi Rangers, 2 goals 3 tries, (13 poin's) ; Runcorn, 1 goal 1 try, (5 points).</p>
        <p>SWINTON .v.-WIG&amp;N.</p>
        <p>Beth teams were strongly represented at Swinton, and a close game resulted in the home players' favour by one goal to nil. J. V dentine scoring a penalty goal.</p>
        <p>RoORDALE HORNECS v. TY6DE3LEY.'</p>
        <p>A drawn game wai the result of this 'witch at Roch- dale. Francs scontd a try, which he improved upon for Tyldeeley, whilst Rig kicked a penalty pal and obtained a try for the H3rnets. LEIGEi V. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>Leigh, playing--;C—hohl.e,— —d—ilr—et—s4 Oldham by two goals and one try to one try. MAynetis arid Duabavin dropped g)a!s for ths winters, Davies adding a try, whilst Oldham's noinl wa ainod br Williams. STOCKPORT v. &amp;ILFORD.</p>
        <p>This match, at Stockport, resulted in a win for the visitors by two goals and fir tries to BP7O goals.Saville, Sharp; Griffiti, and Paarson played prdminently and ontritou:ed p Ants.</p>
        <p>YORKSHIRE S ENE° li:CONIPETITIOX.</p>
        <p>CISTLEFORD v. HOLgEOIi</p>
        <p>Castleford s-v;r; if :1;;;;b Cwo goals and one try to two goals. S lin Duke, mad Dunn, .played well tor ttssi r rmscpictive side'. _ HUN3LEr v. HLTDDEEtiFIELD.</p>
        <p>This fixture was dm,ded at Ht.uslet. Th 3 game was fairly even throughout and it was close oa " time " when Lorriniau scored f,r Huddersfield, who won by one galt nil. __MINNINGHASI v. BRIGHOUSE RINGERS. _</p>
        <p>Manoin;hatn, on their owa ground, accounted for Brighouie t the ext,-nt of two goals two tries to nil. Rhodei, Bentley, and Niedbans contributed to the win- score.</p>
