      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>JAMES W. C. JATER.</head>
        <p>JAMES W. C. JATER.</p>
        <p>There are many reasons why Scott's E-nulsion is 131 effectual in coughs, colds, and all affections of the throat and lungs. After inserting a letter from a gentleman who was very weak from an acute attack of tonsilitis, we will tell volt why Sot's Emulsion has so many uses. Mr. Jater's letter is as follows :</p>
        <p>" Montrose Lodge, "Truro Road, Wood Green, N., " June Bth, 1898</p>
        <p>" Gentleman,—! had a very severe attack ef ton- silitis, and was just on the verge of diphtheria. I could not walk for days, owing to thP less of strength. Being advised to take Scott's Emulsion, I did so, and after three bottles I found I had gained my lost strength and energy, and now I an pleased to say lam quite well again. I was very pleased with the taste of Scott's Emulsion, and think it very palatable. I think your valuable preparation cannot be too highly recommended.—Yours very truly,</p>
        <p>" (Signed) JAMES W. C. JATER."</p>
        <p>:Sr" IT WILL PAY</p>
        <p>ISMUMWEIRMENi6SZS.Rg.,..-Ve.,:f</p>
        <p>aer</p>
        <p>2</p>
        <p>Send for Name of nearest Agent.</p>
        <p>Manufacturer—CHAßLES HAM LIN, 38a, High St., Salisbury.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. RAPHAEL TI7CIK AND SONS' CHRISTMAS CA.RLS, ART NOVELTIES, ETC.</p>
        <p>" Tempus Fugit !" is the motto with which Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons' come before the public this Yuletide. " Tenapus Fugit ! and how rapid its flight I" Well may they remind us that time flies. It seems but a short interval since we were last engrossed in the light and pleasing labour of reviewing the selection of art novelties re- ceived from Messrs. Tuck, and yet a year has flown with all its multitudinous experiences, joys, and sorrows. And now a new season dawns upon us— a new century almost—bringing with it new copes and possibilities. We know of no firm which more fully appreciates the fact that time flies than Messrs. Tuck. They are always early in the field, and they invariably take the place of horaour among high-class pub- balers of Christmas and New Year cards, calendars, booklets, and artistic novelties of nearly every description. It is like gilding refined gold to accord praise year by year to the celebrated firm whose productions are eagerly sought for the wide world o'er. Immense variety, wide rang-,and charming originality have always been characteris- tic of Messrs. Tuck's productions, and never more so than on the present occasion. We could not possibly give an adequate impression of their thou- , sand and one excellencies in this brief notice, but I we are glad to be able to afford our readers some idea of what they have to choose from in Messrs. Tuck's collection. There is seldom any prominent departure from established style in these Christmas dainties, but there is always some new idea of em- bellishment embodied in the majority of the more up-to-date cards. Last year there was a great run on black and white etchings, platinotypes, etc., many of which were exquisitely beautiful. This year, in Messrs. Tuck's cards, we notice a partiality for marble columns, and stately Grecian architecture as a basis of adorn- ment. Exceedingly handsome too are these replicas of the dignified columns and porticoes which abounded in ancient Greece, and the remains of which are now admired with perhaps more intensity than in the day of their formation. This style of card ought to win favour as it is produced in the most approved tones and colouring. There are also a number of platino panels and "black and whites" included in the immense assortment of artistic productions. In our opinion they are not Ito be excelled for simple beauty/ and taste by any. thing of the kind either new or old. Among other specialities in fancy cards are Duo-Chrome, Royal Porcelain, Japanesque, and Geisha series, the latter being particularly smart and attractive. Messrs. Tuck, with reason, point proudly to their greatly augmented collection of comic cards. The novelties of this type are far ahead of anything previously tarned out by the firm, and apart from their im- mense variety they are imbued with real wit and humour both in design and wording. Nothing of a vulgar nature can be attributed to any of them, and for provoking the merriment of the recipient some of these cleverly designed cards would be hard to beat. For calendars and toy books Messrs. Tuck possess an unapproachable reputation, to up- hold which nothing has been left undone in the production of this class of Christmas gilt. Father</p>
        <p>Scott's anubion contains cod-liver oi and the hypopholethites of lime and soda, blended Into a perfect and palatable emulsion that does not tax digestion. Cod-liver oil and glycerine are both specifics for affoctins of the throat and lungs, and the glycerine is also of great anvantage because it assists in a quick absorption of the oil ,into the blood ; hsnce, by making this combination, we not only soothe the inflamed membranes, but we also enrich the blood with properties which give strength and vitality to the whole system. The hypophosphites aid digestion, feed the nerves, and assist in the general building up of the body. Scott's Emulsion is approved of highly by the medical profession, physicians declaring that it is the best form of cod-liver oil in the world. All chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
        <p>You may ohtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by sending threepence to cover postage to Scott &amp; Bowne, Ltd., Manufacturing Chemists, 95, Great Saffron Hill, London, E.C., and menttoning this paper,</p>
