      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>PARISH CHURCH BAZAAR.</head>
        <p>THE PARISH CHURCH BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The lett,r over the signature, " A Subscriber," which appeared in "The Examiner" of the 26:h Nov. was rather ill-timed, and reminded one dt the proverbial folly of shutting the stable door when the have hal gone. This "great admirer of the Rector in all his pub- lic work " should have entered his protest against tee sale of drink at the bazaar before the license was granted and not after the bazaar was over. But why protest at all ? If beer, whisky, and other intoxicants " art good creatures of God," as some men say they are,why should they be excluded fum the bazaar, or even from the Church itself. We know that these liquors are commended and legalised by the " gentlemen of England" in Parliament asseu.bled, that they enjoy the distinguished patronage of Royalty, and contribute largely to the help of the church at election times. And, moreover, it is well known that the Rector of Warring- ton is not asham,A, now and then to acknowledge his gratitude to the brewers for assisting him threu.eh their purses in the work of the church. But all this does not alter the character of the drinks themselves. They are male and sold for the express purpose of producing in- toxication, and the crime and destitution which follow their use is beyond description. If church people cannot or will not support their various schemes with- out bazaars, raffles and other shady dr vice,, no one, not even "subscriber," would object to the use of legitim- ate articles, such as food, clothing, and wenderful quilts as all these things are good and useful in themselves. But the sale of intoxicants for religious purposes is nothing short of a gross scandal,and tends to lower one's opinion of the kind of religion which can accept assis- tance from such a source. Yours truly, lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>LECTURE AT WARRINGTON ON CROMWELL.</head>
        <p>LECTURE AT WARRINGTON ON CROMWELL.</p>
        <p>The fifth of a course of six lectures on the history Of the English people from 1514 to 1689 was de- I livered in the Co-operative hall, Sankey-street: Warrington, on Wednesday evening, by Mr. W. G. Deßurgh. There was again a large attendance. The subject of the lecture was "Cromwell." At the outset the lecturer said in order to give the frame- work to the picture of the Civil war in England he would refer to the three clearly marked periods in- to which it fell. In the first place there was the period from the outbreak of the first civil war in August 1642 to the battle of Naseby and the sur- render of Charles I in 1615. The se.cond period com- prised a break in the hostilities and a period of negotiation lasting from 1645 to the middle of 1648. During this period the Parliamentary party on the One hand, and the army on the other, each representing distinct and antagonis- tic principles the Parliamentary party re- presenting rigid Presbyterianism and the srm7 cham- pioning a wider and mere tolerant Independency—these two sections bid against each other for the 000 session of the king. The third period was from 1648 to 1649 in which they had the army triumphing over the Parlie- =notary party at the battle of Preeten,and the trial and execution of the king. The lecturer proceeded to show that from the outbreak of the Civil War the Puritan re- volution centred in Cromwell. He therefore intended, instead of giving a mere historical summary to take Cromwell's life history and study the story of the Civil war and the years which followed in connection with Cromwell's personality and char actor (Applause.) Cromwell who was born in 1599 was a country gentle- r&amp; n of the upper class, and was connected in ancestry with the famous Thomas Cromwell who in many ways made his mark in the sooi d life of the England of his ' day. Cromwell did not spring from the masses of the people as we in the present day underetoed hat phrase. The lecturer gave a brief sketch of the early history and Parliamentary life of Cromwell top to 1642,and remarked that Cromwell's history prior to the Civil war was not of first rate importance, and he cut no promiuent figure in Parliament up to that time. It was not until August 1642 at the outbreak ef the Civil War that Cromwell became a leading pare sonality. The Civil war, the lecturer pointed out WA a two-fold struggle —Parliament against the King , and Indepeudency against Presbyterianism. The first resu'ts of the Civil war ended in victories for the Rovalists, aid these defeats led Cromwell to form hie corps of Ironsides which became the finest corps of cavalry in Europe at that time. Cromwell's victories were cavalry victories. The organisation of the Eastern Counties Association by Cromwell wee explained, as well as the earrying out of what was called the new model army. Proceeding to refer to the period of negotiations after the victories of Marston Moor and Naseby when the king had surren- dered himself to the Scots,the lecturer stateci that Crom- well was anxious to get the king into his hands. In order to do so he offered Charles far more liberal condi- tions than the Presbyterians. He offered Charles the army, the restitution of the Royal power, amnesty to all Royalists on c ondition that Cromwell and Cromwell's army should be at the right hand ; that Parliament should be summoned every two years, a reform of the law and other salutary reforms. There was to be a very fair measure of tolera- tion in matters of religion, not merely to the Puritans but to the Episcopal form of church government. It Was Charles's fatal blunder that he never closed thwi the offer of Cromwell.. Had he done so it would have been all over with the Parliamentary party. Char!ee, however, played with both parties, and was not given I to that straightforwardness which the English liked. The second outbreak of the Civil war resulting in the triumph of Cromwell, the execution of the King and sub- sequent overthrow of Parliament were touched upon. The lecturer then proceeded to give a brief lucid history of the Commonwealth. Referring to the war in Ireland the lecturer said that was not by any means the brightest spot in the government of England by Cromwell. But it Meowed that Crania ell was determined to be thorough. E very act of Cromwell, the lecturer contended, was dcne sine qely. (Hear, bear). They might disapprove of what a man like Cromwell or Bismarck did, but they most he sincere ha their work or they would fall immediately into the second racks. The great object of Cromwell was to find what e called " a settlement of these nations." The various aspects of Cromwell's government including has foreign policy,his championing of religious toleration and his four political exper mrnts te fled a settlement for the government of the country were described. A pathetic allusion was made to Cromwell's death in 1658, and the lecturer concluded by a generous tribute to the genius of Cromwell. Councillor PIIONICS said the committee were endeavour- ing to arrange for another course of lectures, and at the fleet lecture which was the last of the present series, an xpremien of opinion would be taken as to what course e ef lectures should be adopted.</p>
        <p>SKINS ON FIRE! SKINS ON FIRE! With tortur. • ing,, disfiguring eczemas, and every species of honing - scaly, and blotchy skin and scalp humours are instantly relieved by a warm bath with CIITICURA SOAP, a ehame anointing with CUTICVEA (oinrment), the ssreat shin cure, and a full dose of CITTICCEA RESOLVENT. grnteat of blood purifiers and humour cure], When all else fails. To th^se who have suffered long and hopelessly,andho have lost faith in doctors, modicines, and all things human, Cu TICURA. remedies appeal rvi:h startling force. i They are, n truth, the greatest skin cures, blood puri- fiers, and humour remedies of modern times.</p>
        <p>'• LINSEED COMPOUND "Trade Mark of Compound &amp;Boum, of Linsead, for Coughs and Colds —Advt. ASK. or CUNNINGHAM S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt "LINSEED COMPOUND" for Coughs and Colds, allays irritation. Uives immediate relief .—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>_ -</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>T lON MATT E RS.</p>
        <p>lettets to the C-Zittor.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Whatever has ben the matter with the gas this last week? The light has beet abominable anl the Stench ten times worse. It is quite time somebody had an increase of saiary.—D;ubtless our gas mina,ger will think it yrntle hi i while to give the ratt!Tayers an ex- planation, Yours,</p>
        <p>A SUFFERER.</p>
        <p>A FEW QUERIES CONCERYING CORPORA-</p>
        <p>Sir,—ls it true that on Monday week ort the motion of Alderman Sutton, the Property Purchasing Committee of the Corporation, which has done such valuable work duaing the past two year; and has saved the ra:epayers =any thousands of pounds was abolished? Is it also a hot that th 3 Street Improvement Com- nalttee, of which Alderman Sutton is chairman, claim to be authorise" to do this important work in future? If these quanta are correct thee something should be done at once to prevent the foss to the town of the ser- vices in this direction of Aldermen Fairclough and ltoberts, and to Councillor Saw Green' to whose fore- Sight, ability, and pugnanity a good deal of the success of the old Committee is due. Why is Alderman Sutton so ;anxious to keep these men from being associated with this important work?— Tours etc.,</p>
        <p>A RATEPAYER.</p>
        <p>DRIPPINGS FROSI RAILWAY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have frequently bee 1 annoyed by the leaka les from the railway bridges which unfortunately run over our main streets, so much so teat I have several times called the attention of our town councillors (?) to the matter, but as usual, without result. I have boen a frequent sufferer by tho oily droppings from the bridges on rainy days, and it was only last week that three ladies were passing in aa open carriage under the one in Town Hall Ward, and their dresses ware completely Spoiled. My opinion is that we should enter an action for damages in the first instance against the Corporation (Street Improvement Committtea), conjointly with one against the Railway I,,ompanies. After this protest, to say the least, I trust with your assistance, and exposure, the nuisance will be at once rectified—Yours truly,_</p>
        <p>NEW BONNET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
