      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Tuesday marning before Mr. C. B. F. Barron, Aichael Haffure and Joseph Ravenscraft, a travelling shawman, were charged with being drank in Earlestown, and were each fined 2s 61 and casts. EARLEBTOWN PRIZE BAND</p>
        <p>A social evening in connection with this band was neld ii the Mission Hall on Saturday evening. There was a very large attendance,and dancing was indulged in until nearly eleven o'clock. The ar- rangements were well carried out and everything passed off satisfactorily. THE NEW POLICE BUILDINGS AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The new buddings in course of erection at New- ton. in connection with the County Constabulary, are proceeding very slowly, and from the appear- ance of the place, some considerable time will elapse before they are ready for occupation. The buildings consist of a sergeant's house, three cells and a court room, and when completed will be an ornament to an otherwise ugly, vacant space.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the above society was held in the Co-operative Hall on Sunday after- noon. Mr. Thompson was in the chair. The vocal- ists were the Misses Holdsworth, and Miss R. War- burton was the reciter. The speaker was MrA. Holdsworth, who gave a capital address on" A young man's difficulties with the Bible." _ _ .</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF ORGANIST AT ST. JOHN'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The position of organist at St. John's Church, Earlestown rendered vacant by the retirement of Mr. H. W. Radford, has been filled by the appoint- ment of Mr. James Harrison ot Wargrave-road, Earlestown. Mr. Harrison is a capable musician, and at present is doing duty at St. Peter's Church, Newton, for Mr. P. Pairclough, who is ill. He will take up his duties'at St. John's on the llth</p>
        <p>NEWTON' CoNGREGATIONAL LITERARY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>At the usual weekly meeting of this society, held in the Congregational Schoolroom, Newton, on Monday evening, the Rev. J. Allett presided and there was a good atteneance of members. A paper was read by Mr. T. E. Clough on " G. R. Sims ;an appreciation." The paper was of an interesting character and proved to be are view of the writings and characteristic: of the subject of the essay. A discussion on the variou3 paints br3ught out, followed, and an interesting and instructive gather- ing resulted. Votes of thanks were accotded the essayist at the close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>TB T NEW VICAE OF NEWTON.</head>
        <p>TB T NEW VICAE OF NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. James Ryder, who has been appainted to the vacant position of V:car of St. Peter's, New- ton, has vi,ited Newton, and decided to accept the appointment. It is not yet known when he will be finally installed, but it ie expected this will take place in the course of the next week or two. It is important that no undue delay should take place, as there are various matters in connection with the church which neel imm34iate attention. SALE OF WORK AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>In connection with tke Congregational Branch of the Young People's Society of Christian Endeavour, a sale of werk, in aid of liquidating the debt re- maining on the school restoration fund was held in the Congregational schoolroom, Isiewton,on Tuesday evening. There were three stalls erected in the room, that for the sale of useful and fancy articles being presided over by Miss Allatt assisted by the Misses Lily Dadgeon and Maggie Pearson. Toe fruit and flower stall was under the supervision of the Misses Jessie Allett, Lottie Browni es and Amy Bethel!. The refreshment stall was in charge of Miss Hall and Miss Jessie Howard. In addition to those mentioned there were several of the juniors in characteristic dress moving about the room in the endeavow• to dispose of sun lry articles. They were, Annie Forster Milian girl), Grace Bethell (Red Riding Hood). Allan Owen (Little By Blue), W Brown (the muffin man), Harold Turner (Jack Tar). During the evening musical selections were given at intervals by members of the Junior Society. There was a very fair attendance of the public, and a satisfactory business was done. EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>In connection with the re-opening of the above society, which for a considerable period has been disbanded, Pn enttTtainment was given in the Wes- leyan schoolroom on Saturday evening. The Rev. P. R. Pasomore presided, and he was supported on the platform by Mr. W. Skelton of Glasop, Messre. T. Parr and J. Anders (secretary of the Band of Hope). Addresies on temoeraece matters were given by the C lair:nail and Mr. Skelton, bath of whom gave words of encouragement for the future conducting ot the Band of Hope.An excellent enter- tainment was giyon. and the various items were very efficiently rendered aril enthuiiaitically re- ceived. The following was the proy,ramme :—Song, " Remember now thy Creator," Mr Twemlow; hu- morous quartette " Spreauing the news," Messrs Twemlow and Webster and Misses Barr and Clarke; recital " Why," Mrs Pearson ; song " By they Foun- tain," MiiB M Barr : recitation " Christmas'Day at the Workhouse," Mr Webster ; duet "In the dusk of the Twilight," Misses Barr and Clarke ; song " Al- ways look on the sunny side," Mr Twemlow; recital "Me and Jim," Mr Twemlow; song " Zayder Zee," Mr B Clarke ; duet "The spider and the fly," Mr Webster and Miss Barr; recital " The coward," Mr Webster ; quartette " The saint and the snakes,' Messrs Webster, Twemlow, Misses Barr and Clarke humorous quartette " Betteetis.to laugh than to cry." Miss Greenall presided at the piano. E AnLRSTOWN WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Sunday vvas set aside HS a special day for the preaching of sermons on Temperance in the above ' chapel. The Rev. J. H. Dodrell of Prescot occueied the pulpit, and his remarks were altogetherdirected against the indulgence in alchololic liquors and for the encouragement of temperance. In the Sunday School, in the afternoon, instead of the ordinary routine, a temperance meeting was held. Mr. W. Wheatley addressed the juniors, and read a paper on" Father Mathew" the great temperance reformer, which was of an interesting na'ure. In the senior school Mr. J. H. Profit presi led, an-1 the Rev. J. H. Dodrell was also present. Mr. prnat gave a very interesting address, and the Rev. J. H. Dolrell,also dealt with the drink question. De sail the great curses which they had to overcome in this country were drinking and gambling. He was going to say smoking, but as no doubt there were many present who smoked, he would not do so, especially as he was a slight smoker himself. Mr. Dodrell after a hymn had been sung,referred aeain to the question of smoking, stating that he understood somebody to say that drinkingegarnbling and smoking were on a level.—Mr. Graham : "I said they wee related," and added that he thought Mr. Dedrell could not contradict that.—Mr. Dodrell said he would venture to say that there was no connection between smok- ing, and drinking and gambling. But although he smoked, himself, his expenditure in this direction was most trifline. Still his advice to young men Was "do not smoke until you are 21 years of age,and after that do not smoke at all." (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Pearson added hit testimony in support of the harmful and expensive habit of smoking.—Mr. Graham wished to reply to Mr Dedrall's statement, but the Chairman said it was no place fcr a dis- cussion of that sort,—We understand the discus- sion was continued after the meeting and was of a rather heated character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A GARSTON MAN KILLED AT Sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>James Seddon, aged f:irty, a stearri-ciaTt-e-d-rIV-er, who resided at 16, Wainwr!ght-grove, Garston died at the Providence ilospital, St. liohans, at half-past one on Saturday morning. On Thursday last he was called to St. Flelens to take charg:i of ~he steam crane in the goods yard, which runs on a gantry over thirty friet fr )rn the ground. At twenty min Ites past four on Friday after- noon, Walter Owen, the yard toreman,an i a man named Benebury, who were working under the crane, heard decewed fall to the ground. When picked up it was found that his left arm was hrotten,saveral ribs fr tc!u red and his head was badly injured. He was taken to the Providence Hospital, where he died. It is not known how Seddon canna t.) fall, as he was alone in charge of the crane. He had on many previous ocCiiions worked this particular crane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Oa Monciailii:..i..-i.--,IOr-ie-s-;cfeiiu-t-y-c-ounty coroner, held an inquest at St. Fie lens on the body of William Maurice James Bwinaerton,a bottleinaker, aged twenty- two, who resided at 68, Lowe-street, who was found with his head cut off on the main line of the London and NorthrWe3 ern Railway Company near St. Helens Station on Wednesday morning last. William Latham, 57, Hall-street, said he WAS foreman plate'ayer for the railway company, and deposed to finding the body at half-past five on Wednesday morning. It was lying by the side cf the main bras to Liverpool, with the head cut off. The head was between the rails, and quite separ- ated from the body. If the deceased had been caught accidentally he oil not think it wool I hays been found in that position.—The jury r,?turned a verdict of " but said there was not sufficient evidenca to show what state of mind deceased was in at the time.</p>
        <p>" LINSEED COM.POUND " For Coughs and Colds. Asthma, and Bronchitis, Of Chemists only,—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 2 1698.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 2 1698.</p>
        <p>influence tor cue party which they believed Was endeavouring to improve the conditions of the people net only in this country but over a vast territory which they were called upon to rule. (Applause). They had a gre it responsibility resting on their shoulders, because 61-ley c3nli never say they ware unaware of what wee going on throlghout the world, they could never plead ignorance of the wrongs and needs which fez:lei:al, and if women recognised that they were a great force in influencing public opinion between One election and another, and by the work they could do at elections, they would do something to help the one party with which they aereel. Dealing with the second part of her subject as to why women should be Liberals, the Countess said the Liberal party had endeevoured in tee pest an I was endeavouring to-day to work for the welfare of the whole nation and not for one class or section or interest. Sae could not sze one p elitice! question with regard to which the interests of women were not se important as men. Tnis W 33 PO in foreign affairs. Oae of the greatest triumphs of the Liberal party during the past three years was the fact th •t at the present moment everybody was saying that after having done almost everstaine wrong Lord Saha- bury had at last done one thing well in foreign affairs. That he had done well in tne Faseoda question was 1 because he had followed simply and purely on the lines laid down by Lard Rosebery and Sir Edward Grey. (Sear, hear.) The Fashoda difficulty would not have eisisted at all if Lord Salisbury had net shown himself so prone during the last three years to give way to foreign nations, and more especially to France, whenever an occasion for conflict arose. She Was not a war loving person. She did not say that Lord Sslisbury should have gone to war over Siam, Tunis,or Madagascar, but thy had seen over the Fesh oda question that it was not fleece ary to go to war because they had a certain fixed principle, and because they acted upon it. (Applause ) She did not believe that the Fashoda ques- tiers would even have reached the acute stage it did reach if Lord Salisbury hid :sot made so many previous " graceful concessions," as they were called. In illustration of her remarks that foreign affeirs affected women equally with men, she said Lord Salisbury had allowed to be taken froin u: wiry considerable treaty rights in the island of Madagascar. We had done a good flourishing trade with the people of that island, but the French go 7s3nment had imposed such pr otective upon the imports of Enelise manufacture tine it was new practically impossible for this nation to do any trade with that island on profitable lines. That meant that in sonne trade or some department of trade there must he at any rate a temporary slackening of trade, and a temeorary if not a p irmaneet depression in that trade, and that would mean short time and short wages for the nasn women and children. See thought thet showed quite clearly how the great question of imperial politics was as deeply important te women as men. They were told that won-'n did not suffer the same because tleey did not fight. But she kaew of two prominent Liberal women who within the last few months had lost their sons in the b Beans Soudan cam- paign, and she began th feel that the question of peace and war had eomAbing to do with Liberal women. Women could no more escape from politics then they could escape from the air they breathed. (Applause ) She would base her reasons for being a Liberal—if she could possibly ietagine herself being anything else— (laughter)—because the Liberal party hail been the. party in the past which had ten led to make life easier for the masses of the country, which had acted more h arnanely, and had been and still was the party which appeared te be mere zealous fur the moral mini for the social im- provement of the nation. (Applause.) The Countess proceeded to refer to tee beneficent measures which had ben passed by the Liberal Party. Speaking of religious equality she remarked that if for no other reason they as Women, who were perhaps, stronger in their religious convictions, should remember that the Liberal party hid been the champion of religious free- dom and equality, and they ought, therefore, to be Liberals if they had any gratitude in them. To-Jay the Liberal party still stood for freedom and equality in the cause of religion, while with regard te e meatier], though the Tory party told them that it had given them free education it was the Liberal party who had been the pioneers and the reforming power in that direction. (applause.) In all probability they would never have had the educational system which now existed in this country, and which, faulty as it was, hid so enormously improved the cendition of the people, but for tee Liberals. They had also the boon of cheap newspapers, cheap books, cheap postage and telegrams, and all these things they were apt to forget were the work of the Liberal pary. She was a great lover of book; and of newspapers, although there WAS a great deal of rubbish published. Still the influence of the Press and of the books Was good, and it should be remembered that the giving of cheap heroics and of the cheap newspapers, to the people was owing directly and noels tively tol their ' lealer, Mr. Gladstone. (Applause.) She was sure that if the Liberal party had been in power they would have male better use of the surplus in the budget, and given them a free breakfast table. (Applause.) Ref Irence was also made to the department- al work of Mr. Aclaud and Mr. Asquith in the last Liberal admiestra.tion. Special meati on was made to the appointment of women as factory inspectors, and to the Parish and Distrist Councils' Act, to the value of allotments, and the Workmen's Compen- : setion Act. Dilating on the subject of temperance the Countess said women were more deeply interested,more closely interested, and more enthusiastic than were the men. She could not conceive how any earnest woman who knew how invariably in every election, by-election, or genes* al electien they had the force of the drink traffic on one side and the Tory influence also, while on the other side they had a party who were endeavouring tie improve the social life of the nation in the direction of temperance—She could not conceive how a woman in these things could fail to see that it was her duty, if eke were an advocate of social reform, to work honestly and earnestly for the Liberal party in these times. (Ap- plause.) With resp'ct to the Czar's peace proposals she observed that even though the Czar of Russia's motives might not be all they were said to be—personally she was inclined to believe they were genuine—he had made a definite proposal, and they who professed to be a Christian and a peace-loving nation, and whose interests were lareely bound up with peace for the reason that they were the greatest commercial nation of the world,ought to leave no stone unturned to assist the Czar in considering whether a partial if not a complete disarmament might not be possible, even at the present time. (Applause.) She was pleased the Warrington Women's Liberal Association was increasing in member- ship, a 3 it was a great power for good, and she hoped they would nails, that fact, persuade the women of the town to become Liberals, and then they could only give effect to their Liberalism and their enthusiasm by be- coming member of the Women's Liberal Asseciation. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Miss Dorrora said she had very great pleasure in according a vote of thanks to the Countess frr her fluent, intereiting and graceful address. In the course of her remarks /11,es Dutton said the enthusiasna which had been experienced that evening' re-awoke her feelings I of pride and gratitude that she was a Liberal, and that I she had beea lei to take some interest in polities. They felt that women ought to take their part in politics. I Countess Kiarney was doing a very great work when she stirred up the women of the country to be interested in the affairs of that country and in the affairs of the localities in which they live I. If a woman should be a 1 lady guardian—and they had agreed by their enthusiasm that she was in the light place when she took that posi- tion—really her duty to her c-)untry and to her town and the clanmunity called her als r to take some part in Ipolitics. (Applause.) She had an influence in the home if she couli get no further. If all women would work in the spirit Countess Kearney had shown in her admir- able address they would take up the work with enthu- siasm and promote it Vigorously. (Applause.) Mrs. ARNIITAGE sae incl.d the motion, and said that no words were neceimary for her to commend it to the approval of that rnietiog It hid been a great treat to have the Conntess with them again, but the Chairman made a mistake when he said that she addressed them a year age. It was two years since the Countess Kearney Was here before. She was sun that alt 'r the excellent ad tress which had been given no lady would care to leave the room without becoming a membrr of the association: (Laughter and applause.) In conclusion, she apologised for the absence of Mr. Armitage. The Cirmatraeg, in putting the resolution to the meet- ing, observed that the spueh from Countess Kearney had suggested to him t wo refhctions ; one was by Burke who had said that there was no solace for private trouble Ike noble public endeavour ; and the other by Mr. Gladstone who said that if they wanted reereaVon and relaxation they would best find it in varied forms of activity. Whether their lot might be a little cloudy or very bright, the speech of the Countess [Mewed how mach women might lift their thoughts and widen their outlook on the ever-increasing intirees of life if they woucd t lte that thoronsth and intelligent interest in politics which had such an incalculable interest upon the ever-widening circle around them. (Applause). The motion was heartily agreed to. Countess KEARNEI replied, and proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman, which was carried with enthu- I sia,m.</p>
        <p>Mr.-CROSFIELD having replial, a collection was taken on behllf of the Aimociation funel.;, and the meeting terminated.</p>
        <p>A ST. HELENS MA-E'S FATAL FALL. Mr. F. A. Jon.s, deputy coroner, held an in- quest at St. Helens on Mondavon the b?dy of Lawrence Begley, aged fitty-,ight, of 37, Fox-street, who died early on Mondly morning as a result of falling down- stairs and breaking his neck about midnight on Satur- day.—Rnsa BPgley, the widow, said her husband was a man who rambled ab nit a good deLi during the night after having had drink TrliS WAS thl third time la'r husband bad falen downstairs undir somewhat similar circutnstances.—Thl jury returned a vlrdict of " Acci- dental death."</p>
