      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARRINGTON WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. VISIT 0? COUNTESS ALICE KEARNEY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. VISIT 0? COUNTESS ALICE KEARNEY.</p>
        <p>A public meeting under the auspices of the Warring- ton Wornenie Liberal Association was held in the Co- operative Hall, Sankey-street, oa Mouthy evening. The chief speaker was Ceuntese Alice Kearney. Mr. A. G. Cresfield presided over a fairly-largeeeudience. He was suppertee on the platform be Mrs. Armitage, Mrs. Henry Roberts and Mrs. Gritnshaw (hon secretary). The CYCLIRMAN said he was sure thar, all who were present on the last occesioa would aeree with him when he said that they were very lucky to have such an early opportunity of again listening to Couutess Kearney. (Applause.) If there were any who had the temerity or the hardihood to break a lance with Countess Kearney on any subject he did not think they would the less appre- ciate the acumen and force of her argumentative skill. He nought they were else fortunate in a topic which was so suggeetive and fruitful as that for discussion that evening. It suggested to him an analogous question, namely that many women were supporters of opponents because they laboured under misapprehensions. (Hear, hear.) It had often struck him that some of their oppon- ents amongst the gentler sex had an impression in their minds that Liberals were Socialists in disguise and inas- much as Socialism pushed to its extreme, violated the sanctity of home life and the privacy of the household. naturally that aroused an antagonism amongst women. He thought they could howeetly saw if they looked balk on the history of this century, and if they considered the efforts that were !seine made by the Liberal party at tee present time, that Liberalism had Mead been the very force in English politics which ha] kept Secialistn in its extreme form in the background. (Hear, hear.) They I had striven to give the individual in his or her surround- ings and in the opportunities of life a Fair charace.And the considerable measly e of success that had attended the work of the home +t labours of Liberalsin this country, was he thought, accountable for the fact that in no country in the world were the wilder delusions of the Seel diets at a heavier discount. (Hear, heir.) He teouelat there was one other misapprehension under which women laboured respeceing Liberalism and that was the im- pression that the aristocracy were the great representa- tives of good taste and good tone. That created a cer- tain symoathy amongst women, and to his mind was an equally great and unfortunate misapprehension. Th sre was no doubt whatever that the work he had just alluded to had done more than anything else perhaps to extend this good tone and taste and the opportunities of what he might call a rational and civilised life amongst the people at large. Tne tendency had been not to level down but to level up and no esi a man then Professor Huxley had said that if YO3 want to get the element oe what makes thee gen- tleman or gentlewoman, goo I feeling, and consideration and reverence, he would add, they were perhaps as I likely to find them amongst the ranks of our better ! artizen elasses—(applause)—as they were in any other section of society. He had no doubt that the question to be put before them that night would be proved by Countess Heather up to the very hilt. Perhaps argu- ments might be drawn more largely from imperial poli- tics and from a wider survey than from their own local field. But to teem who had had an experience of the public work in Warriragtoe he thought a very cogent answer and very forcible argument might be drawn from their own local experience. He would not feebly forestall what they would hear hem Countess Kearney but h 3 ceeld say that looking at the influences in W ars rington—he would say nothing against his personal friends, many of them amongst the Conservative Party—but in their politic 1 influences and effect as regarde I the enlightenment and progress of the town they were not in the same street and net in the hunt with the sm +II body of earn- est, eatable and conscientioue men and women wise in Werrin,gton were striving to do good to their fellow townsmen and their town. (Applause.) He thought oa local grounds they had every right te appeal to the ladies who exercised such an illi antable isfiusacs on the whole field of their work to help these few people who had the unselfishness ant the wisdom and true patriot- ism to put their heart into the work hee alluded to. (Ap- plause,) Countess KEARNEY said she had been both interested and stratified by the last iremarks which had fallen from the Chairmen's lips. What he had said with r-gard to the work done in local government entirely bore out what had been hr view of the two political parties. I She knew it was often the custom amongst the very best people to say that in matters of local government they ought not to consider whether they were Tory or Liberal, hut only to the fact that hear she was a good man or a good woman. She quite agreed with the principle, but her strong feeling was that in nine cases out of ten the bast men and women would be the Liberal men and the Liberal women. (Applause.) There was barfly a single question dr Local Government on which the Liberal attitule was not an entirely different one t) the Tory attitude. On School Boards and on Boards of Guardians especially the Liberal at itude of mind was in its very essence a different one to the Tory attitude. (Applause.) She wished to speak about two things (1), " why women should take air inter :at ia politics ; and (2) why they eh ou'd be Liberals." Her answer to the first question sheuld almost prevent her having to speak on I the sesortd one, because she thought that womea had exactly the same reason that men had, as every man in this country had—if he knew his own jut -rests aad the highest intereets of the nation—for taking an interest in political questions. Upon all questions with regard to material benefits and 1 advantages women were as much affected as men, and on the other hand, if they took the higher moral reason, I teere were surely not many earnest women in their I midst who would be inclined to say that they were not just as anxious for the moral and the social elevation of the nation as men were. With regard to the material side, women were as mach affected as the men, and in ssme ceses they were possibly more closely affected. (Applause). Speaking broadly, it was the man who had to go Out to earn a living for the wife anu the family, and the woman took her part in the care for the comfort of the home and the well-being of the children, and it roust be easier for the man to go out and to fight hard Ira the battle of life than for the worn who had te endeavour to make that difficult eff rt to make two ends meet where often enough the ends were not quite long enough to be brought together. (Laughter and Applause.) Women were very frequently accused, and prehape justly aCCII9B.I, of being a great deal more narrow-minded than the men, and worrying about trivial matters—about matters men would forget whea the circumstances which called them forth had pissed. The reason was that women in the past had been so circumscribed in th ir interest that they had had nothing else to lift them above the little troubles of their daily lives. She was sure that ths'char- eaters of women bad boon largely warped by nisi nar- rowing down of their interest. For the happiness of the woman herself there were very few things which were more important and useful than an intelligent interest in the great political questions of the day. Thera was nothing which made women more able to look philoso- phically upon things in general than the fact that she had an interest in the great questions outside her home life. She was very often met with the objection that the danger in all this interest in political matters was thot a weman would be led to neglect her home inter- ests But that was, she contended, a very absurd pro- position. They never thought that because a man once in a month or in six weeks went to a public meeting, and eead his daily or his weekly newspaper, or because he gave some of his hard-earned leisure time to work for a political party, that he would neglect his business or his work; therefore she did not se why a similar amount of interest, a similar amount of int dligent interest on the part of women should make them less useful in their homes and less devoted to tee interests theroin. She (Counte ,s Kearney) thought a woman would go back with more zest and interest to her work if she had an in- terest in public questions and these little relaxations ! which would relieve her mind of the petty troubles of ' her life. Could they imagine anything more depressing to a man who took a deep interest in politics than to go home to a wife who was absolutely incapable of under- standing one word of those questions in which he felt such an interest? That interest was all they had to rely upon if they wanted to see the country justly governed. They wanted to see that interest far more extended among the electorate and working-classes as a whole. If they had more interest in politic i at the present time they would not find so many men who had literally to be dragged to the poll, and they would not have so many men led astray arid gulled by the promises and hopes such as were held out by the Tories at the last general election. She believed that if women attended °en- eional meetings and belonged to an association such as Oat under whose auspices they were met towether, end heard what was goiag on in the great world with- out, taking an interest in great questions and becom- ing intelligent politicians, they would find that the men would come more and more to appreciate the society of their fireside and less and less that outside intercourse which they had at the present time. (Applause.) There I was no question whatever that women were a great , force in politics to-day if they would only take team up and betong to a political association. They had not yet got the weapon of the Parliamentary vote which men possessed, but they had a great deal of power and inflisence if they would only take up political questions in a serious manner. It was beComing more and more recognised by the two great political parties. The Tories recognised it when they I.rmed their Primrose League and Liberals were coming to recognise it largely owing to the honest and earnest wok done by the Women's Liberal Association. (Applause.) During twelve months she had taken part in eleven contested by-eleetione,and in every one of them she found en increasing number of women at work on the Liberal el la, and everyone had resulted magnifi- cently, on the whole, for the party to which she be- longed. And not only did the women work hard and honestly, but their services were appreciated and recog- nised by the candidates and their friends and supporters. (Applause.) They as women had no light to fraud aside from using kheir</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WARRINGTON SOLICITOR 8c THE R.S.P.C.A. INSPECTOR.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON SOLICITOR 8c THE R.S.P.C.A. INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>SCENE IN COURT.</p>
        <p>I During the hearing of a case at the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday, Inspector Great- rex, of the Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals aroused the ire of Mr. Jenkins who was defending a case,and the solicitor retorted " You are a very impudent fellow." It was in the case of John Morrisey, of 59 Nicholson-street, who was summoned for working a horse while in an un- fit state on November 26th, and Arthur Morris, of 89 Nicholsoa-street,who was summoned for allowing the animal to be workei.—Mr. Jenkins appeared for the defendants, and pleaded not guilty. P.C. Whittaksr said he was in Horsemarket-street 1, on Saturday about noon, when he received informa- tion that he was wanted near to Grace's pottery. He proceeded there and saw the defendant Morrisey in charge of a black horse attached ' to a cart laden with coke. The animal was afterwards taken into Mr. Grace's yard. It was suff ;ring from a wound on the off-shoulder about the size of a five-shilling piece, and another on the back about the size of a shilling. The horse was aged and in poor condi- tion. He received defendant into custody and took .him to the Police Station. He was afterwards uttilld. out. Witness charged him with ill-treating the horse, and he replied " I admit it is in an unfit state."—Cross-examined: The wound on the shoulder was not dry. Inspector Greatrea said he was on duty in Winwick- street no Saturday morning, and saw the younger de- fendant in 3harg3 of a black horse and a cart laden with coke. Taa h nee seem el to be going on one side. Wit- ness eramin Id the bores, and found it ta be aged and in peor couditi no. Oa rem )ving the collar be found an old wound on tno off side shoulder, the sin aa Sc piece. Tho wunl wic v,r 7 Tnu3q,swollei and inflamed. The collar w is pre“ing on it, ind E. appeared to be very pain- iOn the back there was a wound the size of a shilling, •al sad b the frie:Mn of the crupper. Witness advised defendant not to work the horse. Ile replied that he oust take that load. ELs einplover, he said. was Mrs. 'doreis. Mr. dorris cline up subsequently and asked am what rie had at ,sped the horse for. In answer to lies:ions ha said the horse was not his, bat belonged to ais wife. Witeeis asked him to get another horse, and he re.:lied that the horse was 6.G to work the load. Wit- ness said his; would measure the wound, and was taking -a!,3 out Let nil )ek Is when defendant Morris said he did not know his bun's, all asked him what right he hal to r1314.311"51 avv mud. Witnai. then gave him into custody and w, t ikea to tha Police Station. At the Police Station to, he woald take the full responsibil- ity, as he we te cmn fir his wife and he had sent Morrisey oi co wo:k the horse. lie afterwards said ta witness "I ..;id spend a cocked hat on you to-rnorro and have veterinary surgeon." Witness told him he would Emma one far Min if he liked. Alderman Harrison : Is a veterinary surgeon a cocked hat ? (Laughter). Toe Deputy tiingistral.ee' Clerk (Mr. G. F. F. White) : Perhape he meant Mr. Jenkins. (Laughter.) The Chief Canetple remarked that it was a slang term bra guinea.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins : We live and leara. Inspector Greitrex produced the collar, and referring to the hole winch hal been made in it to cover the wound said it was saturate I with matter. Mr. Jenkins exp.essed surprise, and lospector Greitrex, hinding the collar to him, said he could see tor himself it h 3 would put hi S spectacles On _</p>
        <p>bstr. Jenkins : That is just whit I am going to com- plain of. You are a very impudent fellow. I don't need gla ;gas t.) see whither Vie collar is in a proper state or net, and eh it is not the right way to treat an advocate in this curt. I treat every person with courtesy, and I will be treated so by you. The Chairmen : Had you not bstter make a complaint to the society ? Mr. Jenkins : I can mike it better here. If this 'man is capable of conducting himself in that way before a bench of magistrates after I nave treated him as a gentleman, what will he do when he is outside? Inspector Greatrex : I meant nothing wrong. You were in the shade and I thought you would used your spsctacles.to see it.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Jenkins That is very adroit. I have had ex- perience of you at Newton and other places, and seen your conduct to those whom you should have treated in a proper manner. Is that the kind of way in which you treated this youth ? Did he not tell yeti that he had done his best to prevent the horse suffering pain ? Inventor Greatrex : He did not. Did he not tell you that he tested the wound by pres- sure before sending the horse out ?—lie did not.—ad you retort, •‘ You are a liar. It was four of five days ago, as I an told, when the wound showed itself ?"—I never used such language, and never called a man a liar in my life.—What reason had you for dragging this respectable man to the Police Station P—He wanted to work the animal after I had stopped it, Mr. Jenkins : I believe him before I believe you. The Chairman : That only prejudices the case. Mr. Jenkins : You say you took a rule out. Did you not take a lance out ?</p>
        <p>InapeCt-or—Gr—rea-Crei No. I did net even take the rule out. I never had a lanc3. IV conduct wai per- fectly legitimate. I tr3ated defendant with every respect._ _ _</p>
        <p>Superintendent Monaghan was called, but Mr. Jenkins thought there was no necessity for his evidence. They admitted there was a heat lump CPEI the shoulder.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : Ils is a question of a wound. The Deputy-Magistrates' Clerk said the question was, whether the horse was fit to work or not.</p>
        <p>Superintsndent Monaghan said he i3;w the gorse at the Police Station on Satorday afternoon. There was a raw wound a little larger than half-a-crown on the off shoulder. Ths animal was in a Very poor condition. He saw both defendants. Morris said he nt inaged the business for his wife. He said he did not think the wound was as bad as it was. Inspector Greatrek con- ducted him elf properly towards defendants at the Police Station.</p>
        <p>_ Th D3puty Magistrat3's Clerk : Defendant was not locked up.</p>
        <p>_ John Dixon, veterinary surgeon, said he was called to examine ths horse on Saturday afternoon. Is was suffering from a sore shoulder. The wound had been a heat lump, and had burst. The wound was larger than a five-shilling piece, but the raw surface was about the size of half a crown. When he pressed in the immediate neighbourhood of the wound the horse flinched. It was in poor condHon. The collar had pressed on the wound, the hole mwles in it not losing large enough. Mr. Jenkins said the defendant had erred in work• ing the borse when it had a heat lump. He would have been wiser had he put it in the stab'e. The Inspector had, however made more of the case that it deserved. Defendant did his bat. He had a hole made in the collar, but it did not prove to be large enough. On Feiday however, the lump was notobroken, and seemed quit s sound. He tried the horse by pressure, but did not flinch.</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman : That cannot bo, acc)rding to the evidence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins : lam not telling an untruth. If they do I cannot help it. The evidence is written in my brief. The d 4end int said the hone did not flinuh. On Saturday morning he sent it out to take a small load of coke from the gas works to the Infirmary, and the hor3e wai stopped opposite the gas works, fie was bound to confess that for other reasons the hone should not have been worked. But the horse did not belong to the de- fendant, and they could not convict him. _</p>
        <p>The Dioaty Mlgistrates' Clerk : He i 9 charged with causing the horse to be worked. It is not said he is tho owner,</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins said Mrs. Morris had made an unfortut nate purchase. Silo thought the horse, if properly fed- Mii,ht turn out a sound animal. It had not, and he hopsd the magistrates would lo3k on the matter as de- fendant's misfortune,and not his fault.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shaweross, veterinary surgeon, said the animal was net fit for work. Ds unltoess arose from its poor condition, and not from trio wound. The wound had been a heat lump. He pressed the wound when he ex- amined the h.orse,and the animal did not flinch. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins: It would b 3 fit for light work on a farm.</p>
        <p>The Chairmln : T'iss best plaa i 3 to 9enl it to the knacker's yard. Morrisev was fined 23. 61. and Morris 53. and costs (21 119. ). The Chairman said the Bench could not help bit feel that he htd doae a cru3l t'aing to the animal to send it out to work in such a condition.</p>
        <p>AST HELENS TRADESMAN CHARGED WITH LUNACY.</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens Police Court on Tuesday, Ilichard linswarth, j Auer, of 26, Westfield-street, was charged as a lunatic. Constable Wilkes said that at a quarter to three that morning he received .Unsworth into cus- tody from the Prescat police. He had been rambling about the country. Witness thought it necessary to lock him up.—Dr. Challenor said that Unsworth told him that on Monday he set off to see a gentleman in Kiln-lane, at the top of Denton's Green, but when he got to the Lingholme Hotel his mind became a perfect blank,and he did not remember anything until he found , himself in the hands of the patio° at Prescot. About three or four months ago he had a lot of financial trouble, and he had ben drinking, and at that time ha attempted tocommit suicide. He inflicted wounds upon himself, but he recovered, and they sent him away to some friends, and told him to give up the drink alto- gether. He did not think that Unswortla had been drinking lately,but his wife said that he had been suffer- ing from loss of sleep.Fle required medical treatment and to be kept underobservation,and he (witness) Suggested that he should be sent to the workhouse infirmary for fourteen days.—Trie magistrates made the order for fourteen days' detention in the infirmary. VEHICLE LANIP3.—To comply with the new Borough Bye-Laws. Lowest prices.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUG 3Y FOO rB A party of abut tweutv,fitteen of whom were Wavers, travelled to Mor scainbe on Saturday with the intention of participating in, or witnessing toe gams between the sea-side club and Warrington. Even the fact that the match meant a trip " to' tre wattsr- side " failed to draw aoy supporters of the home club, and enthusiasm must surely be at a low ebb when the team is allowed to go away without any followers at all to cheer thsm when the tido of battle goes against them. The London and North-Western Company did not do anything to encourage people to make the jo rney to Morecambe,but acted in a manner more likely to have the opposite effect. Instead of the usual low rate charged for football excursionists, the fare on Saturday was ss. 51,—an outrageous sum. If it had not been for toe fact that I had had a run for the train equal to two Sheffield handicaps rolled into one, I should have declined to mike the journey at such an expense, but having mide such a mighty eff3rt to reach the station in time I decided to go. As the match itself av,,,s not very edifying, or even interesting, those who preferred to stay at horns, and did so, had much the best of the bargain. . .</p>
        <p>Warri-rigtod got their tvo points all right though no wichout a severe struggle. BAII3 500 More3ainhriaos witnessed the game, though 'there thy all came from was a mystery, and is yet. The town had the forlorn, forsaken app iarance willed popular watering p1a035 have in the winter time,and diving the "constitutional" which the lot of us took upon the prornena le, preceding the match, we did not see more than a score of people. It is a wonder that the shop-keepers did not fancy " the season" was going to re-commence wheo the gallant band from Warrington W. 13 seen monopolising the " prom."</p>
        <p>After the slight difficulty caused by the appointed ref- eree being among the missing had been surmounted the teams entered the field in charge of one of the touch judges, and to the encouraging shouts of the aforemen- tioned 500. Seeing that Warrington received an even bigger dubbing than the wooden-spoonists the previous week, there was some confidence at Moss-lane that the visitors' won't' leave two points behind them. This hope was incr.sas3d when the news went round that the Warrington captain, who has always given an excellent account of himself at Morecambe, was on the sick list. But, though Warrington were thus handicapped they proved a little too strong for the sea lidera, and More- camlogs two points are still in futurity.</p>
        <p>It was a hard game all through, though up to half- time no bad feeling was sh.own on either side with one exceptional in?tance. Warrington took up the attack almost immediately, alai aft 3r scrapping about in the vicinity of tee dorecambe goal-line, tile, never crossing it, Hallam gave them the lead with a well-judged drop- kick. Presently, L. Jackam, o[lo of the Morecambe halves, took an equally well judged drop-kick at Ballad?. with excellent-‘effuet, for it dropped th ..droppist. This see, of dropping being contrary to rule, h weii-ar, and moreover a cowardly piece of play, Mr. Jackon was i given his pas3port to the frontier and retired. Hallam was hurt, but the medicine-man effected a rapid cure with his patent rouse-the-dead embrocation. The game was resumed, Ashurst ran up a4ainst the bill whici was corning his way, and sctred, and Hallam, the :le wly- risen, popp9d the ball over the bar from the touch lino. It was a flue goal for a lame man,and some of the hot/se- aters said as much,to the ace ompaniment of sundry dark hints, but then thy have never seen or used that per. ticu;ar embrocation.</p>
        <p>The second half opened witle Morecambe pesse;sing only 14 men ant at tee same time be'ng iu a min rite' of 7 points. Play had not pr ocee lei many mientes before there was another stoppage on aceount of casualties. Morecambe's sole remaining half-back, Armistead had sustained a kick on th j ave, and walkid off the field with his head in his hands. This was rather ough on the homesters, and they began to act as if they thought Warrington to blame for their depleted forces. Rough play b-came pretty general, and after nearly every mix-up there was some unhoc'ey combatant left prone on Mother Earth. Nothing serious hap- pened, though it looked very probable that Morecamb 's thirteea men would defeat Warrington's fifteen. They played like demons, and Armistead got so excited as to forget his aching jaw, and joined in the attack, the stay was but brief. I don't know. whether he had the imprint of Ashurit's bt upon his jaw, but he seemed to have something against the Warring ton half-back, and the first time he tackled him he procee- ded to inter his opponent. The referee saw the pre- liminary obsequies and ordered both combatants off the field. Ashurst appeared innocent, but he had to go. Gowanlock,a forward nearly as long as his narne,crossed the Warrington line after being tackled a few times, and had his try disallowed. It may have been a try and it may not, but Warrington got the benefit of the doubt, aud the referee got the loren,fit of the, spectators' coaver- satioiaal powers. H. Had wen saved his side trona retir- ing pointlessly, by dropping a fine goal. Darkness had nearly come o'er the scene when the finish arrived with Warrington winning by 7 points to 2.</p>
        <p>Good football was conspicious by its absence in this match. The first half was full of chances far Warring- ton which were missed through lack of combination and method. The backs wer3 all at spa ; principally through the poor passing of the men behind the R. Bate was fat too slow, and got tackled times without number. Cross and Isherwood did well on the right wing. the latter playingda particularly good game under difficulties A considerable improvement was manifested in the forwards. They easily beat the home pack when in full strength, NAylor, Hill, and Boardman being prominent with good work.</p>
        <p>----- Warrington have now obtained 11 points out of 11 matches,which is abmt their average—and a very poor one. There is still a debit of nearly 30 more points scored agains,, than for, and this will take some wiping off. To make the ba'ance of points a credit balm:vie new men will be needed in several departments.</p>
        <p>The results of niatclses, and the positions of the clubs in the Lancashire Senior C c,npetitton, up to date, are as follow :</p>
        <p>Nor. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Broughton Rang 11...10 .. 1... 0..24 38-162... 4 5-- 23...20 Widnes 11— 9... 0... 2 .34 18— 82... 7 4 25...20 Salford ll.. 9... 2... 0... 9 25 93...1) 6 38...18 *Swinton 11... 8... 1... 2...16 28-116,. 5 4 22...16 Leigh 13... 8.. 5... 0...21 20-102... 8 19— 73...16 Oldham 11... 7... 4... 0... 9 36-126... 8 10— 45...14 Warrington 11... 5... 5... 1... 8 13— 55... 9 21-- 81-.11 St. Helens .... 12... 5... 6... 1...17 16— 82... 7 17— 60...11 Runcorn Wigan 11... 3... 8... 0...13 10— 55-2) 15— 85... 6 Tydlesley 12. 2... 8... 2... 9 9 45,..18 28-123... 6 Rochdale Hornetslo... 2... 7... 1...10 4 32...10 24-112... 5 Stockport 9. 1... 8... 0... 7 6 29...22 23-119... 2 Morecambe l2 0.12... 0... 3 2 12...21 35-147... 0 *Swinton have had two points de itacted for briach of professional rules.</p>
        <p>Northern Union games in the Lancashire section pro- duced two surprises on Siturday. Swinton were gener- ally expected to adminster a very sound thrashing to Wigan, but the "Loos" had the greatest difficulty in averting defeat. They won the match, but not because they were the better team. The score was a goal to nothing, and Wigan must be accounted unlucky to lose. Oldham went down before Leigh at the ground of the latter club, the score being seven points to three. Den- bavin and Molyneux, the clever half-backs,seem to have won the game for Leigh by their drop-kicking. Tyldes- ley, td the surprise of most peonlo, drew witn Rochdale by the Roach, the rtcord being five points each. Run- corn, on their own ground, were beaten to the tune of 13 points to five by the Broughton Rangers, and Widnes and St. Helens, on the St. Helena ground,fought a stub- born contest, in which neitner could claim victory—no points being scored,</p>
        <p>MAt people expected an easy win for the "A" team against the Runcorn Recs. tut were hardly prepared for the to eriog which took place. With the exception of one occasion only the pressure was all the time from the homesters. Thera was rather toe much individual play, bat perhaps the utter collapse of the visitors forced it into that channel. The Warringtoa crowd were rather amused by an eapression of opinion from one of the fol- lowers of the Runcorn team, "that they were throwing the game away," and this in the last stages of the game. The visitors possessrd a capital sprinter, but his team were not in a position to give him much support. The Warrington backs to a man played a capital game, Harris doing most ISf the scoring. Let Us hops the "A" team will keep a stout he srt and pursue their victorious career ; on form they have nothing to fear.</p>
        <p>The positioma of the club and the remits of matches up to date in tk 1, Lancashire" A " team competition are as folow</p>
        <p>For. Against, P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Salford IL.. 9... 2... 0... 17 41...157... 3 11... 39...18 Warrington B.. 7... 1... 0... 8 18... 70... 5 4... 22...14 Stockport Widnes Brongton Rang 6... 4... 2... 0... 1 15... 57... 4 8... 32... 8 Oldham 8 4... 3... 0... 10 25.., 95... 5 6... 23... 8 Swinton 6. 3 3... 0... 13 18... 81.., 6 15... 57... 6 Runcorn 5. 1... 4... 0... 2 7... 25...10 24... 91... 2 Leigh Rochdale Hornets 7... 1... 6... 0... 3 3... 15...19 40...158... 2 Tyldesley</p>
        <p>From what I Lear the Warrington committee have recently approached Evans, of Fetcher Russell's team, who is a most promising'player. He is the son of Coun- cillor James Evans,and is a young man of fine physique and a speedy runner. Already ke has gained a reputa- tion in junior football as a smart wing threequarter. This is his first season on the Rugby football field, al- though he has previously footed the leather under the Association code, and has also, I believe, won a prize or two on the running path. Several Warrington commit- tee men were on Fletcher Russell's field on S iturday, ostensibly with the object of taking stock of him. I be- have there is likely to be some difficulty in getting Isis consent to play.</p>
